** τις**,
**τι**, Indef. _pronoun_ **any one, any thing**, enclitic through all cases (for exceptions see below):—but τί; τί; Interrog. _pronoun_ **who? what?**, _oxytone_ in the _monosyllable_ cases, _paroxytone_ in the others:—Dialectal forms: {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria}} σις (**si se**) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.10 H.; 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} σις (with ?? for σ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).262.25 (Mantinea, 5th c.BC)}}; Thess. κις {{Refs|[prev. work]9(2).515.12 (from Larissa), 1226.4, 1229.27 (from Phalanna), _plural_ κινες [prev. work]517.41 (from Larissa)}}, _neuter_ κι in διεκί, ποκκί (which see); _neuter_ _plural_ _Doric dialect_ σά, _Boeotian dialect_ τά, _Aeolic dialect_ _dative_ τίω, τίοισι (see. infr. B). (I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **q[uglide]i-**, cf. Latin _quis_, **quid**, etc.; for σά, τά, see at {ἄσσα}, σά μά; with τέο (see. infr. B) cf. OSlav. _genitive_ _ česo_)
**A** Indef. _pronoun_ τις, τι, _genitive_ _Ionic dialect_ τεο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.305, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.58}}; more frequently τευ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.388, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.30, +others, 5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 7, etc.}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ του {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 21, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 329, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, etc. (sometimes _feminine_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 290, “OT” 1107 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 370, etc.)}}; του is rare after {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC, never in LXX or “NT”}}, but found in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).798.17 (Tenos, 3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 250.6, 647.23 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.23.3}}, revived by the Atticists, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.29, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 20, etc.}}; τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.90, “IG” 12.16.17, 65.41, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 5, “Ch.” 102, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 698, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109, +others (“Rh.Mus.” 72.483), etc.}}; _dative_ _Ionic dialect_ τεῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.502, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48, 5.86}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ τῳ (also in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.299, 12.328, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.308, 20.297, +others, always in _masculine_) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1045, “IG” 12.39.54, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.45; as _feminine_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 472, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 80, etc.}}; τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in the form οὔ τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.68, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.96) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.26, +others, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.12, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114 (elsewhere _feminine_, 2.62, 3.69, 83, 4.113), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1041, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 443, 495, etc.}}; _accusative_ τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.62, 5.761, etc.}}, _neuter_ τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.122, etc.: dual τινε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 237d, “Prm.” 143c, 149e}}: _plural_ τινες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in οὔ τινες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.279, 17.587 and οἵτινες (see. ὅστις))}}; _Doric dialect_ τινεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 527.127 (Drerus, 3rd c.BC)}}; _nominative_ and _accusative_ _neuter_ τινα (ὅτινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.450)}}, never in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, or 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, _falsa lectio_ in 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.74, first in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 163d, “Ep.” 325a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.63, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 19, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 110, 4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.22, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094a5, “IG” 42(1).121.35 (Epid., 4th c.BC), etc.}}; ἄσσα (which see) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.218}}, never in Trag. or {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}; _Attic dialect_ ἄττα first in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.113, 2.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 173, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400a, etc., never in LXX, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus}}, revived by the Atticists, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3, etc.}}; _genitive_ _Ionic dialect_ τεων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.175, 5.57, τεῶν conjecture for γε ῶν in 4.76}}; τινων not in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, first in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 977 (Lyric poetry)}}; _dative_ τισι, τισιν, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.113, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.18}}; N.-W. _Doric dialect_ τινοις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“GDI” 1409.5 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; _Ionic dialect_ τεοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.113, 9.27}} (for τεοις and τεον see at {τεός}); _accusative_ τινας [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.735, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.371 (also in οὕστινας, ὅτινας, see at {ὅστις}), etc.}}]; _neuter_ τινα (see. above):—**any one, any thing, some one, some thing**; and as _adjective_ **any, some**, and serving as the Indef. _Article_ **a, an**; θεός νύ τίς ἐστι κοτήεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.191; καί τις θεὸς ἡγεμόνευεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.142; οὐδέ τις αὐτὸν ἠείδη δμώων [prev. work] 205; ἤ τι ὀϊσάμενος, ἢ.. [prev. work] 339; μή τίς μοι ὑποδείσας ἀναδύη [prev. work] 377, compare 405 - 410; εἴ τινά που μετ᾽ ὄεσσι λάβοι [prev. work] 418, compare 421, +others; τις θεός construed as if τις θεῶν, 19.40, compare 11.502, “IG” 12.94.19, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1039.}}
**A.II** special usages:
**A.II.1** **some one** (of many), i.e. **many a one**, ὧδε δέ τις εἴπεσκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.201, etc.}}: sometimes with meiosis, implying **all** or **men**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.638, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.224}}; so in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49 near the end, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, etc.}}
**A.II.2** **any one concerned, every one**, εὖ μέν τις δόρυ θηξάσθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.382}}; ἀλλά τις αὐτὸς ἴτω let **every man** come himself,{{Refs|17.254; ἵνα τις στυγέῃσι καὶ ἄλλος 8.515, compare 16.209, 17.227, +others}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, even with the _imperative_, τοῦτό τις.. ἴστω S {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Aj.” 417 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 346, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1187; ἀγορεύω τινὶ ἐμὲ μὴ βασανίζειν [prev. author] “Ra.” 628}}; τοὺς ξυμμάχους αὐτόν τινα κολάζειν that **every man** should himself chastise his own allies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, compare 6.77; ὅ τί τις ἐδύνατο [prev. author] 7.75}}; ἄμεινόν τινος better than **any others**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.66, compare 19.35}}:—this is more fully expressed by adding other pronominal words, τις ἕκαστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.65, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, etc.; πᾶς τις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1205, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.94, etc.; ἅπας τις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.113, etc.; οὐδέν τι μᾶλλον [prev. author] 4.118}}. In these senses, τις is frequently combined with _plural_ words, οἱ κακοὶ.. οὐκ ἴσασι, πρίν τις ἐκβάλῃ, for πρὶν ἐκβάλωσι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 965}}; οἷς ἂν ἐπίω, ἧσσόν τις πρόσεισι, for ἧσσον προσίασι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.85; ἐτόλμα τις.., ὁρῶντες [prev. author] 2.53, compare 7.75}}; especially after εἴ or ἤν τις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.62, +others}}
**A.II.3** in reference to a definite person, whom one wishes to avoid naming, οὐκ ἔφασαν ἰέναι, ἐὰν μή τις χρήματα διδῷ (i.e. Cyrus) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 1.4.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 552, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.122}}; so also euphemistic for something bad, ἤν τι ποιῶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.74; ἂν οὗτός τι πάθῃ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.11}}: hence for the _1st pers._ or _2nd pers._ _pronoun_, ἅ τιν᾽ οὐ πείσεσθαι ὀΐω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.289, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 751}}; ποῖ τις τρέψετα; for ποῖ τρέψομα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 603, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 245 (Lyric poetry), 1138, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.40, 5.7.31, etc.}}
**A.II.4** indefinitely, where we say **they**, French **on**, sometimes with an ironical force, φοβεῖταί τις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 59 (Lyric poetry); μισεῖ τις ἐκεῖνον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.8}}; as _vocative_, τὸν Πλοῦτον ἔξω τις κάλει call P. out, **somebody**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1196.}}
**A.II.5** τις, τι may be opposed, expressly or by implication, to οὐδείς, οὐδέν, and mean **somebody, something**, by meiosis for **some great one, some great thing**, ηὔχεις τις εἶναι you boasted that you were **somebody**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 939; εἰσὶν ὅμως τινὲς οἱ εὐδοκιμοῦντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1293b13; τὸ δοκεῖν τιν᾽ εἶναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 156; τὸ δοκεῖν τινὲς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.213; ὡς σὲ μὲν ἐν τῇ πόλει δεῖ τινὰ φαίνεσθαι, τὴν πόλιν δ᾽ ἐν τοῖς Ἕλλησι μηδενὸς ἀξίαν εἶναι [prev. author] 10.71}}; κἠγών τις φαίνομαι ἦμεν after all I too am **somebody**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.79, cf. NT.Act.5.36}}; also in _neuter_, οἴονταί τι εἶναι ὄντες οὐδενὸς ἄξιοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 41e, compare “Phd.” 63c, “Phdr.” 243a, “Euthd.” 303c, etc.:— so τι λέγειν to be near the mark, opposed to οὐδὲν λέγειν, [prev. author] “Prt.” 339c, “R.” 329e, “Phdr.” 260a, etc.; ἵνα καὶ εἰδῶμεν εἴ τι ὅδε λέγει [prev. author] “Cra.” 407e; οἴεσθέ τι ποιεῖν, οὐδὲν ποιοῦντες [prev. author] “Smp.” 173c.}}
**A.II.5.b** τις is sometimes opposed to to another word, ἀελλοπόδων μέν τιν᾽ εὐφραίνοισιν ἵππων τιμαί.., τέρπεται δὲ καί τις.. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 221; τισὶ τῶν πολιτῶν ἀποροῦσι συνεξέδωκε θυγατέρας.., τοὺς δ᾽ ἐλύσατο ἐκ τῶν πολεμίων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.59; μέρος μέν τι σιδήρου, μέρος δέ τι ὀστράκινον LXX.Dan.2.33 (more frequently with the Article, see below 10 c)}}; ἔστιν οὖν οὐ πᾶν τὸ ταχύ, ἀλλά τι (sic codices BT) αὐτοῦ ἀγαστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 412c; ἀναγκαῖον ἤτοι πᾶσι τοῖς πολίταις ἀποδίδοσθαι πάσας ταύτας τὰς κρίσεις ἢ τισὶ πάσας.. ἢ τινὰς μὲν αὐτῶν πᾶσι τινὰς δὲ τισίν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298a9, compare 1277a23}}; τὸ μεῖζον τοῦθ᾽ ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἑτέρου λέγεται· τινὸς γὰρ λέγεται μεῖζον greater than **something**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.” 6a38; τὸ πρώτως ὂν καὶ οὐ τὶ ὂν ἀλλ᾽ ὂν ἁπλῶς [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1028a30}}; πότερον τῷ τυχόντι ἢ τισί; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1269a26.}}
**A.II.6** with (Proper name)s τις commonly signifies **one named** so-and-so, ἦν δέ τις ἐν Τρώεσσι Δάρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.4, etc.}}; with a sense of contempt, Θερσίτης τις ἦν there was **one** Thersites, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 442.}}
**A.II.6.b** **one of the same sort**, converting the (Proper name) into an appellative, ἤ τις Ἀπόλλων ἢ Πάν **an** Apollo or **a** Pan, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 55 (_anapaest meter_)}}; [πόλιες] ταὶ μέλονται πρός τινος ἢ Διὸς ἢ γλαυκᾶς Ἀθάνας Lyric poetry in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PVat.” 11v xi7; Σκύλλαν τινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1233, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 181, “Av.” 512, “Ra.” 912: so also ὥς τις ἥλιος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 288}}; ἰσθμόν τιν᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 647.}}
**A.II.7** with _adjectives_ τις combines to express the idea of a _substantive_ used as predicate, ὥς τις θαρσαλέος καὶ ἀναιδής ἐσσι προΐκτης **a** bold and impudent beggar, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.449, compare 18.382, 20.140, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.220}}; ἐγώ τις, ὡς ἔοικε, δυσμαθής **a** dull**ard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358a, compare “Prt.” 340e}}; φόβου πλέα τις εἶ **a** cow**ard**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 696, compare “Th.” 979 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 1140 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὡς ταχεῖά τις.. χάρις διαρρεῖ in what swift **fashion** (={ταχέως πως}), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1266, compare “OT” 618, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.198}}; δεινόν τι ποιεύμενος thinking it **a** terrible **thing**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.155, 5.33.}}
**A.II.8** with numerals and _adjectives_ expressing number, size, or the like, εἷς δέ τις ἀρχὸς ἀνὴρ.. ἔστω **some** one man, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.144; ἕνα τιν᾽ ἂν καθεῖσεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 911; δώσει δέ τι ἕν γε φέρεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.83; τινὰ μίαν νύκτα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61; προσκαλεσάμενός τινας δύο τῶν ἑκατονταρχῶν NT.Act.23.23}}; sometimes the τις softens the definiteness of the numeral, ἑπτά τινες **some** seven, seven **or so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.34; ἐς διακοσίους τινάς [prev. author] 3.111, compare 7.87, 8.21}}; so without an actual numeral, ἡμέρας τινάς **some** days, i.e. **several**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.52}}; στρατῷ τινι **of a certain amount, considerable**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.3}}; ἐνιαυτόν τινα a year **or so**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.68}}; so οὐ πολλοί τινες, τινὲς οὐ πολλοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 510, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.94, etc.}}; ὀλίγοι τινές or τινὲς ὀλίγοι {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.17, 3.7}}; οὔ τινα πολλὸν χρόνον no **very** long time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.48; τις στρατιὰ οὐ πολλή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61}}; so also ὅσσος τις χρυσός what **a** store of gold, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.45, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, 2.18, etc.; κόσοι τινές [prev. author] 7.234; πηλίκαι τινὲς τιμωρίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.3; πολλὸς γάρ τις ἔκειτο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.156; ἐκ πολλοῦ τευ χρόνου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.58.}}
**A.II.9** with Pronominal words, ἀλλά τί μοι τόδε θυμὸς.. μερμηρίζει **something**, namely this, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.38, compare 380}}; οἷός τις what sort of **a** man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.638 (uncertain reading), compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.348, 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313a, etc.; ποῖός τις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 42, “OC” 1163, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.24, etc.; ὁποῖός τις [prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.2.2, +others; εὐτυχίη τις τοιήδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.1, etc.; τοιοῦτός τις [prev. author] “An.” 5.8.7.}}
**A.II.10** with the Article,
**A.II.10.a** when a noun with the _Article_ is in _apposition_ with τις, as ὅταν δ᾽ ὁ κύριος παρῇ τις when the person in authority, **whoever he be**, is here, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 289}}; τοὺς αὐτοέντας.. τιμωρεῖν τινας (variant τινα) {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 107.}}
**A.II.10.b** in Philosophic writers, τις is added to the _Article_ to show that the _Article_ is used to denote a particular individual who is not specified in the general formula, although he would be in the particular case, ὁ τὶς ἄνθρωπος **the individual** man (**whoever he may be), this or that** man, opposed to ἄνθρωπος (man in general), ὁ τὶς ἵππος, ἡ τὶς γραμματική, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 1b4, 8}}; τὸ τὶ μέγεθος, opposed to ὅλως τὸ μέγεθος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1283a4, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.223; but in ἑνὸς γὰρ τό γε τὶ φήσεις σημεῖον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 237d}}, the _Article_ is used as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}} cc. (which see) ὁ, ἡ, τό [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.5: later ὅ τις (or ὁ τὶς) much like{ὁ δεῖνα}}}], δεῦρο ὅ τις θεός, ὄφθητί μοι in a general formula of invocation, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.236; αἴρω σε, ἥ τις βοτάνη [prev. work]287; εἰς τήν τινα κρείαν (to be read χρείαν) [prev. work]289.}}
**A.II.10.c** frequently in opposed clauses, ὁ μέν τις.., ὁ δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1141, “Hec.” 624, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99b, etc.; ὁ μέν τις.., ἄλλος δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1407; ὁ μὲν.., ὁ δέ τις.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.15: _plural_, οἱ μέν τινες.., οἱ δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.91; οἱ μέν τινες.., οἱ δὲ.., οἱ δέ τινες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.10, etc.; οἱ μὲν.., οἱ δέ τινες.. [prev. work] 6.1.26}}, etc.: also combined with other alternative words, ὁ μέν τις.., ὁ δέ τις.., ἕτερος δέ τις.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 2.6}}; ὁ μὲν.., ἕτερος δέ τις.., ὁ δὲ.., etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 162f}}: also in _neuter_, τὸ μέν τι.., τὸ δέ τι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 358a; τὸ μέν τι.., τὸ δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40}}; in _adverbial_ sense, τὸ μὲν.., τὸ δέ τι.. **partly.., partly.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.73.4}}; and τι remains unaltered even when the _Article_ is _plural_, τὰ μέν τι μαχόμενοι, τὰ δὲ καὶ ἀναπαυόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.14, compare “HG” 7.1.46}}; also τὸ δέ τι.. but **in some** measure.., without τὸ μέν preceding, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107, compare 118, 7.48.}}
**A.II.10.d** later τις is used as in b above but without the _Article_, γράψον.. ὅτι τι καί τι εἴληφας that you have received **such** and **such** things, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 937.22 (3rd c.AD); κληρονόμους καταλείπω τὴν θυγατέρα μού τινα καὶ τὸν σύντροφον αὐτῆς τινα καί τινα [prev. work] 1034.2 (2nd c.AD)}}; τίς τινι χαίρειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|A to B greeting (in a draft letter),[prev. work] (2nd c.AD).}}
**A.II** the _neuter_ τι is used,
**A.II.a** collectively, ἦν τι καὶ ἐν ταῖς Συρακούσαις there was **a party.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48}}; so perhaps τῶν ἄλλων οὔ πέρ τι πεφυγμένον ἐστ᾽ Ἀφροδίτην, οὔτε θεῶν, οὔτ᾽ ἀνθρώπων no **class,** {{Refs|hymnus ad Venerem 34}} (but _masculine_ τις in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 143).}}
**A.II.b** euphemistic for something bad, see above {{Refs|3.}}
**A.II.c** joined with Verbs, **somewhat, in any degree, at all**, ἦ ῥά τί μοι κεχολώσεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.421; παρεθάρρυνέ τι αὐτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.7, etc.: with _adjectives_ or Adverbs, οὕτω δή τι ἰσχυραί, οὕτω δή τι πολύγονον, etc., 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.12, 108, compare 4.52; so also ὀλίγον τι ἧσσον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.365; οὐδέ τι μᾶλλον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.123, etc.; ἧσσόν τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75, etc.}}; οὐ πάνυ τι, πολύ τι, σχεδόν τι, see at {πάνυ} {{Refs|1.3, πολύς 111.1a, 2a, σχεδόν IV}}; also in conjunction with οὐδέν, μηδέν, οὐδέν τι πάντως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.3}}; οὐδέν, μηδέν τι μᾶλλον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 522, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 280; μηδέν τι λίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1234:}} —also καί τι καὶ.. ὑποψίᾳ **in part** also from suspicion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107; καί πού τι καί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.28.}}
**A.II.12** τίς τε frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ὡς ὅτε τίς τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.33, 4.141, see τε 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**A.II.13** ἤ τις ἢ οὐδείς **few** or none, **next to none**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.140, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.45, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 47.5, 48.4}}; ἤ τι ἢ οὐδέν **little** or nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17b; ἢ οὐδεὶς ἤ τις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 41.62 (assuming variant).}}
**A.II.14** τις is _pleonastic_ in such phrases as οὐδέν τι or μηδέν τι, see above {{Refs|11c.}}
**A.II.14.b** repeated in successive clauses, ὅσα λέγει τις ἢ πράσσειτις ἢψέγειν ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 689; εἴ τις δύο ἢ καὶ πλέους τις ἡμέρας λογίζεται [prev. author] “Tr.” 944 (where however κἄτι πλείους is probably conjecture),compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1218}} (whereas τις is sometimes omitted in the first clause, οὔτε φωνὴν οὔτε του μορφὴν βροτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 21, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 3): but in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 734, ἔστι γάρ τις οὐ πρόσω.. πόλις τις}}, the repetition is pleonastic, as also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 57f (Lyric poetry, assuming variant).}}
**A.II.15** τις is sometimes omitted, οὐδέ κεν ἔνθα τεόν γε μένος καὶ χεῖρας ὄνοιτο (i.e. τις) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.287}}; ὡς δ᾽ ἐν ὀνείρῳ οὐ δύναται (i.e. τις) φεύγοντα διώκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.199, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1226 (Lyric poetry), “Leg.Gort.” 2.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456d}}: τις must often be supplied from what goes before,{{Refs|[prev. work] 478c, “Prt.” 319d.}}
**A.II.15.b** sometimes also τις is omitted before a _genitive_ case which must depend upon it, as ἢ [τις] τᾶς ἀσώτου Σισυφιδᾶν γενεᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 189 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἢν γαμῇ ποτ᾽ αὐτὸς ἢ [τις] τῶν ξυγγενῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1128; ἐν τῶν πόλεων “IG” 12.56.14.--Cf. ὅστις, οὔτις, μήτις, ἄλλο τι.}}
**A.III** Accentuation and position of τις:
**A.III.1** accentuation: τις is normally enclitic, but in certain uses is orthotone, i.e. theoretically oxytone (τίς, τινά, τινές, τινῶν, etc.,{{Refs 4th c.AD+|compare 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.373 H.) and barytone when followed by another word (τὶς}} or τις, τινὰ, τινὲς, τινῶν, etc.). According to {{Refs|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.240 H. its orthotone accent is τίς (not τὶς), τίνα, τίνες, etc. The orthotone form is used in codices:}}
**A.III.1.a** at the beginning of a sentence, τίς ἔνδον..; is **any one** within? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 654 (τὶς conjecture Hermann)}}; τί φημ; ={λέγω τι}; am I saying **anything?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 865, “OT” 1471}}; <τίς ἦλθ;> ἦλθέ τις has **anybody** come? {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Somebody” has come, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus [same place]}}; τὶς κάθηται, τὶς περιπατεῖ, **so and so** is sitting (walking), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.97}}; τὶς αἰπόλος καλούμενος Κομάτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.78; τίς ποτε οἰκοδεσπότης.. ἐκοπία 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor in “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum” iii ; or after a pause, πῶς γὰρ ἄν, ἔφην ἐγώ, ὦ βέλτιστε, τὶς ἀποκρίναιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 337e}}; τι οὖν (τὶς ἂν εἴποι) ταῦτα λέγει; [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.14 (variant); ἔντοσθεν δὲ γυνά, τι θεῶν δαίδαλμα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.32; οὐ γυμνὸν τὸ φίλαμα, τι δ᾽ ὦ ξένε καὶ πλέον ἑξεῖς 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 1.5 (variant for{τὺ}).}}]
**A.III.1.b** when τις is opposed to to another τις or to some other word, τισὶ μὲν συμφέρει, τισὶ δ᾽ οὐ συμφέρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284b40, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.2; τινὲς μὲν οὖν.., ἡμεῖς δὲ.. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.1; τὸ τὶ μὲν ψεῦδος ἔχον, τὶ δὲ ἀληθές 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.127; ἀλλὰ τινὰ μὲν.., τινὰ δὲ.. 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 14.6; ποτὲ μὲν πρὸς πάντα, ποτὲ δὲ πρὸς τινά 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.48: without such opposition, τοῦτ᾽ εἰς ἀνίαν τοὔπος ἔρχεται τινί}} for **a certain person**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1138}}. Codices are not consistent; in _signification_{{Refs|11.5a, 10c, 13}} they make it enclitic; in _signification_ {{Refs|11.5b sometimes enclitic, sometimes orthotone (see. above)}}; sometimes enclitic and orthotone in the same sentence, πάντα δὲ τὰ γιγνόμενα ὑπό τέ τινος γίγνεται καὶ ἔκ τινος καὶ τί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1032a14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 165c.}}
**A.III.2** position:
**A.III.2.a** τις is rarely first word in the sentence, and rarely follows a pause (see. above {{Refs|111.1a, b)}}; it may stand second word, ἔσκε τις ἐνθάδε μάντις ἀνήρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.508, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.515, 23.331}}; but in general its position is not far before or after the word to which it belongs in sense, ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε δή τινα μάντιν ἐρείομεν {{Refs|1.62; φυλακὴ δέ τις ἔμπεδος ἔστω 8.521.}}
**A.III.2.b** in _Ionic dialect_ Prose it sometimes stands between its genitive and the Article of that genitive, τῶν τις Περσέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; τῶν τις ἱρέων [prev. author] 2.38; τῶν τινες Φοινίκων [prev. author] 8.90; ἐς τῶν τι ἄλλο στομάτων τοῦ Νείλου [prev. author] 2.179}}; so also in late Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.108d, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1402.18, 1659.27, 1676.1.}}
**A.III.2.c** it stands between the _Article_ and _substantive_ in _signification_{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.10b.}}
**A.III.2.d** τίς τι is the correct order, not τί τις, “IG” 12.110.46, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.14 (codices dett.), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.22, etc.
**A.III.2.e** whereas in _Attic dialect_ the order ἐάν τις is compulsory, in _Doric dialect_ the usual order is αἴ τίς κα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 9.43, +others, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.105, +others (but αἴ κά τις 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 35, 159; αἰ δέ κα μή τις “Leg.Gort.” 5.13)}}: later _Doric dialect_ εἴ τί κα {{Refs|“GDI” 2101.3, +others}}; καἴ τι ἂν (={καὶ εἴ τι ἂν}) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.50 (Andania, 1st c.BC}}, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.1b)}}:—this _Doric dialect_ order influenced the Koine, as in the rare εἴ τις ἂν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 15.}}

5101:
** τίς**
**B** Interrog. _pronoun_ τίς, Elean and _Laconian dialect_ τίρ (which see), τί:—_genitive_ _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ τέο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.225, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.1, etc., or τεῦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.509, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106, etc.}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ τοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 614, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1223, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_, Trag., and _Attic dialect_ τίνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 154, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.80, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 563 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 892, Ar “Ach.” 588, etc.}}; _dative_ _Ionic dialect_ τέῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, +others (as _feminine_, 4.155)}}; no _dative_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ τῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 401, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.60, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ τίῳ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 104}}; τίνι first in {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.57, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 715 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 919, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.115, etc.; _accusative_ τίνα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.703, etc.; _neuter_ τί 1.362, etc.: dual τίνε (elided) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 107: _plural_. _nominative_ τίνες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.172, etc.; _neuter_ τίνα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 102a, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.81, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.1.4, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 17.12, NT.Heb.5.12}}; _genitive_ _Epic dialect_ τέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.387, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.192}}, and as _monosyllable_ {{Refs|6.119, 13.200}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ τίνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1476, “OC” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1089, etc.}}; _dative_ τίσι first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1126, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1455, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 332d, etc. (no _dative_ in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus); also τοῖσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 984 (_anapaest meter_)}}; _Ionic dialect_ τέοισι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37, compare 2.82 (variant{ὁτέοισι})}}]; _Aeolic dialect_ τίοισι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 168; _accusative_ τίνας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 115, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 370 (troch.); _neuter_ τίνα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.30.3}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.82 (_adverb_)}}; Megarian dialect σά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 757, 784 (_adverb_)}}: of the _plural_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only _nominative_ τίνες}} with _genitive_ τέω; ποῖος (**what? which?**) is sometimes preferred (especially in _neuter_ _plural_) to the _adjective_ τίς, e.g. τὰ ποῖα ταῦτα χρήματ; [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1270, compare 1337, “Th.” 621, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 391e, 395d, 406d; see at {ποῖος} I.3 and IV:}}]
**B.I** in direct questions, **who? which?** _neuter_ **what? which?** ὦ ξεῖνοι, τίνες ἐστ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.252; τί νύ μοι μήκιστα γένητα; 5.299; τίς δαίμων τόδε πῆμα προσήγαγ; 17.446}}; τίς ἀχώ, τίς ὀδμὰ προσέπτα μ᾽ ἀφεγγή; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 115 (Lyric poetry), compare 561 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; properly at the beginning of the sentence; but this position may be varied,
**B.I.a** for grammatical reasons, as between the _Article_ and _participle_ or noun, τοὺς τί ποιοῦντας τὸ ὄνομα τοῦτο ἀποκαλοῦσι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 206b}}; τῆς περὶ τί πειθοῦς ἡ ῥητορική ἐστιν τέχν; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 454a}}; ὁ σοφιστὴς τῶν τί σοφῶν ἐστι; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 312d.}}
**B.I.b** for emphasis, ἃ δ᾽ ἐννέπεις, κλύουσα τοῦ λέγει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 412, compare “El.” 1191}}; πόλις τε ἀφισταμένη τίς πω.. τούτῳ ἐπεχείρησ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45}}; especially when the Verb begins the sentence, δράσεις δὲ δὴ τ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1246}}; ἦλθες δὲ κατὰ τ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 239}}; διαφέρει δὲ τ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.205.--The person frequently follows in _genitive_ _plural_, as τίς θεῶ; 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.182, etc.}}; and of things or conditions, τί is frequently with the _genitive_ _singular_, of all genders, πρὸς τί χρεία; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1174}}; ἐλπίδων ἐς τ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1749 codices (Lyric poetry); κἀνήρετ᾽ ἐν τῷ πράγματος κυροῖ ποτε [prev. author] “Aj.” 314, etc.}}
**B.I.2** sometimes as the predicate, τίς ὀνομάζετα; **what** is he named? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 123}}; so also may be explained the union of τίς with a _demonstrative_ or _possessive_ _pronoun_, or with a Noun preceded by the _Article_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί ἐστι τουτ; τίς ὁ τρόπος τοῦ τάγματο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ichn.” 114}}; also with _pronoun_ in _plural_, τί ταῦτ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 382, “Andr.” 548, etc.}}; τί γὰρ τάδ᾽ ἐστί; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 200}}; τί ποτ᾽ ἐστίν, ἂ διανοούμεθ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154e}}; τί ποτ᾽ ἐστὶ ταῦτα{{Refs|[prev. work] 155c; σκεπτέον τί τὰ συμβαίνοντα [prev. author] “Grg.” 508b}}; so τί is used as predicate of a _masculine_ or _feminine_ subject, τί νιν προσείπ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 983(997)}}; τί σοι φαίνεται ὁ νεανίσκο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 154d:}} —also τίς δ᾽ ὅδε Ναυσικάᾳ ἕπεται; **who** is this that follows N.? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.276}}; τίς δ᾽ οὗτος ἔρχεα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 84.1, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 328, 388, “Ant.” 7, 218, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 501, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43c}}; and in the reverse order, τήνδε τίνα λεύσσω..; **who** is this I see? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 821}}; τίνι οὖν τοιούτῳ φίλους ἂν θηρῴη; **with what means** of such kind.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.9}}; τί τοσοῦτον νομίζοντες ἠδικῆσθα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.53}}; τί με τὸ δεινὸν ἐργάσ; **what** is the dreadful thing which.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 492, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 598, 1488, etc.}}; τίν᾽ ὄψιν σὴν προσδέρκομα; **what** face is this I see of thine? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 557}}; παρὰ τίνας τοὺς ὑμᾶ; **who** are 'you' to whom [I am to come]? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 203b:}} —the _Article_ is exceptionally added to τίς, when it leads up to a word which requires the _Article_, ληφθήσει.. Πανήμου εἰκάδι· καὶ Λῴου τῇ--τίν; τῇ δεκάτῃ on the twentieth of the month Panemus and of Loüs on the --**what** day? the tenth, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 46:}} —in Comedy texts also τὸ τί; **what** is that? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 775, “Pax” 696, “Av.” 1039, “Pl.” 902, etc.}}; τοῦ τίνος χάρι; {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 6.29 (2nd c.BC)}}; and with _plural_ _Article_, τὰ τ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 693.}}
**B.I.3** with properly names treated as appellatives (see. τις _indefinite_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.6b), τίς ἆρα Κύπρις ἢ τίς Ἵμερο; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 874}}; τίς σε Θηρικλῆς ποτε ἔτευξ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 43}}; τίς.. Χίμαιρα πύρπνοο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.3.}}
**B.I.4** τίς ἂν θεῶν.. δοί; like{πῶς ἄν}, **would that some one.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1100, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1448 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.I.5** a question with τίς often amounts to a strong negation, τῶν δ᾽ ἄλλων τίς κεν οὐνόματ᾽ εἴπο; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.260}}; τίς ἂν ἐξεύροι ποτ᾽ ἄμεινο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 498}}; τίνες ἂν δικαιότερον.. μισοῖντ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64, etc.}}
**B.I.6** sometimes two questions are asked in one clause by different cases of τί; ἡ τίσιν τί ἀποδιδοῦσα τέχνη δικαιοσύνη ἂν καλοῖτ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 332d; τί λαβόντα τί δεῖ ποιεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.36:—a like doubling of the question lies in the union of τίς with other _interrogative_ words, τίς πόθεν εἰς (εἶς codices) ἀνδρῶ; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.170, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 421.}}
**B.I.7** τίς with Particles:—τίς γά; **why who? who possibly?** τίς γάρ σε θεῶν.. ἧκε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.182; see below 8 f.}}
**B.I.7.b** τίς δ; ὦ κοῦραι, τίς δ᾽ ὔμμιν.. πωλεῖτα; {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 169.}}
**B.I.7.c** τίς δ; **who then?** τίς δή κεν βροτὸς.. ἅζοιτ᾽ ἀθανάτους {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 747}}; τίς δῆτ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 518.}}
**B.I.7.d** τίς ποτ; **who in the world? who ever?** τίς ποτ᾽ ὢν γενεὰν καὶ ποίαν τινὰ φύσιν ἔχω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.6, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 975}}; τίς δήποτ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 106 (but τίς ἄν ποτε is probably conjecture).}}
**B.I.8** the usages of the _neuter_ τ; are very various:
**B.I.8.a** τ; alone, as a simple question, **what?** τί γά; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 336 (Lyric poetry)}}:—on ὅτι τ; ὅτι τί δ; ὅτι δὴ τ; see at {ὅτι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1b}}; on ὡς τ; see {ὡς} F.1.
**B.I.8.b** τί τοῦτ; τί ταῦτ; see above 2.
**B.I.8.c** τί μο; τί σο; **what** is it to me? to thee? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 753, etc.}}; with _genitive_, τί μοι ἔριδος καὶ ἀρωγῆ; **what** have I to do with.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.360}}; τί δέ σοι ταῦτ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 514, compare “Ec.” 521}} (where the answerer repeats the question in indirect form, ὅ τί μοι τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι;); ἀλλὰ δὴ τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.18}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; **what** have I to do with thee? {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Judg.11.12, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.19, NT.John.2.4}}; τί σοὶ καὶ εἰρήν; {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.9.18, cf. LXX.Hos.14.9}}; τί πρὸσσ; {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.44, cf. NT.Matt.27.4}}; σοὶ δὲ καὶ τούτοισι τοῖσι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; **what** have you to do with these matters? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36:—followed by a clause, τί δὲ τίν, εἰ κωτίλαι εἰμέ; 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.89}}; or with _infinitive_, τί γάρ μοι τοὺς ἔξω κρίνει; {{NT|NT.1Cor.5.12:—see. εἰμί with 111.2.}}
**B.I.8.d** τίμαθώ; τί παθώ; see at {μανθάνω} see, πάσχω {{Refs|111.4.}}
**B.I.8.e** τ; also often stands _absolutely_ as _adverb_ **how? why? wherefore?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.362, etc.}}; so too in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43c, etc.}}; δόμων γὰρ ζῶσι τῶνδε δεσπόται. Answ. τί ζῶσι; **how do you mean** ζῶσι ζῶσι **forsooth!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 806}}; Κιθαιρὼν--Answ. τί Κιθαιρώ; **what about**K.? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 1177 codices, compare 1182 (both Lyric poetry)}}; compare τίη.
**B.I.8.f** τί with Particles: -τί γά; **why not? how else?** and so it came to mean **of course, no doubt**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1239, “Ch.” 880, “Eu.” 678, etc.}}; used in affirmative answers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258d, “Tht.” 209b, +others}}; to introduce an argument, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a14; see at {γάρ} I.4:}}] —τί δαί; see at {δαί}:—τί δ; serving to pass on quickly to a fresh point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 288c, +others}}; τί δέ, εἰ..; but **what**, if.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1043}}; τί δ᾽ ἄν, εἰ..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 773}}; τί δ᾽ ἢν..; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1444}}; τί δέ, εἰ μὴ..; **what** else but.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.1, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 941, “Ph.” 421}}; so τί δὲ δ; τί δ; τί δή ποτ; **why ever? why in the world? what do you mean?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 470e, “Grg.” 469a, “Sph.” 241d, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1184:}} —so also τί δῆτα; **how, pray?** τί δῆτ᾽ ἄν, εἰ..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 154:}} — (τί μή; _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 668):}} —τί μήν; i.e. **yes certainly**, much like{τί γάρ}; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 162e, etc., probably in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 668:}} —τί μὴν οὔ; in reply to a question, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1280 (Lyric poetry):}} —τί νυ; **why** now? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.414, etc.:}} —τί δ᾽ οὔ; parenthetic, **why not?** as an affirmative answer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 460}}; τί οὐ καλοῦμε; i.e. let us call, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1103}}; τί οὐ βαδίζομε; etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e, etc.:}} —τί οὖν; **how so?** making an objection, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 208; but τί οὖν ἔτ᾽ ἂν σαίνοιμεν.. μόρο; [prev. work] 704}}; τί οὖν οὐκ ἐρωτᾷ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211d:}} —τί ποτε; see at {τίπτε};
**B.I.8.g** with Conjunctions following:—τί ὅτι..; **why** is it that.. ? {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 62 (_falsa lectio_), LXX.Gen.3.1, NT.Luke.2.49, etc.:—with Conjunctions preceding, ἵνα τί}}; see at {ἵνα} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.3 with}}
**B.I.8.h** with Preps.:—διὰ τ; **wherefore?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1111, etc.:}} —ἐκ τίνος; from **what** cause? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.4:}} —ἐς τί; to **what** point? how long? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.465}}; but also, to **what** end? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 403, compare “OC” 524 (Lyric poetry):}} —κατὰ τί; for **what** purpose? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 239:}} —πρὸς τί; **wherefore?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 766, 1027, etc.}}
**B.II** τίς is sometimes used for ὅστις in indirect questions, εἰρώτα δὴ ἔπειτα τίς εἴη καὶ πόθεν ἔλθοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.423, compare 17.368; δεῖξον τίς ἔσται τῇ ταλαιπώρῳ χρόνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 623; οὐδ᾽ ἔχω τίς ἂν γενοίμαν [prev. work] 905 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐκ ἔχω τί φῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 91, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 48, etc.; ἐπισκεψώμεθα τίνες πέπανται σφενδόνας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.18; εἰπὲ τίνα γνώμην ἔχεις [prev. work] 2.2.10}}; frequently in later Gr., where ὅστις is very rare, εἰς τὸ λογιστήριον γράφων.. τί ὀφείλεται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.29.42 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 21.40, +others (3rd c.BC)}}; οὐθεὶς ἐσήμηνεν παρὰ τί ἂν τοῖς προστεταγμένοις.. οὐ κατηκολούθησαν nobody indicated **why** they should not have obeyed orders, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 72.160, compare 61 (b). 227 (2nd c.BC)}}; ὅστις and τίς are sometimes combined, ὡς πύθοιθ᾽ ὅ τι δρῶν ἢ τί φωνῶν ῥυσαίμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 71, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 489f, 617, 623:}} —later with _infinitive_, τί πράττειν οὐκ ἔχω I do not know **what** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 67, cf. Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 29.}}
**B.II.b** sometimes not in indirect questions, **whoever, whatever**, αἰτοῦ τί χρῄζεις ἕν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 773.2}}; ταῦτα οὐκ ἀπέστελλον πάντα, ἀλλ᾽ ἐκλεγόμενοι τίνων αἱ τιμαὶ ἐπετέταντο **whatever** things had risen in price, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.24}}; τίνα δ᾽ ἁ Κύπρις οὐκ ἐφίλησεν **whomsoever** K. has not loved, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.169 (3rd c.BC(?): Nossis Epigrammatica)}}; τίνι ἡ τύχη δίδωσι, λαβέτω Antiochusap.[{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Ptolemaeus Euergetes II Historicus 3 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; λαμβανέτω τί θέλει “Anthologia Graeca” 12.219 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus); τὰν ὀνάλαν κίς κε γινύειτει “IG” 9(2).517.22 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC); καὶ τί ἂν εἶ (={ᾖ}) λοιπόν [prev. work]5(1).1390.50 (Andania, 1st c.BC, nisi to be read καἴ τι ἂν}}], see above{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.2e); τίς ἂν δὲ χεῖρα προσαγάγῃ “epigram Gr.” 376a (from Aezani)}}; τίς σοφός, αὐτῷ προσκολλήθητι {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.6.34; οὐ τί ἐγὼ θίλω, ἀλλὰ τί σύ NT.Mark.14.36}}; τίς σοφίῃ πάντων πρῶτος, τούτου τρίποδ᾽ αὐδῶ Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 9.3 et cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.28 codices (ὃς}} Cobet from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 9); χαῖρε καὶ σύ, τίς ποτ᾽ εἶ “IG” 9 (2).953 (from Larissa), compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 1982 (from Thessalonica)}}; in other places, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1176, “Tr.” 339, “OT” 1144, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 324, this _construction_ cannot be admitted.}}
**B.II.c** τίς ={ὅστις} after a _negative_, μή τίς ἐστιν ἐν ὑμῖν ἀνὴρ ἢ γυνὴ.., τίνος ἡ διάνοια ἐξέκλινεν κτλ.; {{LXX|LXX.Deu.29.18.}}
**B.II.d** = {ὅς} or ὅσπερ, τέων.. Ζεὺς ἐπὶ σαλπίγγων ἱρὰ βοῇ δέχεται Κᾶρες ὁμοῦ Λελέγεσσι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.60, compare “Del.” 185, “epigram” 30.2, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 2; Δωροθέαν, τίς τὸν ἐμὸν ἄνδρα εἶχε “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 10.4 (Cnidus, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}, compare 5.2,8; τίνας ἱερεωσύνας εἶχον ἐπενεγύων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 705.43, compare 56 (Senatus consultum, Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; τίνα με ὑπονοεῖτε εἶναι, οὐκ εἰμὶ ἐγώ {{NT|NT.Act.13.25}}; τίς ἔζησεν ἔτη β **who** lived.., {{Refs|“IG” 14.1560 (from Rome), compare 1391 ([prev. passage])}}; εὗρον γεωργόν, τίς αὐτὰ ἑλκύσῃ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 822.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**B.II.2** τί; τ; in direct or indirect questions may be construed with a _participle_, σὺ δὲ τίς ὢν ταῦτα λέγει; being **who**, i.e. who are you that.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 452a; ἐπειρέσθαι.. τίνες ἐόντες ἄνθρωποι.. ταῦτα προαγορεύουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153}}; καταμεμάθηκας.. τοὺς τί ποιοῦντας τὸ ὄνομα τοῦτο ἀποκαλοῦσ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.1; or in a subordinate clause, ἀλλ᾽ ὅταν τί ποιήσωσι, νομιεῖς αὐτοὺς σοῦ φροντίζει; [prev. work] 1.4.14}}; νῦν δ᾽ ἐπειδὴ τίνος τέχνης ἐπιστήμων ἐστί, τίνα ἂν καλοῦντες αὐτὸν ὀρθῶς καλοῖμε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 448c.}}
**B.III** = {πότερος}; {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 52d, NT.Matt.27.21, NT.Luke.5.23.}}
**B.IV** τί as exclamatory _adverb_, **how.. **! τί ὡραιώθησαν σιαγόνες σου ὡς τρυγόνες {{LXX|LXX.Cant.1.10, compare 4.10}}; τί θέλω **how** I wish! {{NT|NT.Luke.12.49}}; τί στενή variant in {{NT|NT.Matt.7.14.}}
**C** Prosody: τις and τίς keep ῐ in all cases (digamma operates in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.462, etc.).}}
**C.II** τί was never elided; but hiatus is allowed after τί in _Epic dialect_ τί ἢ (see. τίη), also in Comedy texts, as τί ο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 149}}; τί οὖ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 94}}; τί ἔστ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 82, “Av.” 1036}}; τί, ὦ πάτε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 80:—a licence which is rarer in Trag., τί ἔστι; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 733}}; τί οὖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 208, 704, “Eu.” 902, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 873 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 100, etc.}}; τί εἶπα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1203, “Ph.” 917.}}

5102:
** τίτλ-ος**, ὁ,
Latin _titulus, title, inscription,_ {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.John.19.20, “IG” 22.1121.26,41 (4th c.AD), +others, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 1.19}}: also the **stone** bearing the inscription, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 12(7).259.10 (Amorgos, 3rd c.AD), “Supp.epigram” 6.305, +others (from Lycaonia), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus: also _feminine_, ἀνεστήσαμεν τὴν τ. ταύτην “Supp.epigram” 6.370 ([prev. passage], 4th c.AD), compare 284 ([prev. passage]).}}
**2** **tattoo-mark**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor in Rhetores Graeci 7(1).676 W.}}
**II** **title, section**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 29.4.}}

5105:
** τοιγάρ**,
an inferential Particle, **therefore, accordingly, well then,** κέλεαί με.. μυθήσασθαι μῆνιν Ἀπόλλωνος.. · τοιγὰρ ἐγὼν ἐρέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.76, compare 10.427, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.254, 8.402, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 309, “Th.” 1038, “Pers.” 607, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 931 (_anapaest meter_), 994, “Aj.” 666, etc.}}; rare in Comedy texts {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 516 (_anapaest meter_), 901, 902) and Prose (5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.114, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 10 (assuming variant))}}; never in _Attic dialect_ Prose.
**II** strengthened by other Particles, **τοιγαροῦν**, _Ionic dialect_ **τοιγαρῶν**, **for that very reason, therefore,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.149, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 234e, 246b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.9, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.40, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271b3, etc.}}; also in Poets, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 490, “OT” 1519 (troch.), “Ph.” 341, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1098, etc.}}
**II.2** **τοιγάρτοι**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 82d, etc.}}; rare in Poets, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 145, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 654 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 643 (_anapaest meter_):— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always inserts a word between τοιγάρ}} and τοι, τοιγὰρ ἐγώ τοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.413, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.179, 214, +others}} —These forms must begin the sentence, except in late Gr., where τοιγαροῦν may be postponed, as {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.8, Wilcken “Chr.” 491.7 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ τῆς τάξεως τῶν ἰδίων βιβλίων” 2, Vett. Val. 356.3, “Sammelb.” 6222.12 (3rd c.AD), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 318d. (It is doubtful whether τοι- contains the _demonstrative_ stem το-.)}}

5106:
** τοίνυν**,
(τοι, νυν) **therefore, accordingly,** an inferential Particle (never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus), χρὴ τ. πύλας ὕμνων ἀναπιτνάμεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.27, etc.}}; εἰ τ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.57}}; ἂν τ... {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.7}}; introducing a logical conclusion (less frequently than οὖν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 159d}}; φανερὸν τ., δῆλον τ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1260a2, “PA” 641a15}}; also to introduce a minor premiss, or a particular instance of a general proposition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.103, etc.}}
**2** in dialogue, to introduce an answer, **well** or **well then,** ἄπειμι τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1050, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.89, etc.}}; especially an answer which has been led up to by the same speaker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76a, “IG” 42(1).121.31 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; in response to an invitation to speak, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 961, etc.}}; in expression of approval, especially in phrase καλῶς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 433a, etc.; κάλλιστα τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 856}}; also of disapproval or criticism, ἀπόλοιο τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1236, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1067.}}
**3** continuing an argument, **well then,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 178d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.36, 7.7.28, etc.}}
**3.b** resuming the thread of argument or narrative after a break, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562b, “Plt.” 275d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.64, etc.}}
**3.c** adding or passing to a fresh item or point, **further, moreover, again,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 33c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.73, 20.18; ἔτι τ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109a, “Cri.” 52c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.8; καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.25; καί τ. καί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 234a}}; μὴ τ. μηδέ.. **nay,** not even.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.19; οὐ τ. οὐδέ}}**nor again,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 57, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.7.}}
**4** sometimes at the beginning of a speech, ἐγὼ μὲν τ..., referring to something present to the minds of the speaker and hearer, **now** I.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.2, compare “Cyr.” 6.2.14.}}
**5** with _subjunctive_ of exhortation or _imperative_, in _significations_ {{NT|1, 2, 3, εὖ τ. ἐπίστασθε.. [prev. author] “An.” 3.1.36, compare “Cyr.” 2.4.8, NT.Luke.20.25, etc.}}
**B** Position: in early writers τοίνυν is never the first word in a sentence, but this is not uncommon in later authors, as {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Isa.3.10, “Mim. Oxy.” 413.225, NT.Luke.20.25, NT.Heb.13.13, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.526, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.429, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.127 (2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus), “IG” 4.620.13 (from Argos), 6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor 32.34}} F.-R. codex (<τῷ> add. Kaibel); it is usually placed second, but sometimes later, ἥξω φέρουσα συμβολὰς τ. ἅμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 143.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 863, etc.}} [ῠ regularly, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 760, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 71}}: but sometimes ῡ, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1259, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor [same place]; in _anapaest meter_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 429, 435, “Av.” 481.]}}

5108:
** τοιοῦτος**, αύτη, οῦτο,
_Attic dialect_ also **-οῦτον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 281b, etc.}}, which is the _Epic dialect_ form (see. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.309, 13.330)}}, and seems to prevail in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (2.5, 150, 3.27, 85, 5.106 (variant), 7.103), 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus (2.20, +others), etc., while we find τοιοῦτο in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 801, “Ag.” 315, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1399 codex Rav., 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 160, “Pk.” 236, see reading in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86}}: both forms occur in Papyrus, τοιοῦτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.29.17 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 146.8, 32 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.249, 2.270 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Ir.” p.33 W.; τοιοῦτο “PEnteux.” 27.8 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 379.8, 482.13 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.42 W., “Rh.” 2.243 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (citing 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus)}}:—_Aeolic dialect_ **τέουτος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Lyrica Adespota” 51 (codex A 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus); _feminine_ τεαύτα 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 13.4, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.5, 25.10:}} —a stronger form of τοῖος, bearing the same relation to τοιόσδε as οὗτος to ὅδε, **such as this,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus not so common as τοῖος}} but in _Attic dialect_ the most common of the three forms; _antecedent_ to οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.269, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 199d, etc.}}; to ὡς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.428}}; to ὅς, ὅς, ὅσπερ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 691, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.1, 30.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 7.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349d, etc.}}; to οἷόσπερ, see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.2}}; less frequently to a _conjunction_, as ὥστε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1075, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175d: frequently also _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.16, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, etc.}}; frequently with implications, **so good, so noble, so bad,** etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.242, etc.}}; τοιοῦτον.. ἐστὶ τὸ.. τέλειον ἄνδρα εἶναι **so great a thing** is it.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 281b}}; τοιοῦτος ὤν being **such a wretch,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1298, compare “Ph.” 1049}}; εἴς τι τοιοῦτον ἐμπίπτειν οὗ.. into **such a condition** in which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511c}}; frequently coupled with τοσοῦτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.6, etc.}}; with οὗτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 461e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.26, etc. (so in the expression οὗτος τοιοῦτος, αὕτη τοιαύτη}} **just as** he (she, it) is, of slaves or animals for sale, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 95.19 (2nd c.AD), etc.)}}; εἰς σὲ τοιοῦτος ἐγένετο, τ. γίγνου περὶ τοὺς γονεῖς, **so disposed** towards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.14: with _dative_, τ. ἦσθα τοῖς λόγοισι}} **such** in thy words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1271: τ. ἕτερος}} **such** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.47}}; ἕτερα τοιαῦτα, ἕτερον τοιοῦτον, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.120, 2.5}}; referring to what precedes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 904d}}; used instead of repeating an _adjective_, ἀθάνατος εἶναι καὶ στρατιῆς τ. ἄρχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, compare 3.82, 7.10. έ, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58}}: with the _Article_, οἱ τοιοῦτοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 952, “Ch.” 291, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 642, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 42; τὰ τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.40; ὀνόματι ὁ τοιοῦτος ἐμὲ προσαγορεύων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.40 codex 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}
**2** the sense is made more _indefinite_ in τοιοῦτός τις or τις τοιοῦτος **such a** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.16, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, etc.; τοιαῦτ᾽ ἄττα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 386a}}; in this case it may frequently be rendered by an _adverb_, ἡ διάρριψις τοιαύτη τις ἐγένετο took place **in this wise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.7; ἐγένετο ἡ διακομιδὴ τοιαύτη τις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.45.6.}}
**3** τὸτ. **such a proceeding,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76, etc.}}; διὰ τὸ τ. for **such a reason,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.21; ἐκ τοῦ τ. [prev. author] 3.37}}; ἐν τῷ τ. in **such a case,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 81, etc. (but also ἐν τῷ τ. in}}**such a place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.7; ἐν τ. τῆς οἰκίας [prev. author] “Eq.” 4.1)}}; also ἐν τ. εἶναι τοῦ κινδύνου to be in **such a state** of peril, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.7.5.}}
**4** in narrative, τοιαῦτα properly refers to what goes before, τοιαῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 500; καὶ ταῦτα μὲν τ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 696, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.12, etc.; compare τοιόσδε near the end}}
**4.b** after a question, τοιαῦτα affirms like{ταῦτα} (see. οὗτος with VII.1), **just so, even so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 776, “El.” 645.}}
**5** τοιαῦτα _absolutely_, τἂ πλοῖα, τὰ τοιαῦτα ships **and suchlike,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.25.}}
**6** τοιαῦτα as _adverb_, **in such wise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1327: regular}} _adverb_ τοιούτως only late, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 650.42.}} —Cf. τοσοῦτος. [τοῐ- frequently in Trag. and Comedy texts, e.g. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 593, “Eu.” 194, 197, 424, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 406, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1399, etc.}}; compare τοιόσδε near the end]

5109:
** τοῖχος**, ὁ,
**wall of a house** or **enclosure,** _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.342, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 130, etc.}}; ἅπαντ᾽ ἐρευνῶν τ. uncertain in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1174; τ. δώματος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.212; μεγάροιο 18.374, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.37}}; τ. καὶ θριγκοί (of the αὐλή){{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.267, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 732; τὸν τῆς αὐλῆς τ. “PEnteux.” 12.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; **wall of a temple,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.372.51, +others; οἰκίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574d; ἐν τοῖσι τ. ἔγραφ᾽ Ἀθηναῖοι καλοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 144, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 859a; εἰς τὸν τ. ἀντεγγραψάτω “IG” 12.94.24}}; νόμους ἀναγράφειν εἰς τοῖχον Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.84}}; κοινοὶ τ. party-**walls**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 483.101, +others (Pergam., 2nd c.AD)}}; τοίχῳ προσιστάμενοι γυμνάζονται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.144}}: of the **side** of a tent or hut, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.219, 24.598, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1158.}}
**b** _metaphorically_, τοῖχε κεκονιαμένε, as a term of abuse, {{NT|NT.Act.23.3.}}
**2** _plural_, **sides of a ship,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.420, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 674, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1573, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.12; τοίχου ἄρχω τοῦ δεξιοῦ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 14.3, compare “JTr.” 49.}}
**3** of other things, as the human body, εἰς ἀμφοτέρους τοίχους (by _metaphorically_ from a ship) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 118 (_anapaest meter_), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 9}}; of a cup, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.2}}; of a vessel, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 359a3}}; of a bath (πύελος), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.709.}}
**4** proverbial, τοίχους τοὺς δύο ἐπαλείφειν 'to run with the hare and hunt with the hounds', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.3.15, compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {δύο τοίχου; ὁ εὖ πράττων τ}. 'the snug side of the ship', 'the right side of the hedge', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 537 (Lyric poetry); ἐς το᾽ν εὐτυχῆ τ. χωρεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 89. (Akin to τεῖχος}} but used in a special sense; later ={τεῖχος}, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.25.12, probably so in “JHS” 24.39 (from Cyzicus).)}}

5110:
** τόκος**, ὁ,
(τίκτω) **childbirth, parturition,** of women, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.119, “hymnus ad Cererem” 101, “IG” 42(1).121.15,17 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.53}}; of animals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.5; πλὴν ὅταν τ. παρῇ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 477; ποιεῖσθαι τὸν τ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 542a25, etc.: _plural_, τόκοισί τε ἀγόνοις γυναικῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 26, compare 173 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1031, etc.}}
**b** **the time of parturition,** ὁ τ. τῆς γυναικός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111}}; **period of gestation,** ἐνιαύσιος ὁ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 777b13.}}
**c** ἡ φύσις τοῦ παιδίου τοῦ ἐν τόκῳ in the **foetal stage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Nat.Puer.” at Title Page (as cited in “Mul.” 1.1).}}
**II** **offspring,** of men or animals, πάντων Ἀργείων ἐρέων γενεήν τε τόκον τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.128, compare 15.141}}; of an eagle, ἐλθὼν ἐξ ὄρεος, ὅθι οἱ γενεή τε τόκος τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.175; μήλων τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 162}}; Οἰδίπον τ. his **son,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 372, compare 407, etc.}} (but also, **daughter,** θεὰ γεγῶσα καὶ θεοῦ πατρὸς τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” [1254])}}; **fry** of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 543a4}}; **litter** of pigs, πασῶν τῶν συῶν ἀπὸ τόκου χοῖρον λαμβάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 15.5.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **produce of money** lent, hence **interest** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 477 (punningly), 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 555e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1258b5), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).9, etc.; τ. ἐπίτριτος, see entry .; τ. πεντώβολος interest at 5}} obols per month on the mina, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).146 B 17 (Delos, 4th c.BC)}}; τ. πεντεκαιδέκατος, i. e. 100/15 = 6 2/3%, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 672.23 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; _singular_ and _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.324.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 18, 20, 34, etc.; τόκους ἀποδοῦναι [prev. work] 739, etc.}}; κομίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus [same place], “PEnteux.” 32.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; λαμβάνειν ἀπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.35; ἀπολαμβάνειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.3; δανείζειν ἐπὶ τόκῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742c; ὀφείλειν ἐπὶ τόκῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.7; τόκοι τόκων}}**compound interest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1156; τόκος τόκου 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 10.10; τῶν τόκων ἔχων τόκους 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 870:— 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus}} plays on the double meaning of the word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Th.” 843f; so also 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.433e.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **interest,** [γῄδιον] ὅτι λάβοι σπέρμα.. δικαίως ἀπεδίδου αὐτό τε καὶ τόκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.38, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 231, 88.10; οἱ δ᾽ εἰς τὸ γῆρας ἀναβολὰς ποιούμενοι, οὗτοι προσαποτίνουσι τοῦ χρόνου τόκους 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 235.9}}; **offspring,** ἢ τίκτων λόγους ἢ τὸν ἑτέρων τόκον λαμβάνων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 12.94}}; **bringing forth,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 17.38.}}
**II.4** **oppression,** as translation of Hebrew τōκη, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.71(72).14, LXX.Jer.9.6.}}

5111:
** τολμ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **τολμέω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77}}; _Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ τολμῇς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.35}}: _future_ τολμήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 471}}, _Doric dialect_ **-ᾱσῶ**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.67}}: _perfect_ τετόλμηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 333}}, _Doric dialect_ **-ᾱκα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.117}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Oxy.” 1606.420 (“Bodl. Quarterly Record” 5 (1928).303)}}:—**undertake, take heart** either **to do** or **bear** anything terrible or difficult:
**1** mostly _absolutely_, **dare, endure, submit** (see. Τλάω), ἐνὶ φρεσὶ θυμὸς ἐτόλμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.232}}; σὺ δ᾽ (i.e. κραδίη) ἐτόλμας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.20; οὐδέ οἱ ἵπποι τόλμων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.51; ἐγὼ δ᾽ ἐτόλμησ᾽ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 237, etc.; αἱ συμφοραὶ τοὺς ἡσυχίους τολμᾶν βιάζονται 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.1; τ. καὶ ἐκλογίζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40}}; τολμῶντες ἄνδρες{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 43, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 583; χρὴ τολμᾶν.. ἐν ἄλγεσι κείμενον ἄνδρα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 555; τόλμα κακοῖσιν [prev. author] 355, 1029; τόλμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 82; τόλμησον [prev. work] 481: in}}_participle_, τολμήσας.. παρέστη **he took courage** and.., {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 22, cf. NT.Mark.15.43.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **endure, undergo,** τ. χρὴ τὰ διδοῦσι θεοί {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 591, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 333, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872e.}}
**II** with _infinitive_, **to have the courage, hardihood, effrontery, cruelty,** or **the grace, patience,** to do a thing in spite of any natural feeling, **dare,** or **bring oneself,** to do, εἰ.. τολμήσεις Διὸς ἄντα.. ἔγχος ἀεῖραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.424, compare 13.395, 17.68, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.332, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 528, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 550, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.2, etc.; τόλμησον ὀρθῶς φρονεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 999, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 81, 377, etc.}}; τ. κατακεῖσθαι **submit** to keep one's bed, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 10; τ. ἀποθανεῖν NT.Rom.5.7; οὐδὲ ἀπαιτούμενοι τὸν λόγον ἐτόλμησαν ἡμῖν δοῦναι “PCair.Zen.” 330.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** sometimes with _participle_, ἐτόλμα.. βαλλόμενος **he submitted to be** struck, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.162; τόλμα ἐρῶσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 476, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 442, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 756.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_, πόλεμον τολμήσαντα **undertaking, venturing on** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.519; [ἐσόδους] τετόλμακε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.117}}; τ. πάντα, δεινά, ἔργον αἴσχιστον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 761, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 133 (_anapaest meter_), “Med.” 695, etc.; ὦ πᾶν σὺ τολμήσασα καὶ πέρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 189; also τ. τὰ βέλτιστα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56, 4.98; πικρὰν πεῖραν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 471}}; see at {τόλμημα}:—hence in _passive_, ο ἐτολμήθη πατήρ such things as my father **had dared** (or **done**) **against him,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 277}}; τοῦτο τετολμήσθω εἰπεῖν **let us take courage** to say this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 503b; τὰ τολμηθέντα 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.3.1; αἱ τετολμημέναι ἐπίνοιαι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.674; τὰ τετ. εἰς ἐμέ “PGoodsp.Cair.” 15.3 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.4** so in _active_, τετολμηκυῖαι [λέξεις] **daring** expressions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.341 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

5114:
Included with: ** τόμος**, ὁ,
**slice,** τ. ἐκ πτέρνης {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 37}}; γαστρός, πλακοῦντος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1179, 1190; τῆς χορδῆς 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 192}}; ἀλλάντων, πυοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.8, 19; γογγυλίδος 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 88}}; τυροῦ, ἡνύστρου, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 150.2, 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.14 (_anapaest meter_)}}: generally, **piece,** κιθῶνος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Michel” 832.20 (Samos, 4th c.BC)}}; of wood, **beam,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).161 D 123, 165.49 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **piece of land,** {{Refs|[prev. work]7.3170.12 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_), compare 1739, 1742 (from Thespiae).}}
**3** Geometry texts, τ. κυλίνδρου **frustum** of a cylinder, portion of right cylinder intercepted between two parallel oblique sections, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra}} **Con.Sph.Def.;** τ. ἀπὸ ὀρθογωνίου κώνου τομᾶς ἀφαιρούμενος **frustum** of the section of a right-angled cone, i e. portion of a parabola cut off by two parallel double-ordinates, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aequil.” 2.10.}}
**II** **roll** of papyrus, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 357.15 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Isa.8.1, “PSI” 10.1146.1 (2nd c.AD), “Sammelb.” 7362.1 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; τ. συγκολλήσιμος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.41.18 (1st c.AD)}}; τιμῆς ἀπὸ τόμου ἀγραφίου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PMich.Teb.” 123vvii 25 (1st c.AD)}}; **tome, volume,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PMich. in “Class.Phil.” 22.10 (2nd c.AD), 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.15: _metaphorically_, ἐν καθαρῷ διανοίας τ. 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 32.}}

5115:
** τόξον**, τό,
**bow,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.124, etc.: frequently in _plural_ τόξα for _sing._, τόξ᾽ ὤμοισιν ἔχων ἀμφηρεφέα τε φαρέτρην 1.45, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.101, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 654; sometimes in Prose, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 51, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.106, “PEleph.” 5.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐτιταίνετο.. τόξα drew **the bow,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.97; also τόξον τιταίνει 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.43}}; τόξον ἕλκετ᾽ (variant{εἷλκεν}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.582; τόξου πῆχυν ἀνέλκειν 13.583}}; τόξον τείνειν, ἐντείνειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 364 (_anapaest meter_), “fragment” 83; τ... ἐντανύσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.245, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173; κυκλοτερὲς μέγα τ. ἔτεινε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.124, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1066}}; τόξου ῥῦμα (i.e. the Persians, the bow being an oriental weapon), opposed to λόγχης ἰσχύς (i.e. the Greeks), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 147 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** τόξῳ **by guess,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 1033.}}
**3** **bowmanship, archery,** τόξων ἐῢ εἰδώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.718, +others; τόξοισιν πίσυνος 5.205, compare 13.716; ἡ τέχνη τῶν τ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73; πρὸς τόξου κρίσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 266}}; τόξῳ (i.e. νικῶν) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 1061.10 (Samos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II** in _plural_ also, **bow and arrows,** τόξα πεπτεῶτ᾽ ἄλλυδις ἄλλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.502, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 68, +others}}; sometimes in _plural_ for **the arrows** only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 652, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 815a.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, τόξα ἡλίου its **rays,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1090}}; ἀμπελίνοις τόξοις δαμέντες, of the effects of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 218; τόξον μερίμνης “Tragica Adespota” 354}}; κότταβος.. ὃν σκοπὸν ἐς λατάγων τόξα καθιστάμεθα for **shooting** of liquor from the cup, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 2.2.}}
**IV** **rainbow,** [{{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschrio Lyricus 4, LXX.Gen.9.13, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {εἴρη}.}}]
**IV.2** **arch,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 9.694.}}
**IV.3** **curved support, cradle** used in amputations, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 47.13.6}}; part of a carriage or cart, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.133, 3p.144 (3rd c.BC).}}

5117:
** τόπος**, ὁ
(_feminine_ by attraction τόπον τὰν καλειμέναν Δαματρείαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).32.80 (Stiris, 2nd c.BC))}}, **place, region,** first in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus (see. below)}}, afterwards frequently in all writers; _periphrastic_, χθονὸς πᾶς τ., i.e. the whole earth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 249; ἐς τὸν Ἑλλήνων τ. [prev. author] “Pers.” 790}}; ἐν Ἑλλάδος τόποις in Greece,{{Refs|[prev. work] 796; ἐν Αὐλίδοστ. [prev. author] “Ag.” 191 (Lyric poetry); Πέλοπος ἐντ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 703, compare 292}}; πρὸς ἑσπέρους τ. towards the West, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 350}}; πρόσθε Σαλαμῖνος τόπων before Salamis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 447; Θρῄκης ἐκ τόπων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 67; Διρκαίων ἐκ τ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 1027 (Lyric poetry)}}: so in Prose, **district,** ὁ τ. ὁ Ἑλληνικός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.107, compare “Ep.” 1.8; ὁ περὶ Θρᾴκην τ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.59; ὁ ἐπὶ Θρᾴκης τ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.9, 3.73}}; ὁ τ. οὗτος, ἐν τούτοις τοῖς τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.4, “Cyr.” 2.4.20}}; ὅλος τ. a whole **region,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.230; κατὰ τόπους καὶ κώμας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 119a}}; οἱ τῆς χώρας τ. the **places** of a country, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 760c, etc. (but ὁ τ. τῆς χώρας}} the **geographical position,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.31}}; **region,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705c)}}; ὁ ἅγιοστ., of Jerusalem, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.2.18 (compare below 5)}}; the universe divided into three τόποι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 706b3, “Cael.” 312a8 (contrast “PA” 666a15, etc.)}}; οἱ κοινοὶ τ. public **sites** or **buildings,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).65.8 (Epid.); ἄσυλος τ. “BGU” 1053 ii 9 (1st c.BC), “PTeb.” 5.83 (_plural_, 2nd c.BC)}}; οἰκίαι καὶ τόποι houses and **sites,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 281.12 (2nd c.BC)}}; so ψιλοὶ τ. **sites** not built upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 52.2 (Ptolemais, 3rd-2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **place, position,** οὐ τὸν τρόπον, ἀλλὰ τὸν τ. μόνον μεταλλάξαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.78}}; ὑπολιποῦ τ. leave a **space** (in a document), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 327.83 (3rd c.BC); περικήπῳ τ. καταλιπεῖν [prev. work] 193.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. ἔχειν have a **place,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 23, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.646a}}; φίλου τ. ἔχειν hold **the place** of.., {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.4.5; Μερόλας ὁ αἱρεθεὶς ὕπατος εἰς τὸν τοῦ Κίννα τ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 38 / 39.3; ἐνεγράφη εἰς τὴν ἱερωσύνην εἰς τὸν Αευκίου Δομιτίου τ. τετελευτηκότος 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “fragment” 127.4J., compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.73; ἀναπληροῦν τὸν τ. τοῦ ἰδιώτου NT.1Cor.14.16}}; τ. ἔχειν also = have **room** (to grow), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.7.1}}; τόπῳ with _genitive_, **in place of, instead of,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.14.5}}; ἀνὰ τόπον on **the spot, immediately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 604 (Lyric poetry, uncertain reading); so ἐν τόπῳ “IG” 12(7).515.63 (from Amorgos)}}; ἐπὶ τόπου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.73.8; ἐπὶ τῶν τ. “PEnteux.” 55.5 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 70.16 (2nd c.BC), “CIL” 3.567.3 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “POxy.” 2106.23 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.1}}; παρὰ τόπον at a wrong **place,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.21, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.21.16 (but παρὰ τ. καὶ παρὰ καιρόν}} by virtue of the **place** and the time, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.21.14).}}
**3** **place** or **part** of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.46, “Loc.Hom.” at Title Page, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.40, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus}} in titles of works, e.g. περὶ τῶν πεπονθότων τόπων, περὶ συνθέσεως φαρμάκων τῶν κατὰ τόπου; especially ὁ τόπος, **pudendum muliebre,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 572b28, 583a15, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.62 (_plural_).}}
**4** **place, passage** in an author, κατὰ τόπους τινὰς τῆς ἱστορίας {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.25f. 1, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.63, NT.Luke.4.17, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.57, 58, etc.}}; the word is probably interpolated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.20.}}
**5** **burial-place,** {{NT|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(7).401 (from Amorgos), +others, NT.Mark.16.6}}; also in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1041 (perhaps to be read τάφον)}}; later ὁ ἅγιος τ. is frequently of the **grave** of a martyr, or of a **monastery** associated with it, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 94.18 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**6** in Egypt, **district, department,** a entry-division of the νομός, ={τοπαρχία}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 43.8 (3rd c.BC), “Theb.Ostr.” 27.2 (2nd c.BC): but most frequently in _plural_, ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν τ. στρατηγός, πράκτωρ, etc., “PEnteux.” 27.9 (3rd c.BC), “PRein.” 7.17, 35 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; οἱ ἔξω τ. uncertain meaning in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 87.2 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1114.6 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**7** a **room** in a house, τόπον ἕνα ἄνευ ἐνοικίου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 896.4 (2nd c.AD)}}; δύο τόπους ἤτοι συμπόσια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“POxy.” 1129.10 (V 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator), compare 502.34 (2nd c.AD), 912.13 (3rd c.AD).}}
**8** **position** on the zodiac, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 139.13}}; especially the twelve **regions**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|of 300, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 128, 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.12.}}
**9** αὐτὸς ὁ θεὸς καλεῖται τόπος, τῷ περιέχειν τὰ ὅλα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.630, compare “Corpus Hermeticum” 2.12, 3rd c.AD: Hippolytus Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Refutatio Omnium Haeresium” 6.32.}}
**II** **topic,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.109, 10.38, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.216, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.19.2, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.119S., etc.}}
**II.2** **common-place** or **element** in Rhetoric, ὁ τοῦ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1358a14, compare 1396b30, 1397a7; τὸ αὐτὸ λέγω στοιχεῖον καὶ τ. [prev. work] 1403a18: _plural_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.226S.}}
**II.2.b** ={ὁμολογουμένου πράγματος αὔξησις}, Hermog {{Refs|“Prog.” 11; κοινὸς τ. [prev. work]6.}}
**II.2.c** generally, **sphere,** ὁ πραγματικὸς τ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 1.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **opening, occasion, opportunity,** ἐν τ. τινὶ ἀφανεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54 (but τρόπῳ is probably conjecture)}}; ὀργῇ διδόναι τ. {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.462b; μὴ δίδοτε τ. τῷ διαβόλῳ NT.Eph.4.27}}; δότε τ. τῇ ὀργῇ leave **room** for the wrath (of God), i.e. let God punish, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Rom.12.19; μὴ καταλείπεσθαί σφισι τ. ἐλέους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.88.2; μετανοίας τ. οὐχ εὗρε NT.Heb.12.17; οὐδὲ φυγῆς τόπον εὐμοιρήσαντες 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 6.13}}; τ. διδόναι τινί with _infinitive_, give **occasion** to.., {{LXX|LXX.Sir.4.5.}}

5118:
** τοσοῦτος**, αύτη, οῦτο
(or τοσοῦτον, see entry near the end); _Epic dialect_ **τοσσοῦτος**; _Aeolic dialect_ **τεσσοῦτος** (which see); ={τόσος} in all senses, but like{τοσόσδε} with stronger _demonstrative_ sense: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has both common and _Epic dialect_ forms, but not so frequently as τόσος or τόσσος}} while in Trag. (not in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus) it is common, and in Prose the prevailing form, compare τοσόσδε:—frequently answered by the Relat. ὅσος, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1076, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330b, etc.}}; by ὁπόσος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 214a, etc.}}; by ὅστις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49}}; also by _adverb_ ὡς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.402}}; τ. ἐγένετο ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 1.9}}: frequently also, like{τοσόσδε}, _absolutely_, ἄφενος τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.99}}; of persons, **so large, so tall,** etc., καί σε τ. ἔθηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.485}}; also, **so great** (in rank, skill, or character), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1140, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177c, etc.; τ. καὶ τοιοῦτον τὸ θεῖον ὥσθ᾽ ἅμα πάνθ᾽ ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.18; τηλικοῦτος καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177a}}: _plural_, **so many,** τ. ἔτεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.328}}; [χρήματα] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.258: with a qualifying word, mostly in _accusative_, μεγάθεα τοσοῦτοι}} **so** big, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103}}; τοσοῦτος τὸ βάθος **so** deep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7; τοσοῦτοι τὸ πλῆθος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283b12; τὴν ἡλικίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 50}}; also τοσοῦτος ἐν κακίᾳ (variant{εἰς κακίαν}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 1; τοσοῦτος ἡλικίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 69 (assuming variant): with numeral _adverbs_, δὶς τ., πολλάκις τ., etc., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330b, etc.; also ἕτερον τοσοῦτο}}**of the same height,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149}}; ἕτεροι or ἄλλοι τοσοῦτοι **to the same number,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.21: εἰς τοσούτους τεταγμένοι}} drawn up **only so few** in file (opposed to οὕτω βαθεῖα φάλαγξ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.3.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.29.}}
**II** _neuter_ as _substantive_, **so much, thus much,** τοσσοῦτον ὀνήσιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.402, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 836, “OC” 790; τ. οἶδα [prev. author] “Aj.” 748, compare 441, etc.}}; referring to what precedes, τοσαῦτα.. εἰρήσθω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.113; τοσαῦτ᾽ ἔλεξε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 372, compare “Pr.” 621, “Ag.” 680, etc.: frequently with Preps., διὰ τοσούτου}} at **so small a distance,** so near at hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; ἐς τοσοῦτο (ν)}}**so far,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.113, 6.134}}; ἐς τ. ἥκομεν, ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.10; ἐς τ. ἐλπίδων βεβώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 771, compare “OC” 748, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 832, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25e, etc.}}; ἐκ τ. from **so far, so far** off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.16}}; ἐν τοσούτῳ in **the meantime,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 420, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.64}}; ἐπὶ τοσοῦτο **so far,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1300a9}}; κατὰ τοσοῦτον **so far,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 129a, etc.}}; μέχρι τοσούτου ἕως ἂν.. **so far, so long,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23; παρὰ τοσοῦτον ἐλθεῖν κινδύνου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.49, 7.2, compare 6.37: τοσούτου δέω, see at {δέω} (B) 1.2.}}]
**III** _neuter_ also as _adverb_, **so much, so far,** ἢ τοσσοῦτον.. ἢ ἔτι μᾶσσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.203; τ. ὀδύρομαι 21.250}}; σθένειν τ. ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 453, etc.}}; τοσοῦτον, ὅσον.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.49, compare 1.11, 88, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.45, etc.: _plural_, τοσαῦτα μάχεσθαι ὅσα ἀναγκάζονται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.108a: with _adjectives_, τοσοῦτον φιλέλλην 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hippocr.” 8; νεώτατος τ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.476; τ. εὐτυχέστεροι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.16:}} —but τοσούτῳ is more frequently with Comparatives, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 576b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.4, etc.}}; or with words implying comparison, τοσούτῳ διέφερεν ὥστε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|.. [prev. work] 3.1.10, compare “An.” 1.5.9. (The _neuter_ is τοσοῦτον (_Epic dialect_ also τοσσοῦτον) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.476, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 621, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 771, +others}}, and _Attic dialect_ generally (very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Prt.” 314b, +others, but τοσοῦτο}} is found in {{Refs|“Tht.” 153a}} as cited by {{Refs|Anonymus “in Tht.”): τοσοῦτο}} is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.35}} (τοσοῦθ᾽ ὅσον) and in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 201, 427, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 832, where τοσοῦτον}} (which is variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same place])}} is metrically possible; also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (passim) and as see reading in codex B of 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.59 and codices CG of [prev. author] 8.76}}, in all codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.34, 6.17}} and in the first hand of codex X in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|14.2, also in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.12; so later, “PCair.Zen.” 367.38, “PMich.Zen.” 28.17 (both 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.47 W., “Rh.” 1.206S.; τοσοῦτ᾽ ἐπ᾽ αὐτούς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.23; τοσοῦτ᾽ ἀπέχειν 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 36(48).100.)}}

5119:
** τότε**,
_Doric dialect_ **τόκᾰ** (which see) (both forms in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (τότε “P.” 2.89, τόκα “O.” 6.66) and 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus (τότε 13.23, τόκα 7.154))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **τότᾰ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 1789 “fragment” 1i12 (τόκα}} _accusative_ to codices of {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.39}}, compare ποτα, ὄτα):— _adverb_ **at that time, then,** corresponding to Relat. ὅτε or ὁπότε (below {{Refs|1.5), and to _interrogative_ πότε}}; mostly of some point in past time, opposed to νῦν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.724, etc.: with _genitive_, τ. τοῦ χειμῶνος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31}}: also of a future time, τότε κέν μιν.. πεπίθοιμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.100, compare 4.182; λέξεις καὶ τότ᾽ εἴσομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1517 (troch.)}} (or of imagined circumstances, **in that case** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (νῦν 1.4), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 334c)}}; sometimes, **then, next,** πρῶτον μὲν.., εἶτα.., καὶ τ... {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.48}}:—in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ also in _indefinite_ sense, **formerly,** καὶ νῦν θ᾽ ὁμοίως καὶ τότ᾽ ἐξεπίσταμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 907, compare “Ant.” 391, “Aj.” 650, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1117, “Lys.” 1023 (Lyric poetry); ὃ δὴ καὶ τότε ἐλέγομεν}}**just now,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157a}}; τ. μὲν.., νῦν δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 799 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 915 (_anapaest meter_); νῦν.. τότ᾽ [prev. author] “Med.” 1402 (_anapaest meter_); ὁμοῖοι καὶ τ. καὶ νῦν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86, compare 3.40, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.12; also τότ᾽ ἢ τόθ᾽, ὅτε τὸ κύριον μόλῃ}}**at one time or other,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 766 (Lyric poetry); συμφοραὶ.. βροτοῖσιν ἢ τότ᾽ ἦλθον ἢ τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 853.}}
**2** joined with other Particles, καὶ τ. even **then,** or (at the beginning of a clause) and **then,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.691, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 536, etc.; καὶ τ. δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.92, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.299; καὶ τότ᾽ ἔπειτα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.426; καὶ τ. μέν 21.40; δὴ τ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 417, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.25, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 214 (Lyric poetry), etc.; τ. δή ῥα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.52; τ. γ᾽ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.224, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.250; δὴ τ. γ᾽ 15.228}}; τ. δ᾽ ἤδη by **that time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.699}}; ἀλλὰ τότ᾽ ἤδη when **that time** comes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 588, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 911, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.66, etc.}}: repeated with emphasis, τότ᾽ ἄρα τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1273 (Lyric poetry); τ. δὴ τ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.47.}}
**3** with the _Article_, ἄνδρες οἱ τ. people **then** living, the men **of that time,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.559, etc.; οἱ τότ᾽ ἐόντες ἀοιδοί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).27(45); ὁ τ. τυραννεύων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20; οἱ τ. ἄνθρωποι [prev. author] 8.8; ἡ τ. ἀρωγή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 73 (_anapaest meter_); τῇ τόθ᾽ ἡμέρᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1134; ὁ τ. κόσμος NT.2Pet.3.6; ἐν τῷ τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.92, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 110d; ἐν τῷ τ. χρόνῳ [prev. work] 111e (χρόνῳ omitting codex A), “Plt.” 270e; εἰς τὸν τ. χρόνον [prev. author] “Lg.” 740c.}}
**4** εἰς τ. with _future_, **on the day, then** (see. εἰς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|11.2), ἔμπροσθεν τοῦ ἀγῶνος ἐμανθάνομεν.. ἂν μάχεσθαι.., μιμούμενοι πάντα ἐκεῖνα ὁπόσοις ἐμέλλομεν εἰς τ. χρήσεσθαι [prev. work] 830b, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.24, etc.}}; ἐκ τ. or ἔκτοτε (which see) from **that time,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 48, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.26.4; so ἀπὸ τ. LXX.Psa.92(93).2, NT.Matt.4.17.}}
**5** in apodosi, answering to ὅτε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 778, etc.}}; to ὅταν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 971, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1116 (troch.)}}; to ὁππότε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.244, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.257}}; to ὁππότε κεν or ὁπότ᾽ ἂν δή, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.702, 21.341; to ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δή, [prev. work] 451; to εἰ, 4.36; to ἐπεί κε, 11.192}}; to ἡνίκα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 773: also after a}}_participle_, like {εἶτα}, πάντα ἐάσαντες καὶ μόνον οὐχὶ συγκατασκευάσαντες αὐτῷ τ... ζητήσομε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.17, compare 9.73 (interpolated), etc.: frequently joined with other Particles, δὴ τ.}} after η ος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.476; after αὐτὰρ ἐπεί, 12.17}}; after ὁππότε κεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.294}}; also καὶ τ. δή after ἦμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.69, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.59}}; after ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δή, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.209, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.461}}; δή ῥα τ. after εὖτ᾽ ἄν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 565; τότ᾽ ἔπειτα after αὐτὰρ ἐπὴν δή, [prev. work] 616}}; καὶ τότ᾽ ἔπειτα after ἦμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.478.}}
**II** later for ὅτε, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 422, 595.}}

5120:
** ὁ**,
**ἡ**, τό, is, when thus written,
**A** _demonstrative_ Pronoun.
**B ** in _Attic dialect_, definite or prepositive Article.
**C ** in _Epic dialect_, the so-called postpositive Article, = relative Pronoun, ὅς, ἥ, ὅ.—The _nominative_ _masculine_ and _feminine_ _singular_ and _plural_, ὁ, ἡ, οἱ, αἱ, have no accent in codices and most printed books, except when used as the relative; but ὁ, ἡ, οἱ, αἱ differ only in writing from ὃ, ἣ, οἳ, α; the _nominative_ forms of the article are said by Hdn.Gr.1.474 to be oxytone, and by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 8.7 not to be enclitic. The forms τῶν, τοῖς, ταῖς were barytone (i. e. τὼν, τοὶς, ταὶς)}} in _Aeolic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 51.26. For οἱ, αἱ some dialects (not 5th c.AD: Cypria, compare “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.30H., nor Cretan dialect, compare “Leg.Gort.” 5.28, nor Lesbian, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 81, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.1) and 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus have τοί, ταί (though οἱ, αἱ are also found in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus): other Homeric forms are _genitive_ _sing._ τοῖο}} _genitive_ and _dative_ dual τοῖιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.34, +others: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ τάων [ᾱ], _dative_ τοῖσι, τῇς and τῇσι, never ταῖσι or ταῖς in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}— In _Doric dialect_ and all other dialects except _Attic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ the _feminine_ forms preserve the old ᾱ instead of changing it to η, hence _Doric dialect_ etc. ἁ, τάν, τᾶ; the _genitive_ _plural_ τάων contracts in many dialects to τᾶ; the _genitive_ _singular_ is in many places τῶ, _accusative_ _plural_ τώς, but Cretan dialect, etc., τόνς {{Refs|(“Leg.Gort.” 7.7, +others) or τός ([prev. work]3.50, +others)}}; in Lesbian _Aeolic dialect_ the _accusative_ _plural_ forms are τοὶς, ταὶς, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).645 A 13, B 62}}; _dative_ _plural_ τοῖς, ταῖς (or τοὶς, ταὶς, see above), {{Refs|[prev. work]645 A 8, [prev. work]1.6}}; ταῖσι as _demonstrative_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 16. The _Attic dialect_}} Poets also used the _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ forms τοῖσι, ταῖσ; and in Trag. we find τοὶ μέν.., τοὶ δέ.., for οἱ μέν.., οἱ δέ.., not only in Lyric poetry, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 584, “Th.” 295, 298; οἱ μέν.. τοὶ δ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1404 (_anapaest meter_)}}; but even in a trimeter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 424. In _Attic dialect_ the dual has usually only one gender, τὼ θεώ (for τὰ θεά) 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.113}} {ὅ}; τὼ πόλεε Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23; τὼ ἡμέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11; τὼ χεῖρε [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.18; τοῖν χεροῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155e; τοῖν γενεσέοιν [prev. author] “Phd.” 71e; τοῖν πολέοιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.75 (τά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 769, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 424, 484, ταῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.17, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.16, etc. have been corrected)}}; in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus the form τοῖς}} functions as _genitive_ dual _feminine_, μεσακόθεν τοῖς κράναιυν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 664.8 (Orchom., 4th c.BC):}} —in Elean and _Boeotian dialect_ ὁ, ἡ (ἁ), τό, with the addition of -ί, ={ὅδε}, ἥδε, τόδε, _nominative_ _plural_ _masculine_ τυΐ **the following** men, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 485.14 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC), +others, compare infr. VIII. 5. (With ὁ, ἁ}} cf. Sanskrit _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ **sa, sā,** Gothic _sa, sō,_ ONorse **sá, sú,** Old Latin _accusative_ **sum, sam** (Enn.): —with τό [from *τόδ] cf. Sanskrit _tat (tad),_ Latin _is-tud,_ Gothic _pata_: —with τοί cf. Sanskrit _te,_ Lithuanian _tĩe,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **pá,** etc.:—with τάων cf. Sanskrit _tāsām,_ Latin _is-tarum_:— the origin of the relative ὅς, ἥ, ὅ (which see) is different.)
**A** ὁ, ἡ, τό, DEMONSTR. PRONOUN, **that,** the oldest and in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} the commonest sense: frequently also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (1.86, 5.35, +others)}}, and sometimes in Trag. (mostly in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1047, etc.; in trimeters, [prev. author] “Th.” 197, “Ag.” 7, “Eu.” 174}}; τῶν γάρ.., τῆς γάρ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 358, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1082}}; seldom in _Attic dialect_ Prose, except in special phrases, see infr. VI, VII):
**A.I** joined with a _substantive_, to call attention to it, ὁ Τυδεΐδης **he**—Tydeus' **famous** son, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.660}}; τὸν Χρύσην **that venerable man** Chryses, I.II: and so with _appellative_, Νέστωρ ὁ γέρων N.—**that**aged man, {{Refs|7.324}}; αἰετοῦ.. τοῦ θηρητῆρος the eagle, **that which is called** hunter, {{Refs|21.252, +others}}; also to define and give emphasis, τιμῆς τῆς Πριάμου for honour, **namely that** of Priam, {{Refs|20.181}}; οἴχετ᾽ ἀνὴρ ὤριστος a man is gone, **and he** the best, {{Refs|11.288, compare 13.433, +others: sometimes with words between the _pronoun_ and Noun, αὐτὰρ ὁ αὖτε Πέλοψ 2.105; τὸν Ἕκτορι μῦθον ἐνίσπες 11.186, compare 703, +others}}:—different from this are cases {{Refs 8th c.BC+|like 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.409 αἴ κέν πως ἐθέλῃσιν ἐπὶ Τρώεσσιν ἀρῆξαι, τοὺς δὲ κατὰ πρύμνας τε καὶ ἀμφ᾽ ἅλα ἔλσαι Ἀχαιούς}} if he would help the Trojans, but drive **those** back to the ships—**I mean** the Achaeans, where Ἀχ. is only added to explain τούς, compare {{Refs|1.472, 4.20, 329, +others}}
**A.II** frequently without a _substantive_, **he, she, it,** ὁ γὰρ ἦλθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.12, +others}}
**A.III** placed after its Noun, before the Relat. Prons., ἐφάμην σὲ περὶ φρένας ἔμμεναι ἄλλων, τῶν ὅσσοι Λυκίην ναιετάουσι far above the rest, **above those to wit** who, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.172}}; οἷ᾽ οὔ πώ τιν᾽ ἀκούομεν οὐδὲ παλαιῶν, τάων αἳ πάρος ἦσαν.. Ἀχαιαί such as we have not heard tell of yet even among the women of old, **those women to wit** who.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.119, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.332; θάλαμον τὸν ἀφίκετο, τόν ποτε τέκτων ξέσσεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.43, compare 1.116, 10.74:}} —for the _Attic dialect_ usage see below
**A.IV** before a Possessive _pronoun_ its _demonstrative_ force is sometimes very manifest, φθίσει σε τὸ σὸν μένος **that** spirit of thine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.407, compare 11.608; but in 15.58, 16.40, and elsewhere it is merely the _Article_}}
**A.V** for cases in which the Homeric usage approaches most nearly to the Attic, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [near the start]}}
**A.VI** ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ.. without a _substantive_, in all cases, genders, and numbers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.: sometimes in Opposition, where ὁ μέν}} properly refers to **the former,** ὁ δέ to **the latter**; more rarely ὁ μέν **the latter,** ὁ δέ **the former,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 359e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.32,34}}: sometimes in Partition, **the one.., the other..,** etc.—The Noun with it is regularly in _genitive_ _plural_, being divided by the ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ.., into parts, ἠΐθεοι καὶ παρθένοι.., τῶν δ᾽ αἱ μὲν λεπτὰς ὀθόνας ἔχον, οἱ δὲ χιτῶνας εἵατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.595; τῶν πόλεων αἱ μὲν τυραννοῦνται, αἱ δὲ δημοκρατοῦνται, αἱ δὲ ἀριστοκρατοῦνται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338d, etc.}}: but frequently the Noun is in the same case, by a kind of apposition, ἴδον υἷε Δάρητος, τὸν μὲν ἀλευάμενον τὸν δὲ κτάμενον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.28, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.73, etc.}}: so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 22, etc.; πηγὴ ἡ μὲν εἰς αὐτὸν ἔδυ, ἡ δὲ ἔξω ἀπορρεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255c}}; if the Noun be collective, it is in the _genitive_ _singular_, ὁ μὲν πεπραμένος ἦν τοῦ σίτου, ὁ δὲ ἔνδον ἀποκείμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.6: sometimes a Noun is added in apposition with ὁ μέν or ὁ δέ, ὁ μὲν οὔτασ᾽ Ἀτύμνιον ὀξέϊ δουρὶ Ἀντίλοχος.., Μάρις δὲ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.317 - 19, compare 116; τοὺς μὲν τὰ δίκαια ποιεῖν ἠνάγκασα, τοὺς πλουσίους, τοὺς δὲ πένητας κτλ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.102, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501a, etc.}}
**A.VI.2** when a _negative_ accompanies ὁ δέ, it follows δέ, e.g. τὰς γοῦν Ἀθήνας οἶδα τὸν δὲ χῶρον οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 24; τὸν φιλόσοφον σοφίας ἐπιθυμητὴν εἶναι, οὐ τῆς μὲν τῆς δ᾽ οὔ, ἀλλὰ πάσης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475b; οὐ πάσας χρὴ τὰς δόξας τιμᾶν, ἀλλὰ τὰς μὲν τὰς δ᾽ οὔ· οὐδὲ πάντων, ἀλλὰ τῶν μὲν τῶν δ᾽ οὔ [prev. author] “Cri.” 47a, etc.}}
**A.VI.3** ὁ μέν τις.., ὁ δέ τις.. is used in Prose, when the Noun to which ὁ refers is left indefinite, ἔλεγον ὁ μέν τις τὴν σοφίαν, ὁ δὲ τὴν καρτερίαν.., ὁ δέ τις καὶ τὸ κάλλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.41; νόμους.. τοὺς μὲν ὀρθῶς τιθέασιν τοὺς δέ τινας οὐκ ὀρθῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 339c, compare “Phlb.” 13c.}}
**A.VI.4** on τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., or τὰ μέν.., τὰ δέ.., {{Refs|see infr. VIII. 4.}}
**A.VI.5** ὁ μέν is frequently used without a corresponding ὁ δέ, οἱ μὲν ἄρ᾽ ἐσκίδναντο.., Μυρμιδόνας δ᾽ οὐκ εἴα ἀποσκίδνασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.3, compare 24.722, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.12, etc.: also followed by ἀλλά, ἡ μὲν γάρ μ᾽ ἐκέλευε.., ἀλλ᾽ ἐγὼ οὐκ ἔθελον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.304}}; by ἄλλος δέ, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.147, etc.; τὸν μὲν.., ἕτερον δέ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 843, etc.; ὁ μέν.., ὃς δέ.. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 205 (variant{οὐδέ}): less frequently ὁ δέ in the latter clause without ὁ μέν preceding, τῇ ῥα παραδραμέτην φεύγων, ὁ δ᾽ ὄπισθε διώκων (for ὁ μὲν φεύγων) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.157; σφραγῖδε.. χρυσοῦν ἔχουσα τὸν δακτύλιον, ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα ἀργυροῦν “IG” 22.1388.45, compare μέν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator III; γεωργὸς μὲν εἷς, ὁ δὲ οἰκοδόμος, ἄλλος δέ τις ὑφαντής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d, compare “Tht.” 181d.}}]
**A.VI.6** ὁ δέ following μέν sometimes refers to the subject of the preceding clause, τοῦ μὲν ἅμαρθ᾽, ὁ δὲ Λεῦκον.. βεβλήκει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.491; τὴν μὲν γενομένην αὐτοῖσι αἰτίην οὐ μάλα ἐξέφαινε, ὁ δὲ ἔλεγέ σφι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.3, compare 1.66, 6.9, 133, 7.6}}: rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose, ἐπεψήφιζεν αὐτὸς ἔφορος ὤν· ὁ δὲ οὐκ ἔφη διαγιγνώσκειν τὴν βοήν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87; ἔμενον ὡς κατέχοντες τὸ ἄκρον· οἱ δ᾽ οὐ κατεῖχον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.6: this is different from ὁ δέ in apodosi, see below 7; also from passages in which both clauses have a common verb, see at {ὅ γε} 11.}}]
**A.VI.7** ὁ δέ is frequently used simply in continuing a narrative, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.43, etc.}}; also used by [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in apodosi after a _relative_, see at {ὅδε} III.3.}}]
**A.VI.8** the opposition may be expressed otherwise than by μέν and δέ, οὔθ᾽ ὁ.. οὔθ᾽ ὁ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.417; ἢ τοῖσιν ἢ τοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 439; οὔτε τοῖς οὔτε τοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701e.}}
**A.VII** the following usages prevailed in _Attic dialect_ Prose,
**A.VII.1** in dialogue, after καί, it was usual to say in _nominative_ _singular_ _masculine_ καὶ ὅ; in the other cases the usual forms of the _Article_ were used (see. ὅς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} II.I and cf. Sanskrit _sas,_ alternatative form of **sa**); so, in _accusative_, καὶ τὸν εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.9, etc.; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, καὶ τὴν φράσαι 6.61, +others}}
**A.VII.2** ὁ καὶ ὁ **such and such,** τῇ καὶ τῇ ἀτιμίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 721b}}: but mostly in _accusative_, καί μοι κάλει τὸν καὶ τόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784d; τὰ καὶ τὰ πεπονθώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.141, compare 9.68; τὸ καὶ τό [prev. author] 18.243}}; ἀνάγκη ἄρα τὸ καὶ τό it must then be **so and so,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1401a4, compare 1413a22}}; but τὰ καὶ τά **now one thing, now another,** of good and bad, τὸν δ᾽ ἀγαθὸν τολμᾶν χρὴ τά τε καὶ τὰ φέρειν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 398, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.55, 7.20, +others; τῶν τε καὶ τῶν καιρόν [prev. author] “O.” 2.53}}; so πάντα τοῦ μετρίου μεταβαλλόμενα ἐπὶ τὰ καὶ ἐπὶ τά, of excess and defect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 46; compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VI.8.}}
**A.VIII** _absolutely_ usages of single cases,
**A.VIII.1** _feminine_ _dative_ τῇ, of Place, **there, on that spot, here, this way, that way,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.752, 858, +others: followed by ᾗ, 13.52}}, etc.: also in Prose, τὸ μὲν τῇ, τὸ δὲ τῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.12.}}
**A.VIII.1.b** with a notion of motion towards, **that way, in that direction,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.531, 11.149, 12.124; τῇ ἴμεν ᾗ.. 15.46; δελφῖνες τῇ καὶ τῇ ἐθύνεον ἰχθυάοντες 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 210:}} —only _poetry_
**A.VIII.1.c** of Manner, τῇ περ τελευτήσεσθαι ἔμελλεν **in this way, thus,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.510.}}
**A.VIII.1.d** repeated, τῇ μέν.., τῇ δέ.., **in one way.., in another..,** or **partly.., partly..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 356, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211a, etc.: without μέν, τῇ μᾶλλον, τῇ δ᾽ ἧσσον 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.48.}}
**A.VIII.1.e** _relative_, **where, by which way,** only _Epic dialect_, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.118, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.229.}}
**A.VIII.2** _neuter_ _dative_ τῷ, **therefore, on this account,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.418, 2.254, +others (see. below): also in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 239, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 510 (Lyric poetry); in Prose, τῷ τοι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179d, “Sph.” 230b.}}
**A.VIII.2.b** **thus, so,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.373, 13.57, etc.: it may also, especially when εἰ}} precedes, be translated, **then, if this be so, on this condition,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.239, 3.224, 258, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.11.—In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus the true form is probably τῶ, as in codex A, or τώ,compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 199.2.}}
**A.VIII.3** _neuter_ _accusative_ τό, **wherefore,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.176, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.332, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 142 (Lyric poetry)}}; also τὸ δέ _absolutely_, but **the fact is..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23a, “Men.” 97c, “Phd.” 109d, “Tht.” 157b, “R.” 340d, “Lg.” 967a}}; even when the τό refers to what precedes, the contrast may lie not in the thing referred to, but in another part of the sentence (compare above{{Refs 5th c.BC+|VI. 6), τὸ δ᾽ ἐπὶ κακουργίᾳ.. ἐπετήδευσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37; τὸ δὲ.. ἡμῖν μᾶλλον περιέσται [prev. author] 2.89}}; φασὶ δέ τινες αὐτὸν καὶ τῶν ἑπτὰ σοφῶν γεγονέναι· τὸ δὲ οὐκ ἦν but he was not, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 58J.}}
**A.VIII.4** τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., **partly.., partly..,** or **on the one hand.., on the other..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36, etc., compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46}}; more frequently τὰ μέν.., τὰ δέ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 534, etc.; also τὰ μέν τι.., τὰ δέ τι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.14; τὸ μέν τι.., τὸ δέ τι.. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 14; τὰ μέν.., τὸ δὲ πλέον.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90: sometimes without τὸ μέν..}} in the first clause, τὸ δέ τι {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.107, 7.48: rarely of Time, τὰ μὲν πολλὰ.., τέλος δέ}} several **times.. ** and finally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85.}}
**A.VIII.5** of Time, sometimes **that** time, sometimes **this** (present) time, συνμαχία κ᾽ ἔα ἑκατὸν ϝέτεα, ἄρχοι δέ κα τοΐ (where it is possible, but not necessary, to supply ϝέτος) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9.3 (Olympia, 6th c.BC): so with Preps., ἐκ τοῦ, _Epic dialect_ τοῖο}} from **that** time, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.493, 15.601.}}
**A.VIII.5.b** πρὸ τοῦ, sometimes written προτοῦ, **before this, aforetime,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103, 122, 5.55, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1204, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 5, etc.; ἐν τῷ πρὸ τοῦ χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 462; τὸ πρὸ τοῦ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.59.}}
**A.VIII.5.c** in Thess. Prose, ὑππρὸ τᾶς **yesterday,** τὰ ψαφίσματα τό τε ὑππρὸ τᾶς γενόμενον καὶ τὸ τᾶμον the decree which was passed **yesterday** (literal before **this** [day]), and to-day's, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.43 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC).}}
**A.VIII.6** ἐν τοῖς is frequently used in Prose with Superlatives, ἐν τοῖσι θειότατον a **most** marvellous thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.137}}; ἐν τοῖς πρῶτοι the **very** first, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, etc.}}; ἐν τοῖσι πρῶτος (πρώτοις codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.4}}; [Ζεὺς] Ἔρωτά τε καὶ Ἀνάγκην ἐν τοῖς πρῶτα ἐγέννησεν first **of all,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 43(1).16, compare 37(2).2: when used with _feminine_ Nouns, ἐν τοῖς remained without change of gender, ἐν τοῖς πλεῖσται δὴ νῆες}} the greatest number of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.17; ἐν τοῖς πρώτη ἐγένετο (i.e. ἡ στάσις) [prev. work] 82}}: also with _adverbs_, ἐν τοῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.74e, 421d, 723e, “Brut.” 6, 11, +others, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.16.3, etc.; ἐν τοῖς χαλεπώτατα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; τὴν Αἴγυπτον ἐν τοῖς μάλιστα μελάγγειον οὖσαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.364c}}: in late Prose, also with Positives, ἐν τοῖς παράδοξον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 48(24).47 codices; with πάνυ, ἐν τοῖς πάνυ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.19, compare 66 (ἐν ταῖς πάνυ _falsa lectio_ 4.14, 15).}}
**B** ὁ, ἡ, τό, THE DEFINITE ARTICLE, **the,** to specify individuals: rare in this _signification_ in the earliest Gr., becoming commoner later. In {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} the _demonstrative_ force can generally be traced, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|see above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} I, but the definite _Article_ must be recognized in places {{Refs 8th c.BC+|like 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.167, 7.412, 9.309, 12.289, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.372}}: also when joined to an _adjective_ to make it a _substantive_, αἰὲν ἀποκτείνων τὸν ὀπίστατον **the hindmost man,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.178; τὸν ἄριστον 17.80; τὸν δύστηνον 22.59; τὸν προὔχοντα 23.325; τῷ πρώτῳ.., τῷ δευτέρῳ.., etc., [prev. work] 265s q}}.; also in τῶν ἄλλων {{Refs|2.674, +others: with Advs., τὸ πρίν 24.543, +others; τὸ πάρος περ 17.720; τὸ πρόσθεν 23.583}}; also τὸ τρίτον{{Refs|[prev. work] 733; τὰ πρῶτα 1.6, +others}}; τὸ μὲν ἄλλο for **the** rest,{{Refs|23.454; ἀνδρῶν τῶν τότε 9.559.}}—The true _Article_, however, is first fully established in fifth-century. _Attic dialect_, whilst the _demonstrative_ usage disappears, except in a few cases, V. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VI-VIII.}} —Chief usages, especially in _Attic dialect_
**B.I** not only with common Appellats., _adjectives_, and Parts., to specify them as present to sense or mind, but also frequently where we use the Possessive _pronoun_, τὸ κέαρ ηὐφράνθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 5}}; τὴν κεφαλὴν κατεάγην **my** head was broken, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61, etc.}}; τοὺς φίλους ποιούμεθα we make **our** friends, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 190}}; τὰς πόλεις ἔκτιζον they began founding **their** cities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12; οὐχ ὑπὲρ τὴν οὐσίαν ποιούμενοι τοὺς παῖδας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b.}}
**B.I.b** omitted with _proper nouns_and frequently with Appellats. which require no specification, as θεός, βασιλεύς, see at {θεός} {{Refs|I.1, βασιλεύς}} III; ἐμ πόλει in **the** Acropolis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.4.1, +others: but added to proper nouns, when attention is to be called to the previous mention of the person, as 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus (3.70) speaks first of Πειθίας and then refers to him repeatedly as ὁ Π.}}; compare Θράσυλος in {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.104, with ὁ Θ. [prev. work] 105}}; or when the person spoken of is to be specially distinguished, Ζεύς, ὅστις ὁ Ζεύς whoever **this** Zeus is, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 480}}; and therefore properly omitted when a special designation follows, as Σωκράτης ὁ φιλόσοφος: seldom in Trag. with _proper nouns_, save to give peculiar emphasis, like Latin _ille,_ ὁ Λάϊος, ὁ Φοῖβος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 729, “El.” 35, etc.: later, however, the usage became very common (the Homeric usage of ὁ with a _proper name_ is different, see A.I).}}
**B.I.c** Aristotle says Σωκράτης meaning the historical Socrates, as in {{Refs|“SE” 183b7, “PA” 642a28, +others, but ὁ Σωκράτης}} when he means the Platonic Socrates, as {{Refs|“Pol.” 1261a6, +others: so with other proper nouns, “EN” 1145a21, 1146a21, +others}}
**B.I.d** for Σαῦλος ὁ καὶ Παῦλος, etc., see at {καί} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.}}
**B.I.2** in a generic sense, where the individual is treated as a type, οἷς ὁ γέρων μετέῃσιν.. λεύσσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.109; πονηρὸν ὁ συκοφάντης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.242, etc.}}
**B.I.2.b** frequently with abstract Nouns, ἥ τε ἐλπὶς καὶ ὁ ἔρως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, etc.}}
**B.I.3** of outstanding members of a class, ὁ γεωγράφος, ὁ κωμικός, ὁ ποιητής, ὁ τεχνικός, see at {γεωγράφος}, κωμικός, ποιητής, τεχνικός.
**B.I.4** with _infinitives_, which thereby become _Substantives_, τὸ εἴργειν prevention, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 505b}}; τὸ φρονεῖν good sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1348 (_anapaest meter_), etc.: when the subject is expressed it is put between the _Article_ and the}}_infinitive_, τὸ θεοὺς εἶναι **the** existence of gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62b}}; τὸ μηδένα εἶναι ὄλβιον **the fact** or **statement** that no one is happy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86.}}
**B.I.5** in _neuter_ before any word or expression which itself is made the object of thought, τὸ ἄνθρωπος **the word** or **notion** man; τὸ λέγω **the word** λέγ; τὸ μηδὲν ἄγαν **the sentiment** 'ne quid nimis', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 265 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ τῇ αὐτῇ **the phrase** τῇ αὐτῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 72e: and so before whole clauses, ἡ δόξα.. περὶ τοῦ οὕστινας δεῖ ἄρχειν}} the opinion about **the question** 'who ought to rule', {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 431e}}; τὸ ἐὰν μένητε παρ᾽ ἐμοί, ἀποδώσω **the phrase** 'I will give back, if.. ', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c, etc.; τοὺς τοῦ τί πρακτέον λογισμούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.148}}; τὸ ὀλίγοι **the term** few, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283b11.}}
**B.I.6** before _relative_ clauses, when the _Article_ serves to combine the whole _relative_ clause into one notion, τῇ ᾗ φὴς σὺ σκληρότητι **the** harshness you speak of, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 435a}}; τὸν ἥμερον καρπόν.., καὶ τὸν ὅσος ξύλινος (i.e. καὶ τὸν καρπὸν ὅσος ἂν ᾖ ξύλινος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Criti.” 115b; τῶν ὅσοι ἂν.. ἀγαθοὶ κριθῶσιν [prev. author] “R.” 469b; ἐκ γῆς καὶ πυρὸς μείξαντες καὶ τῶν ὅσα πυρὶ καὶ γῇ κεράννυται [prev. author] “Prt.” 320d, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 2; ταύτην τε τὴν αἰτίαν καὶ τὴν ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987a8; τὸν ὃς ἔφη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.8: hence the _relative_, by attraction, frequently follows the case of the _Article_, τοῖς οἵοις ἡμῖν τε καὶ ὑμῖν, i.e. τοῖς οὖσιν οἷοι ἡμεῖς καὶ ὑμεῖς, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.25, etc.}}
**B.I.7** before Prons.,
**B.I.7.a** before the _person_ Prons., giving them greater emphasis, but only in _accusative_, τὸν ἐμέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Phlb.” 20b}}; τὸν.. σὲ καὶ ἐμέ[{{Refs|[prev. work] 59b; τὸν αὑτόν [prev. author] “Phdr.” 258a; on ὁ αὐτός, see at {αὐτός} 111.}}]
**B.I.7.b** before the _interrogative_ _pronoun_ (both τίς and ποῖος), referring to something before, which needs to be more distinctly specified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 251, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 696; also τὰ τ; because οἷα went before, [prev. work] 693. Of τίς only the _neuter_ is thus used (see.above): ποῖος is thus used not only in _neuter_ _plural_, τὰ ποῖ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 707; but also in the other genders, ὁ ποῖο; [prev. work] 1704}}; τῆς ποίας μερίδο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.64}}; τοῖς ποίοις..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 227b1.}}
**B.I.7.c** with τοιοῦτος, τοιόσδε, τηλικοῦτος, etc., the _Article_ either makes the _pronoun_ into a _substantive_, ὁ τοιοῦτος **that sort of person,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.21, etc.}}; or subjoins it to a _substantive_ which already has an _Article_, τὴν ἀπολογίαν τὴν τοιαύτην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.13.}}
**B.I.8** before ἅπας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.69, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64, 7.153 (assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1224 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231, etc.}}; also τὸν ἕνα, τὸν ἕνα τοῦτον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b8, 1288a19: on its usage with ἕκαστος}} see entry; and on οἱ ἄλλοι, οἱ πολλοί, etc., see at {ἄλλος} {{Refs|11.6, πολύς 11.3, etc.}}
**B.I.9** the _Article_ with the _comparative_ is rare, if ἤ follows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 313, “OC” 796.}}
**B.II** elliptic expressions:
**B.II.1** before the _genitive_ of a _proper name_, to express descent, **son** or **daughter,** Θουκυδίδης ὁ Ὀλόρου (i.e. υἱός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104}}; Ἑλένη ἡ τοῦ Διός (i.e. θυγάτηρ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 470}}: also to denote other relationships, e.g. **brother,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.24, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.2.10}}; ἡ Σμικυθίωνος Μελιστίχη M.**the wife** of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 46}}; Κλέαρχος καὶ οἱ ἐκείνου Cl. and his **men,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.15}}; ὁ τοῦ Ἀντιγένεος **the slave** of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ χυμῶν” 20.}}
**B.II.2** generally, before a _genitive_ it indicates a wider relation, as τὸ τῶν νεῶν, τὸ τῶν Ἑρμῶν, **the matter of** the ships, **the affair of** the Hermae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.23, 6.60}}; τὰ τοῦ Ἀρριβαίου πράσσειν to promote **the interests** of Arrhibaeus, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.83, compare 6.89, etc.}}; τὸ τῆς τύχης,=ἡ τύχη, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.18}}; τὰ τῆς τύχης **accidents, chance events,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 55}}; τὰ γὰρ φθιτῶν τοῖς ὁρῶσι κόσμος performance of **the rites due to** the dead befits the living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 78 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ τῶν θεῶν **that which is destined** by the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 498 (Lyric poetry): hence with _neuter_ of Possessive _pronoun_, τὸ ἐμόν, τὸ σόν}} **what regards** me or thee, my or thy business or interests, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 124, “El.” 251, etc.}}: and with _genitive_ of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3 _person_, τὸ τῆσδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 48. But τό τινος}} is frequently also, **a man's word** or **saying,** as τὸ τοῦ Σόλωνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}; τὸ τοῦ Ὁμήρου **as** Homer says, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 183e}}; also τά τινος so-and-so's **house,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1432, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.7, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.76, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.52, NT.Luke.2.49.}}
**B.II.3** very frequently with cases governed by Preps.. αἱ ἐκ τῆς Ζακύνθου νῆες **the** ships from Zacynthus, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.13; οἱ ἀμφί τινα, οἱ περί τινα, such an one and his followers, see at {ἀμφί} with 1.3, περί with 1.2}}]; also τὰ ἐπὶ Θρᾴκης **the** Thrace-ward **district,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.59, +others}}; τὰ ἀπὸ τοῦ καταστρώματος **matters** on deck, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.70}}; τὰ ἀπ᾽ Ἀλκιβιάδου **the proposals** of Alcibiades, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.48}}; τὰ ἀπὸ τῆς τύχης **the incidents** of fortune, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.87, etc.}}
**B.II.4** on μὰ τόν, μὰ τήν, etc., see at {μά} IV.
**B.II.5** in elliptical phrases, ἐπορευόμην τὴν ἔξω τείχους (i.e. ὁδόν) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 203a; ἡ ἐπὶ θανάτῳ (i.e. στολή, δέσις), see at {θάνατος}; κατὰ τὴν ἐμήν (i.e. γνώμην), see at {ἐμός} II.4}}]; ἡ αὔριον (i.e. ἡμέρα), see at {αὔριον}; ἡ Λυδιστί (i.e. ἁρμονία) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342b32, etc.: frequently with Advs., which thus take an adjective sense, as ὁ, ἡ, τὸ νῦν}}; ὁ οἴκαδε πλοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.52; οἱ τότε, οἱ ἔπειτα (i.e. ἄνθρωποι), [prev. work] 9, 10}}, etc.; but τό stands _absolutely_ with Advs. of time and place, when one cannot (as in the preceding instances) supply a _substantive_, as κἀκεῖσε καὶ τὸ δεῦρο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 266, compare [315] (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ μὲν τὸ κεῖθεν, ὁ δὲ τὸ κεῖθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 1412 (Lyric poetry): rarely _absolutely_ in _genitive_, ἰέναι τοῦ πρόσω to go forward, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.1; τοῦ προσωτάτω δραμεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 731.}}
**C** as RELATIVE PRONOUN in many dialects; both in _nominative_ _singular_ _masculine_ ὅ, as κλῦθί μοι, ὃ χθιζὸς θεὸς ἤλυθες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.262, compare 1.300, +others; Ἔρως, ὃ κατ᾽ ὀμμάτων στάζεις πόθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 526 (Lyric poetry); Ἄδωνις, ὃ κἠν Ἀχέροντι φιλεῖται 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.86}}; ὃ ἐξορύξη **he who** banishes him, {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 679.12,25 (from Cyprus)}}; and in the forms beginning with τ, especially in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.160, +others), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, +others}}: also in _Ionic dialect_ Poets, ἐν τῷ κάθημαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 87.3, compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.3, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 86 (probably), 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.64, +others: frequently in Trag., τῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1258, “Tr.” 381, 728, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 883 (_anapaest meter_); τῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 14; τήν [prev. author] “OC” 747, “Tr.” 47, “El.” 1144}}; τό {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1427}}; τῶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 1379, “Ant.” 1086.}}—Never in Comedy texts or _Attic dialect_ Prose:—_Epic dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ τεῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.192 (assuming variant).}}
**D** CRASIS OF ARTICLE:
**D.a** _Attic dialect_ ὁ, ἡ, τό, with ᾰ make ᾱ, as ἁνήρ, ἁλήθεια, τἀγαθόν, τᾄτιο; so οἱ, αἱ, τά, as ἅνδρες, τἀγαθ; also τοῦ, τῷ, as τἀγαθοῦ, τἀγαθῷ: ὁ, τό, οἱ, before e gives ου, οὑξ, οὑπί, οὑμός, τοὔργον, οὑπιχώριοι, etc.; also τοῦ, as τοὐμοῦ, τοὐπιόντο; but ἅτερος, θάτερον ([musical notation]), _Ionic dialect_ οὕτερος, τοὔτερον (see. ἕτερος), _Attic dialect_ _feminine_ ἡτέρα, _dative_ θητέρᾳ (see. ἕτερος); τῷ loses the iota, τὠμῷ, τὠπιόντι: ὁ, τό, before ο gives ου, as Οὁδυσσεύς, Οὑλύμπιος, τοὔνομα: ὁ, τό, etc., before αυ gives ᾱυ, αὑτός, ταὐτό, ταὐτῷ (frequently written ἁτός, etc. in Inscrr. and Papyrus); so τὰ αὐτά=ταὐτά, αἱ αὐταί=αὑταί: ἡ before εὐ gives ηὑ, as ηὑλάβεια: τῇ before ἡ gives θη, as θἠμέρᾳ: τὸ before ὑ gives θου, as θοὔδωρ for τὸ ὕδωρ.
**D.b** other dialects: in their treatment of crasis these follow the local laws of contraction, hence, e.g., _Doric dialect_ ὡξ from ὁ ἐξ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.65, ὥλαφος from ὁ ἔλαφος [prev. work] 135}}; _Ionic dialect_ ᾡσυμνήτης from ὁ αἰς-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.45 (Milet., 5th c.BC)}}; ὡυτή from ἡ αὐτή {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 60, etc.}}

5121:
Included with: ** ἐναντίος**, α, ον,
**opposite**, ={ἀντίος} (which is rare in Prose):
**1** of Place, **on the opposite side, opposite**, c._dative_, ἀκταὶ ἐναντίαι ἀλλήλῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.89; Πάτροκλος δέ οἱ.. ἐ. ἧστο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.190, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.89}}: hence, **fronting, face to face**, αὐτῳ οὔ πω φαίνετ᾽ ἐναντίη {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6.329; ὄττις ἐ. τοι ἰσδάνει 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.2}}; δεῖξον.. τὸ σὸν πρόσωπον δεῦρ᾽ ἐ. πατρί **before** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 947}}; τὰναντία τινί **things open to** one's **sight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.345: _absolutely_, ἐ. στάνθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1078 (but ἐ. κεῖσθαι}} look **opposite** ways, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190a).}}
**1.b** with Verbs of motion, **in the opposite direction**, ἔνθα οἱ.. ἐναντίη ἤλυθε μήτηρ came **to meet** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.251; ἐναντίοι ἀλλήλοισιν ὄγμον ἐλαύνωσιν 11.67; δύο ἅμαξαι ἐ. ἀλλήλαις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93; ἄνεμος ἐ. ἔπνει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.3.}}
**1.c** Astrology texts, **in diametrical aspect**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 70.16, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.360.}}
**2** in hostile sense, **opposing, facing** in fight, with _genitive_, ἐναντίοι ἔσταν Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.497, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1284, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.28, etc.: with _dative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.12, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 856, “IT” 1415}}; οἱ ἐ. **one's adversaries**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 375, 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 12 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.}}; **the enemy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.64, etc.}}
**2.b** generally, **opposed to**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.10}}; τὸ ἐ. **the opposite party**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ath.” 1.4}}; **presenting obstacles, hindering**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 642.}}
**2.c** ὁ δι ἐναντίας the **opponent** in a alawsuit, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 1.58.15 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** of qualities, acts, etc., **opposite, contrary, reverse**, τἀναντί᾽ εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1373; δίκαια καὶ τἀναντία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 667: mostly with _genitive_, τὰ ἐ. τούτων}} the **very reverse** of these things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, etc.}}; δείξας.. ἄστρων τὴν ἐ. ὁδόν, i.e. τὴν τοῦ ἡλίου ὁδὸν ἐ. οὖσαν τοῖς ἄστροις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 861: also with _dative_, Ὀρφεῖ δὲ γλῶσσαν τὴν ἐναντίαν ἔχεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1629}}; τἀναντία πρήσσειν [τῇ ὑγιείῃ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 234; δύο τὰ -ώτατα εὐβουλίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42; ἀγαθῷ κακὸν -ώτερον ἢ τῷ μὴ ἀγαθῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491d; ἐναντία λέγει αὐτὸς αὑτῷ [prev. author] “Prt.” 339b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 493; τἀναντία τούτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt” 323d}}; ἐναντία γνῶναι ταῖς πλείσταις [πόλεσιν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.2; τὴν ἐ. τινὶ ψῆφον θέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.65; simply τὴν ἐ. θέσθαι τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 184d: followed by ἤ, τοὺς ἐ. λόγους ἢ ὡς αὐτὸς κατεδόκεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22; τοὐ. δρῶν ἢ προσῆκ᾽ αὐτῷ ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 14; τοὐ. ἔπαθεν ἢ τὸ προσδοκώμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966e, compare “R.” 567c, etc.: frequently strengthened, πᾶν τοὐ., πάντα τἀ}}., quite **the contrary, Lg.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|967a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.4}}; πολὺ τοὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 57; τὸ δὲ πολὺ ἐναντίον ἀποβήσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39c.}}
**3.b** τὰ ἐ. **opposites** in Philos., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986b3, etc.}}
**4** in the Philos. of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τἀναντία (distinct from other ἀντικείμενα, “Metaphorical” 1018a25)}} are **contraries**, especially the two attributes within the same genus which differ most widely from each other (as hot and cold), {{Refs|“Cat.” 6a18, +others}}
**4.b** ἐ. ἀποφάνσεις, προτάσεις, **contrary** propositions (All {{Refs|B is A, No B is A)}}, opposed to **contradictory** (see. ἀντιφατικῶς), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Int.” 17b4, “APr.” 63b28.}}
**II** frequently in _adverb_ usages:
**II.1** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards, _neuter_ ἐναντίον}} as _adverb_, **opposite, facing**, ἐ. ὧδε κάλεσσον here **to my face**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.544}}; εἰς ὦπα ἰδέσθαι ἐ. to look one **in the face**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.107; ἐ. προσβλέπειν τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 968, etc.; γυναῖκας ἀνδρῶν μὴ βλέπειν ἐ. [prev. work] 975: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40, etc.}}: hence, like a _preposition_ with _genitive_, **in the presence of**, τῆς βουλῆς {{Refs|“IG” 12.91}}; τῶνδ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1002}}; μαρτύρων ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 448; ἐ. τοῦ παιδίου [prev. author] “Lys.” 907; ἐ. ἁπάντων λέγειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.25; ἐ. Διός 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.9.2; also _neuter_ _plural_, “IG” 7.1779 (from Thespiae).}}
**II.1.b** in hostile sense, **against**, with _genitive_, ἀνέσταν.. σφοῦ πατρὸς ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.534; ἐ. ἰέναι τινός 21.574; ἐ. μάχεσθαί τινος 20.97; ἐ. ἵστασ᾽ ἐμεῖο 13.448: _absolutely_, ἐ. μίμνειν}} stand one's ground **against**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 106: with _dative_, νεικεῖν ἀλλήλοισιν ἐ. 20.252; ἐ. θεοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 624; ἐ. τῷ ὅρκῳ πράττειν “IG” 22.1258.2.}}
**II.1.c** **contrariwise**, in _Attic dialect_ also with the _Article_, τοὐναντίον **on the other hand**, τοὐ. δέ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 80.4}}; ἢ πάλιν τοὐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 460.5}}; **conversely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e.}}
**II.1.d** _neuter_ _plural_ ἐναντία as _adverb_, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.32, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, etc.}}
**II.2** with Preps., ἐκ τοῦ ἐ. **over against, opposite**, opposed to ἐκ πλαγίου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.15, etc.}}; ἐξ ἐναντίας, _Ionic dialect_ -ίης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.33 (οἱ ἐξ ἐ}}. the **opposing parties**, probably in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.78.26 (4th c.AD))}}; ἐκ τῶν ἐ. **on the contrary**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.9; ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίας 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 1.2; ἀπ᾽ ἐ. Χωρεῖν 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 4}}; κατὰ τὰ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39a: Geometry texts, αἱ κατ᾽ ἐναντίον τοῦ παραλληλογράμμου πλευραί}} the **opposite** sides of the parallelogram, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.9}}; αἱ κατ᾽ ἐ. τομαί **opposite** sections (i. e. branches) of the hyperbola, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 3.23.}}
**II.3** regular _adverb_ -ίως **contrariwise**, with _dative_, τούτοις οὐκ ἐ. λέγεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 642; ἐ. διακεῖσθαί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c; ἐ. ἀντικεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 17b20}}; πικρῶς καὶ ἐ. **like an enemy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.339; ἐ. ἢ ὡς ἀνδραπόδοις τραφεῖσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175d}}; ἐ. ἔχειν to be **exactly opposed**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 278a}}; πρός τι to be **contrary** in respect of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.4}}; in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “Metaphorical” 1057b11, +others, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem” p.268C.}}

5122:
Included with: ** ὀνομάζω**,
_imperfect_ ὠνόμαζον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 682 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; _Epic dialect_ ὀν- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.361, +others}}: _future_ ὀνομάσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 423d}}: _aorist_ ὠνόμασα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.339, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὠνόμακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 219b}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ασθήσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.16, +others:}}_aorist_ ὠνομάσθην and _perfect_ ὠνόμασμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.96, 6.96, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ὀνόμασται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 9.1, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὠνομάδαται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.16}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ ὠνομάζετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1021.}} —_Aeolic dialect_ or _Doric dialect_ _future_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὀνυμάξει (or -εῖ) {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}: _aorist_ ὀνύμαξε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.44}}; _middle_ _future_ ὀνυμάξομαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 7.5}}: _present_ ὀνυμάζεται{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Metop. cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.116}}: (ὄνομα):—**speak of by name, call** or **address by name,** of persons, πατρόθεν ἐκ γενεῆς ὀνομάζων ἄνδρα ἕκαστον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.68, compare 22.415 and ὀνομακλήδην}}; Πυθοδώρου.., ὃν Ἀθηναῖοι οὐκ ὀνομάζουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.1 (interpolated)}}; τοῖς προγόνοις -αζομένοις ἀπομνημονεύεται ὁπόστος ἀφ᾽ Ἡρακλέους ἐγένετο his descent.. is traced by **naming** his ancestors, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ages.” 1.2.}}
**2** of things, **name, specify,** περικλυτὰ δῶρ᾽ ὀνόμαζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.449}}; but also, **name** or **promise,** opposed to giving, εἰ μὲν.. μὴ δῶρα φέροι, τὰ δ᾽ ὄπισθ᾽ ὀνομάζοι {{Refs|9.515}}; εἶναί τι ὀνομάζειν **use the term** 'being', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 160b, compare 166c, 201d}}; **dedicate,** τράπεζαν τῷ δαίμονι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 121}}:—_passive_, λόγοισι.. ὠνόμασται βραχέσι **have been expressed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 294.}}
**II** ὀ. τινά τι **call** one something, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.44, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 681 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1193}}; ὄνομα τί σε.. ὠνόμαζεν λεώ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 87 (Lyric poetry):}} —rarely in _middle_, παῖδά μ᾽ ὠνομάζετο **called** me **his** son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1021}}:—_passive_, ὄνομα δ᾽ ὠνομάζετο Ἕλενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 605; τὴν αὑτῆς ἐπωνυμίαν ὀνομαζόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a; ἀντὶ γὰρ φίλων καὶ ξένων, ἃ τότ᾽ ὠνομάζοντο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.46.}}
**II.b** **nominate,** ὀνομασθεὶς εἰς δεκαπρωτείαν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1257.1, compare 1204.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** εἶναι is frequently added _pleonastic_, τὰς ὀνομάζουσι εἶναι Ὑπερόχην καὶ.. whose **names they say are** Hyperoche and.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33; σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσιν τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e, compare “R.” 428e (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 13, etc.; compare καλέω 11.3 b.}}
**III** **name** or **call** with reference to, in accordance with, or after.., τινὰ or τι ἐπί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493c}}:—_passive_, ἐπί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.183; ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1036, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.12; ὁ τῆς ἀρίστης μητρὸς ὠνομασμένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1105.}}
**IV** **utter names** or **words,** ἐς τρὶς ὀνομάσαι Σόλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; μάλα σεμνῶς ὀνομάζων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.35, compare 122, 21.158:}} —_passive_, φύσις ἐπὶ τοῖς ὀνομάζεται ἀνθρώποισι **the name** φύσις **is given** by men to those things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 8.4, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 9.1}}; παρανομίαν ἐπὶ τοῖς μὴ ἀνάγκῃ κακοῖς ὀνομασθῆναι **the name** of transgression **is applied..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.98}}; ἀπὸ τούτου τοῦτο ὀνομάζεται (i.e. οὐ φροντὶς Ἱπποκλείδῃ) hence this **saying is used,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.130.}}
**V** **make famous,** in _passive_, οἱ ὠνομασμένοι persons **of renown,** variant for{διωνομασμένοι} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.19.}} —Cf. ὀνομαίνω.

5124:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5125:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5126:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5127:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5128:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5129:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5130:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

5131:
** τράγος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
**he-goat,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.239, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 201}}; opposed to αἴξ (she-goat), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.46, “PCair.Zen.” 328.19 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; τῶν αἰγῶν τῶν τράγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.112}}; τράγος γένειον.. πενθήσεις you will mourn your beard like the **goat** in the proverb, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 207}}; Κιλίκιοι τράγοι, of longhaired men, {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 806}}; of men, τράγου ὄζειν, τράγου πνεῖν, to smell **like a goat,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.368 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator Imp., perhaps with play on signification 111), 11.240 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).152.}}
**2** **the age when change of voice and other signs of puberty appear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.4.21, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 14.7.}}
**2.b** **the change of the voice which takes place at this age,** uncertain in {{Refs|“PLond.” 1821.150}}; compare τραγάω, τραγίζω.
**3** **lewdness, lechery,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ep.Sat.” 28.}}
**II** the male of the fish μαινίς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 607b14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 73, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.108.}}
**III** **spelt,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.93, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.44, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.455, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.68.}}
**IV** a rough kind of **sponge,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 548b5, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.120.}}
**V** among the Messenians, **the wild fig,** ={ἐρινεός}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.20.2, cf. Oracle texts cited in 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 8.21}} (where perhaps = **goat**).
**V.2** ={ἐφέδρα}{{Refs 1st c.AD+|111, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.51, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 13.116, 27.142.}}
**V.3** **stinking nard, Valeriana saxatilis,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.8.}}
**VI** part of the ear (compare ἀντίτραγος), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.85, 86, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 44.}}
**VII** a kind of light Lycian ship, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.83.}}
**VIII** a kind of **comet,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Ostentis” 10b.}}
**VIII.2** a constellation of the δωδεκάωρος,{{Refs|Teucer in “Cat.Codex Astr.” 7.204, 8(4).198, [prev. author] in Boll “Sphaera” 48.}}

5132:
** τράπεζ-α** [τρᾰ], ης, ἡ,
_Doric dialect_ **τράπεσδα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 74b}}:— **table,** especially **dining-table, eating-table,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, Τηλεμάχοιο τ., ἐμὴ τ., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.333, 447, compare “IG” 12.330.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 518.2; τ. παραθεῖναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.139, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 171; παρέκειτο τ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.476}}; τ. εἰσφέρειν, ἐπάγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1216, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 2 (but ἐσῄρετο is probably conjecture)}}; ἡ τ. εἰσῄρετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 518; τ. ἀφαιρεῖν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.61, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.1 (_passive_)}}; αἴρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 273, compare 451; ἐκφέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 69.2}}; ξενίη τ. the hospitable **board,** ἴστω Ζεύς.. ξενίη τε τ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.158, compare 21.28; ᾔσχυνε ξενίαν τ. κλοπαῖσι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 401 (Lyric poetry), compare 701 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅρκον μέγαν, ἅλας τε καὶ τράπεζαν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 96, cf. Wilcken “Chr.” 11.58 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ ξενικὴ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.224; τοὺς τῆς πόλεως ἅλας καὶ τὴν δημοσίαν τ. [prev. author] 2.22}}; δέξασθαι τραπέζῃ καὶ κοίτῃ entertain at bed and **board,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20; κοίτης μεθέξουσα καὶ τραπέζης μόνον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 13; ἐπὶ τὰς αὐτὰς τ. ἰέναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.10}}; τράπεζαν Περσικὴν παρετίθετο he kept **a table** in the Persian fashion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130; τ. κοσμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.6, etc.}}; εἰς ἀλλοτρίαν τ. ἀποβλέπειν live at other men's **table,** at their expense, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.2.33}}; τὴν τ. ἀνατρέπειν upset the **table,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.198}}; proverbial of a spendthrift, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.130}}; **table dedicated to the gods,** on which meats and offerings were set out, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.190.4, 840.19, 22.1245.6, 1534.163, 1933.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.2; τ. ἱερά “PCair.Zen.” 708 (3rd c.BC); ἐπὶ τὴν τ. τῶν Διοσκόρων [prev. work] 569.24 (3rd c.BC)}}; τ. Κυρίου, τ. δαιμονίων, {{NT|NT.1Cor.10.21.}}
**2** **table,** as implying what is upon it, **meal,** ἄνομος τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.162, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.22; also βορᾶς τ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1464}}; Συρακοσίων τ., proverbial of luxurious living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 216, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404d}}; Σικελικαὶ τ. proverbial cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.203a; πολυτελὴς τ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.64U.}}; δεύτεραι τ. the second **course,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.133e, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.639b}}; compare τράγημα.
**II** **money-changer's counter,** ἐν ἀγορᾷ ἐπὶ τῶν τ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.70f; αἱ τ. τῶν κολλυβιστῶν NT.Matt.21.12}}; most frequently **bank,** {{Refs|Lys 9.5}}, etc.; ἡ ἐργασία ἡ τῆς τ. the right to operate the **bank,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.6}}; ἡ ἐγγύη ἡ ἐπὶ τὴν τ. security given to the **bank,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 33.10; δοῦναι ἀργύριον ἐπὶ τ. NT.Luke.19.23; τὸ ἐπὶ τὴν τ. χρέως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.24; οἱ ἐπὶ ταῖς τ.}}**bankers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.2}}; κατασκευάζεσθαι τράπεζαν set up a **bank,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator “fragment” 66}}; τῆς τ. ἀνασκευασθείσης the **bank** having been broken, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.9}}; δημοσία τ. public **bank** at Delos, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 22.2336.180 (1st c.BC)}}; in Egypt, {{Refs|“POxy.” 835 (Aug.), etc.}}; βασιλικὴ τ. in Egypt, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 27.22 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 27.70 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; χειριστὴς τῆς ἐν τῇ Πολέμωνος μερίδι τ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 38.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; opposed to ἰδιωτικὴ τ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 305 (1st c.AD), etc.; κολλυβιστικαὶ τ. [prev. work] 1411.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** **any table** or **flat surface on which a thing rests**: as,
**III.1** **cross bench** in which the mast is fixed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.729}}; τ. δολωνική, see at {δολωνικός}.
**III.2** **platform on which slaves were exposed for sale,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 874.}}
**III.3** **tablet** or **slab** with a relief or inscription, τ. χαλκῆ Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.53, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.31.3}}; at a tomb, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.838c.}}
**III.4** **plinth** of a statue, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4702.7 (Egypt, 4th c.BC).}}
**III.4.b** lamp-**stand**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.428.39 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III.5** **nether millstone**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 251.17 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.19.}}
**III.6** **part of a torsion engine,** probably the **plinth,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 54.2, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 100.1.}}
**III.7** **part of the liver,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 560, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.20, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 180.}}
**III.8** **shoulder-blade,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.177.}}
**III.9** **grinding surface of the teeth,**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 93, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 54}}. (The word is shortened from τετράπεζ; hence the question καὶ πόθεν ἐγὼ τρίπουν τ. λήψομα; as if this were an absurdity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 530; τ. τρισκελεῖς 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 301:}} —so τρίπεζα, τρέπεδδα (which see), of three-legged tables.)

5133:
** τρᾰπεζῑτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
_Doric dialect_ **-τας** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).110.10 (Elatea, 4th c.BC)}}, etc.: (τράπεζα 11):—**money-changer, banker,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 1.1, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.28, 49.5, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 159.11, “PEleph.” 10.2 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; οἱ ἀνεσκευασμένοι τῶν τ. broken **bankers,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.68.}}
**2** **director of a state-bank**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.17 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 112 ii 5 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 12(5).880.11, +others (Tenos, 1st c.BC).}}
**II** τραπεζίτης Πάρις **violator of hospitality,** {{Refs|Trag.Adesp. 270 (assuming variant).}}
**III** τραπεζεῖται κύνες, ={τραπεζῆες} (see. τραπεζεύς), Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|2.356, +others [Hdn.Gr. [prev. cited] says -ειτ- is correct in signification 111, -ῑτ- otherwise; in signification 1 -ῑτ-}} is found in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1) [prev. cited] (4th c.BC), 42(1).98.13 (Epid., 3rd c.BC), “PEleph.” [prev. cited] (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 176.63 (3rd c.BC), -ειτ- [prev. work] 174 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 742.55 (Ephesus, 1st c.BC), etc.: probably only -ῑτ- is correct.]}}

5134:
** τραῦμα**, ατος, τό,
_Ionic dialect_ **τρῶμα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, +others, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 2, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ also **τρῶμα**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.50}}:— **wound, hurt,** ἀποθνῄσκειν ἐκ τῶν τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63; τελευτᾶν ἐκ τοῦ τ. [prev. author] 3.29; τραυμάτων ἐτύγχανεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 866; πολλὰ τραύματ᾽ ἐν στέρνοις λαβών [prev. author] “fragment” 362, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 7; ὑφ᾽ ὧν πολλὰ τραύματ᾽ εἰληφώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.262; πολλὰ τραύματ᾽ ἔχων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.20; τραύματα ὑπὸ τῶν πολεμίων τοσαῦτα ἔχων [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.4.1}}; φέρειν, ποιεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1487 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 19.6; τυπτέσθω ἄνευ τραυμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845c; τὰ ἄνευ τραύματος κατάγματα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.28; αἱ χωρὶς τραυμάτων αἱμορραγίαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.127; ἀδύνατον νεκρῶν τραύματα μύειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 167.}}
**II** of things, **hurt, damage,** as of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.16, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.4.12.}}
**III** in war, **heavy blow, defeat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, 4.160; τὸ ἐν Μαραθῶνι τ. γενόμενον [prev. author] 6.132; τὸ τ. τὸ Αακωνικόν [prev. author] 8.66.}}
**IV** ἡ τοῦ τ. γραφή an indictment **for wounding** (with intent to murder), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.93; τραύματος ἐκ προνοίας γραφὰς γραφόμενος [prev. author] 3.212, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.41; δίκαι τραύματος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 57.3.}}

5136:
** τρᾰχηλ-ίζω**,
_future_ _Attic dialect_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.52 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**bend** or **twist the neck of** a victim, βοῦν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 27.5.}}
**II** in wrestling, '**scrag**' one's opponent, τοὺς νεανίσκους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 33}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Amatores” 132c, 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.50 H., 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 23.291b.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **inflict hardship on** a combatant, τοὺς.. φίλους οἱ λειπόμενοι τραχηλιοῦσι πόλεμοι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.131}}:—_passive_, ἐμφυλίῳ πολέμῳ καὶ διχονοίᾳ -ιζόμενοι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.6.2.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **to be overpowered, swept away,** ταῖς ἐπιθυμίαις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.127}}; of ships in a whirlpool, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.3.}}
**III** in a pun on _significations_ {{Refs|1, 11.1, and 11.3, ἰδὼν Ὀλυμπιονίκην εἰς ἑταίραν πυκνότερον ἀτενίζοντα, ἴδε ἔφη, κριὸν Ἀρειμάνιον ὡς ὑπὸ τοῦ τυχόντος κορασίου -ίζεται}} see how the ram's **neck is being twisted,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.61, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.521b; τοὺς πολυπράγμονας ἴδοις ἂν ὑπὸ παντὸς ὁμοίως θεάματος -ιζομένους καὶ περιαγομένους [prev. passage]}}
**IV** _passive_, **to be laid open,** {{NT|NT.Heb.4.13}}; τετραχηλισμένα· πεφανερωμένα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5137:
** τράχηλος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
_Doric dialect_ **τράχᾱλος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.3, +others (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}: irregular _plural_ τράχηλα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 98 (= “Iamb.” 1.147)}}:—**neck, throat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.35, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 608 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.84, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.151, etc.}}; distinguished from αὐχήν by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253e (τράχηλος}} being, _accusative_ to {{Refs|“Geoponica” 19.2.3}}, **the whole neck and throat,** αὐχήν the back part of the neck in human beings, the upper part in animals; this difference is observed in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 37 (compare αὐχήν in 38, 39, 40, 41), 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus 2.21}}; but αὐχήν in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 23 is glossed τράχηλος by 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).264, compare 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 66, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.130; in LXX}}, **NT,** and Papyrus τ. is more frequently than αὐχήν); τ. σώματος χωρὶς τεμών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 241, compare “Supp.” 716}}; ἀποτεμεῖν, ἀποκόψαι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 29, “Flam.” 18, etc.; βρόχον δ᾽ ἐνίαλλε τραχήλῳ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.51}}; ἐς τ. πεσεῖν break one's **neck,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 755}}; ἐπὶ τ. ὠθεῖν τινα thrust **head-foremost,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 27.1, “Merc.Cond.” 39; εἰς τ. 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.135; ἐπιπεσεῖν ἐπὶ τὸν τ. τινός LXX.Gen.46.29, NT.Luke.15.20}}; ἐν βρόχῳ τὸν τ. ἔχων νομοθετεῖ with a halter round his **neck,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.139}}; ἔδει σε ἐν τῷ σῷ τ. ἐμπαίζειν at the risk of your own **neck,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 758.2 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **neck** of animals, of the horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.8; the hare, [prev. author] “Cyn.” 5.30; the camel, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1125b, “BGU” 469.6 (2nd c.AD)}}; **the neck** as a joint of meat, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 11}}; of a fowl, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.788.}}
**II** of parts **resembling the neck,** e. g. **upper part of the murex,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 66, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 14, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 547a16, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.87f; in the κάραβος}}**the narrow part of the abdomen,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 526a3}}; the **neck** of the grasshopper, {{Refs|[prev. work] 556a2.}}
**II.2** **neck** of a vessel, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BCH” 35.286 (from Delos), 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.19, +others}}; of a gourd, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 616a23}}; of parts of the body, τ. μήτρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.169, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.222; ὑστέρας 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.7}}; κύστεως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. passage], 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 14.9, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.171; καρδίας “Placita Philosophorum” 4.5.8.}}
**II.3** **middle part of a mast,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Asclepiades Myrleanus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.475a.}}

5138:
** τρᾱχ-ύς**, εῖα, ύ:
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **τρηχύς**, _feminine_ -εῖα, -εῖαν, -είης, _neuter_ -ύ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 291, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.74)}}; in _Ionic dialect_ Prose _feminine_ τρηχέᾰ, _accusative_ τρηχέᾰν, _genitive_ τρηχέης, _dative_ τρηχέῃ (imperfectly preserved in codices; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, 9.122, codices ABCP have τρηχέη, -έην, -έης}} RSV have -εῖα, -εῖαν, -είη; for codices {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus see Kühleweinip. lxxxvi)}}; τρηχείην (before _consonant_) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 89 codices, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.375 codices}}; τρηχείης (as _proper name_) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 47}}; _genitive_ _plural_ _neuter_ ἐρίων.. τρηχείων {{Refs|“GDI” 5633.14 (from Teos)}}; dual in Trag. τραχεῖ, Ion Trag.67:—**jagged,** λίθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.308}}; χαλινοί, opposed to λεῖοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.9, compare 10.6; τ. καὶ γωνιοειδής 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 65}}; **prickly,** ἄκανθαι, ἄκανθα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.32e, 138d (both _superlative_)}}; **rugged,** ἀκτή, ἀταρπός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.425, 14.1}}; as epithet of Ithaca, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.27, 10.417; so γῆ λιθώδης καὶ τρηχέα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23; Χερσονήσου τῆς Τρηχέης καλεομένης, of the Crimea, [prev. work] 99}}; and frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ of rocky districts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 726, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1083}}; τὰ τραχέα, τὰ τραχύτατα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 4; τ. καὶ χαλεπὴ ὁδός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328e}}; also, **rough,** γλῶσσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.63}}; ἔρια {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“GDI” [same place], “PCair.Zen.” 287.2 (3rd c.BC); σφόγγοι [prev. work] 12.56 (3rd c.BC); χῆμαι [prev. work] 82.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; σινδόνες (towels, opposed to μαλακαί) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.418; χερσὶ μὴ πάνυ μαλακαῖς, ὥσπερ αὖ μηδὲ τραχείαις, ἀνατρίβειν τὸ σῶμα [prev. work]417; τὰ τ. κατὰ τὰς ἀνωμαλίας ἀλλήλοις ἐμπλεκόμενα ἑνοῦται, τὰ δὲ λεῖα κτλ. Diocl.fragment 26; βλέφαρα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.16, “PTeb.” 273 _intransitive_ (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; **shaggy,** τὰ κάτωθεν τ. καὶ τραγοειδής, of Pan, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 408d, compare 420e; λάσιον καὶ τ. [τὸ κέαρ].. ἔχοντες [prev. author] “Tht.” 194e}}; τ. σώματα, opposed to λεῖα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.1}}; of the voice, **harsh,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67c, etc.}}; especially of the voice of boys, when it breaks, μεταβάλλειν ἐπὶ τὸ -ύτερον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 581a18; τὸ τ. τῆς φωνῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 14}}; and of a person, τῇ φωνῇ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.9}}; also τραχυτάτη γλῶσσα (compare τραχύστομος) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28}}; of sounds and their combinations, **harsh,** opposed to λεῖος, σύνθεσις, διάλεκτος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 994.32,36:}} —on τραχεῖα ἀρτηρία, see at {ἀρτηρία}.
**2** of battle and conflict, ὑσμίνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 119; νιφὰς πολέμοιο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).17(35), compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 89; φάλαγγες 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.22.}}
**3** of natural forces, ῥόθιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1048 (_anapaest meter_); -ύτερα τὰ νοσήματα ἀπεργάζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84c}}; of a river, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 60, etc.; ἄελλαι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1078.}}
**4** of persons, their acts, feelings, or conditions, **rough, harsh, savage,** τ. ἔφεδρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.96}}; οὐ τ. εἰμι καταθέμεν I am not **niggardly** in paying,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.76; Ἡσυχία [prev. author] “P.” 8.10; ἅπας δὲ τ. ὅστις ἂν νέον κρατῇ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 35, compare 188 (_anapaest meter_), 326; δικαστὴς τ. εἶ [prev. author] “Ag.” 1421; τ. γε.. δῆμος [prev. author] “Th.” 1049; τ. καὶ τεθηγμένους λόγους [prev. author] “Pr.” 313; τ. ὀργή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 447; λεῖον καὶ τ. πάθημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 63e; νόμοι τραχύτατοι [prev. author] “Lg.” 864c}}; τὸ τ. τοῦ ἤθους, τοῦ νόμου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 406a, “R.” 452c; -ύτερα πράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.18}}; εὐνομίη τραχέα λειαίνει smooths **the rough places,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.35.}}
**II** _adverb_ τρᾱχέως, _Ionic dialect_ τρηχέως, rare in the literal sense, **roughly,** τ. ὑλακτεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 8}}; _neuter_ as _adverb_, τρηχὺ φωνῇ ἠπείλει {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.74; θάλασσα τραχὺ βοᾷ “Anthologia Graeca” 5.179 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** of men's acts, τρηχέως περιέπειν τινά handle **roughly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, 114}}; τραχέως ἔχειν to be **rough, harshly disposed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.33}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.45; -ύτερον ἄρχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.55; τ. ἀποκρίνεσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 21, etc.}}; τ. φέρειν take **hardly,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 15}}; rarely τραχυτέρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Clitopho” 406a; περιέφθησαν τρηχύτατα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.15. (Prob. _cognate_ with θράσσω, compare ἐνθράσσω.)}}

5141:
** τρέμω**,
only _present_ and _imperfect_, except _perfect_ τετρέμηκα {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 606.50}}:— **tremble, quake, quiver,** τρέμε δ᾽ οὔρεα μακρὰ καὶ ὕλη ποσσὶν ὑπ᾽ ἀθανάτοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.18, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 137}}; ὠλένας τρέμων ἄκρας **quivering in**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 283; τρέμουσα κῶλα [prev. author] “Med.” 1169; τρέμει [ἡ φωνή] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 906a17}}; of persons, τρέμειν τὴν φωνήν{{Refs|[prev. work] 948a35}}; **shiver,** in the cold stage of malaria, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 8.}}
**II** especially **tremble with fear,** ὑπὸ δ᾽ ἔτρεμε γυῖα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.390, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.527}}; φόβῳ, φρίκῃ τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1452 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 1026}}: then simply, **tremble, be afraid,** δεδιὼς καὶ τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.263; τ. τῷ δέει τί πείσεται 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 110.6.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **tremble** or **fear to** do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 419 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 128 (Lyric poetry); also τρέμων τὸν ἄνδρ᾽ ἔφευγε μὴ κτάνοι [prev. author] “OT” 947, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 808, 1057.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_, **tremble at, fear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 256, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 643, etc.; τ. τὸ πρᾶγμα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 494; τὰ πράγματα [prev. author] “Eq.” 265 (troch.); τὸ μέλλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 137a; [τῆς εὐδαιμονίας] ἕνεκα τ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.9; περὶ τῆς εὐδαιμονίας [prev. author] 2.3.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 554d}}. (Cf. Latin _tremo,_ Lithuanian _trimù, triìmti_ 'shiver'.)

5142:
** τρέφω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.52, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ **τράφω** (see. entry); _1st pers._ _singular_ _optative_ τρέφοιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 903}}: _future_ θρέψω {{Refs|“hymnus ad Venerem” 257, etc.:}}_aorist 1_ ἔθρεψα, _Epic dialect_ θρέψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.548:}}_aorist 2_ ἔτρᾰφον (see. infr. B): _perfect_ τέτροφα _intransitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.237, (συν-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 11; but _transitive_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 186 (Lyric poetry); also τέτρᾰφα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.25h. 5:}} —_middle_, _future_ θρέψομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 9, “Nat.Puer.” 23, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.49, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐθρεψάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.46, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 928}}, etc.:—_passive_, _future_ τρᾰφήσομαι Ps.-{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.32, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.41}}, etc., but in early writers in _middle_ form θρέψομαι (see. above): _aorist 1_ ἐθρέφθην, _Epic dialect_ θρέφθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 198}}, rare in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 351, 600, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310a; ἐθράφθη “IG” 12(9).286 (Eretria, 6th c.BC):}}_aorist 2_ ἐτράφην [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (sed see infr. B), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 754 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 335 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔτραφεν, τράφεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.348, 1.251}}: _perfect_ τέθραμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 5, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 578, etc.}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ τέθραφθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 625a (but συντέτραφθε}} [assuming variant] in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.14)}}; _infinitive_ τεθράφθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.24 (in both with see l. τετρ-).}}
**I** **thicken** or **congeal** a liquid, γάλα θρέψαι **curdle** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.246}}; τρέφε (_imperfect_) πίονατυρόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.106:}} —_passive_, with _perfect_ _active_ τέτροφα, **curdle, congeal,** γάλα τρεφόμενον τυρὸν ἐργάζεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.32; περὶ χροΐ τέτροφεν ἅλμη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.237.}}
**II** usually, **cause to grow** or **increase, bring up, rear,** especially of children bred and brought up in a house, ὅ σ᾽ ἔτρεφε τυτθὸν ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.283; ἥ μ᾽ ἔτεχ᾽, ἥ μ᾽ ἔθρεψε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.131, compare 12.134; εὖ ἔτρεφεν ἠδ᾽ ἀτίταλλεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.191, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.354; ἐγώ σ᾽ ἔθρεψα, σὺν δὲ γηράναι θέλω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 908, compare “Supp.” 894; μέχρι ἥβης τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.46; γεννᾶν καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274a; τ. τε καὶ αὔξειν μέγαν [prev. author] “R.” 565c: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τ. τινὰ τροφήν τινα}} **bring up** in a certain way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2; also τῶν πρώτων μαθημάτων, ἐν οἷς οἱ παλαιοὶ τοὺς παῖδας ἔτρεφον 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.691}}:—_middle_, **rear for oneself,** θρέψαιό τε φαίδιμον υἱόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.368; αὐτὸν ἐθρέψαντο δράκοντες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.46; τεκοῦσα τόνδ᾽ ὄφιν ἐθρεψάμην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 928; οἱ γεννήσαντες καὶ θρεψάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717c; τεκὼν ἀρετὴν καὶ θ. [prev. author] “Smp.” 212a; ἔτεκον μὲν ὑμᾶς πολεμίοις δ᾽ ἐθρεψάμην ὕβρισμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 458}}:—_passive_, **to be reared, grow up,** ὅς μοι τηλύγετος τρέφεται θαλίῃ ἔνι πολλῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.143; τῇ ὁμοῦ ἐτρεφόμην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.365; ἅμα τράφεν ἠδ᾽ ἐγένοντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.251, etc.}}; κάρτιστοι τράφεν ἀνδρῶν **grew up** the strongest men, {{Refs|[prev. work] 266}}:—properly a boy was called τρεφόμενος only so long as he remained in the charge of the women, i. e. till his fifth year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136}}; ἐξ ὅτου 'τράφην ἐγώ from the time when I **left the nursery,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 322}}; but even of pre-natal growth, ἐν σκότοισινηδύος τεθραμμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 665, compare “Th.” 754 (Lyric poetry):—generally, in Trag., τραφεὶς μητρὸς εὐγενοῦς ἄπο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1229}}; ὅπως πατρὸς δείξεις οἷος ἐξ οἵου τράφης{{Refs|[prev. work] 557; κρατίστου πατρὸς.. τραφείς [prev. author] “Ph.” 3: παῖδες μητέρων τεθραμμέναι}} **true nurslings of** your mothers, implying a reproach for unmanliness (assuming variant), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 792}}; μιᾶς τρέφει πρὸς νυκτός **art nursed** by night alone, i. e. art blind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 374.}}
**II.2** of slaves, cattle, dogs and the like, **rear and keep** them, κύνας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.69, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.22, etc.; ἵππους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.766}}; λέοντος ἶνιν (see. σίνις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 717 (Lyric poetry); μῆλα [prev. author] “Eu.” 946 (Lyric poetry); ὄφιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 226 (conjecture for στρέφουσι); ἰκτῖνα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 628; ὄρτυγας 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 214; ὄρνιθας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197c}}; οἱ τρέφοντες (i.e. τοὺς ἐλέφαντας) the **keepers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 571b33; τ. παιδαγωγούς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.187; also τ. γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 749}}; τ. [ἑταίραν], [πόρνας], **keep**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 87}}; ὁ τρέφων one's **master,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC(?): Nicolaus Comicus 1.11, 36: _metaphorically_, αἰγιαλὸν ἔνδον τρέφει}} **he keeps** a sea-beach in the house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 110}}:—_passive_, **to be bred, reared,** δοῦλος οὐκ ὠνητός, ἀλλ᾽ οἴκοι τραφείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1123}}; ἐν τῇ σῇ οἰκίᾳ γέγονεν καὶ τέθραπται was born and **bred,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85e}}; Ἀγαθῖνον θρεμένον (i. e. τεθρεμμένον, ={θρεπτόν}, see at {θρεπτός} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|1) ἑαυτῶν “MAMA” 4.275 B (Dionysopolis, 2nd c.AD)}}; Νείκην τὴν θρεμένην μου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]276 A (Dionysopolis, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **tend, cherish,** τὸν μὲν ἐγὼ φίλεόν τε καὶ ἔτρεφον, of Calypso, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.135, compare 7.256}}; of plants, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.53; θρέψασα φυτὸν ὥς 18.57, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.175.}}
**II.4** of parts of the body, **let grow, cherish, foster,** χαίτην.. Σπερχειῷ τρέφε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.142; τῷ θεῷ [πλόκαμον] τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 494; ὑπήνην ἄκουρον τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 476 (Lyric poetry)}}; τ. κόμην, ={κομᾶν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82; [τρίχες] πολλῷ ἐλαίῳ τραφεῖσαι 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 112: also τά θ᾽ ὕεσσι τρέφει ἀλοιφήν}} things which **put** fat **on** swine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.410; τεθραμμένη εἰς πολυσαρκίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.22.}}
**II.5** in Poets, of earth and sea, **breed, produce, teem with,** οὐδὲν ἀκιδνότερον γαῖα τ. ἀνθρώποιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.130; ἄγρια, τά τε τρέφει οὔρεσιν ὕλη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.52; φάρμακα, ὅσα τρέφει εὐρεῖα χθών 11.741; ὅσ᾽ ἤπειρος.. τρέφει ἠδὲ θάλασσα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 582; πολλὰ γᾶ τρέφει δεινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 585 (Lyric poetry), compare 128, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1181; θάλασσα.. τρέφουσα πορφύρας ἰσάργυρον κηκῖδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 959}}; ὃν πόντος τ., i. e. the sailors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.48}}: rare in Prose, ἀεί τι ἡ Αιβύη τρέφει καινόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 746b8.}}
**II.6** in Poets also, simply, **have within oneself, contain,** ὅ τι καὶ πόλις τέτροφεν ἄφιλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 186 (Lyric poetry), compare “Tr.” 817}}; τρέφειν τὴν γλῶσσαν ἡσυχαιτέραν **to keep** his tongue more quiet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1089; ἡ γλῶσσα τὸν θυμὸν δεινὸν τ. [prev. author] “Aj.” 1124; τἀληθὲς γὰρ ἰσχῦον τρέφω [prev. author] “OT” 356 (so in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, τ. ἰσχυρὸν τὸ ἐλεινόν “R.” 606b); τ. νόσον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 795; ἐκ φόβου φόβον τ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 28; ἄταν [prev. author] “Aj.” 644 (Lyric poetry)}}; οἵας λατρείας.. τρέφει what services.. **she has as her lot,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 503}}; ἐν ἐλπίσιν τρέφω.. ἥξειν I **cherish** hopes that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 897}}; τὸν Καδμογενῆ τρέφει.. βιότου πολύπονον [πέλαγος] **is** his **daily lot,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 117 (Lyric poetry, but Reiske's conjecture στρέφει is probably)}}; πόνοι τρέφοντες βροτούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 367 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **maintain, support,** τ. ἀνδρὸς μόχθος ἡμένας ἔσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 921, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.106; τοῦ τρέφοντος Ἡλίου χθονὸς φύσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 633; τ. τὸν πατέρα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.13; τὴν οἰκίαν ὅλην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.67; οὐ δίκαιον τρέφεσθαι ὑπὸ πατρὸς ὑὸν ἡβῶντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568e; τὰ κτήνη χιλῷ ἐτρέφοντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.25}}; γάλακτι, τυρῷ, κρέασι τ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 4.3.10}}; σίτῳ, ὄψῳ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lac.” 1.3}}; **feed** a patient, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.503, 19.185}}; **provide the food for** an employee, σοῦ τρέφοντος αὐτόν, ἐμοῦ δὲ ἱματίζοντος αὐτόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1647.14 (2nd c.AD)}}; also τ. ἀπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.1; ἔκ τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1479 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b.}}
**III.2** **maintain** an army or fleet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.9 (_passive_)}}; τ. τὰς ναῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.5, 5.1.24}}; τ. τὸ ναυτικὸν ἀπὸ τῶν νήσων{{Refs|[prev. work] 4.8.9; ἐκ τῶν κωμῶν τρέφεσθαι [prev. author] “An.” 7.4.11, etc.}}
**III.3** of land, **feed, maintain** one, τρέφει γὰρ οὗτος [ὁ ἀγρὸς].. με {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 98.2, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 63, 466, +others}}
**III.4** of women, **feed** or **suckle** an infant, συνεξομοιοῦται τὰ τρεφόμενα ταῖς τρεφούσαις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.88; γυνὴ τρέφουσα [prev. work] 87}}; ἡ τρέφουσα, ={ἡ τροφός}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.44.}}
**III.5** of food, **nourish,** τὰ Ἡρακλεωτικὰ τρέφει οὐχ ὁμοίως τοῖς ἀμυγδάλοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Diocl.fragment 126, compare 117; ἡ οὐκ ἐπιτηδείως τῷ σώματι διδομένη τροφὴ οὐ τρέφει 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.49; πυρῶν.. ὅσοι κοῦφοι.. ἧττον τρέφουσι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Vict.Att” 6; τὸ δέρμα πᾶν αὐτοῖς ὡς ἂν ὑπὸ φλεγματώδους αἵματος τρεφόμενον οἰδαλέον γίνεται [prev. author] 18(2).118, compare 106.}}
**IV** **bring up, rear, educate,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 19, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.53, etc.; τῷ λόγῳ τ. καὶ παιδεύεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534d; θρέψαι καὶ παιδεῦσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.18; Δήμητερ ἡ θρέψασα τὴν ἐμὴν φρένα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 479}}; ἡ θρέψασα (i.e. γῆ) the **motherland,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 47}}:—_middle_, ἐθρέψω Ξέρξην ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἤθεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 695e}}; ἡ θρεψαμένη one's **motherland,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 85:}} —_passive_, ὀρθῶς, εὖ τραφῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 401e, Alcaeus1.120e}}; παιδείᾳ, ἐν ταύτῃ τῇ παιδείᾳ τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 695c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 1.16; ἐν πολυτρόποις ξυμφοραῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44; ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172c; ἐν χλιδῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.54; ἐν ἐλευθερίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175d, “Mx.” 239a; ἐν ἄλλοις νόμοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327a14; ἐν φωνῇ βαρβάρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 341c; πάσαις Μούσαισι “BCH” 50.444 (Thespiae, 4th c.AD).}}
**V** the _passive_ sometimes came to mean little more than **to be,** ἐπ᾽ ἐμοὶ πολέμιον ἐτράφη (i.e. τὸ γένος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 335 (Lyric poetry), compare “Th.” 141, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 805.}}
**B** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses an _intransitive_}}_aorist 2_ _active_ ἔτραφον in _passive_ sense (which is to τρέφομαι, τέτροφα (_intransitive_) as ἔδρακον to δέρκομαι, δέδορκα, etc.), ὃς.. ἔτραφ᾽ ἄριστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.279; λέοντε ἐτραφέτην ὑπὸ μητρί 5.555}}; τραφέμεν (_Epic dialect_ for τραφεῖν){{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.199, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.28, +others}}; ἐπεὶ τράφ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάρῳ, i. e. when he **was well-grown,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.661}}:—as _transitive_ the _aorist 2_ is used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.90, and τράφε in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.53 is _Doric dialect_}}_imperfect_:—ἐτράφην is perhaps post-Homeric; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ τράφη is see reading in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.661, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐτράφημεν and _1st pers._ _sing._ ἐτράφην}} (περ) variants in {{Refs|23.84; τράφη is in all codices of 3.201, 11.222, which should probably be emended from 2.661}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔτραφεν [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.348 (variant{ἔτραφον}), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.417 (variant{ἔτραφον})}}]; τράφεν in all codices of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.251, 266, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.201, also (with see l. τράφον) in 4.723}}: the **vox nihili** ἐτράφεμεν, found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.84}} as cited by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.149, was emended by Scaliger to ἐτράφομεν}}:—the _reduplicate_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ τέτραφ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.279}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τέτραφεν {{Refs|23.348}}, are ff. _lines_, though found in many codices Later this _aorist_ became obsolete, except in _Epic dialect_ imitators, as in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 55, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.774.}}

5143:
** τρέχω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.386, etc.:}}_future_ θρέξομαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1005 (_anapaest meter_), (μετα-) [prev. author] “Pax” 261, (περῖ) [prev. author] “Ra.” 193}}; θρέξω only in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 108}}; but ἀπο-θρέξεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 232:}}_aorist 1_ ἔθρεξα (see. below):—but the usual _future_ and _aorist_ come from the root δραμ-, _i.e. _ δρᾰμοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 878, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.45, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ δραμέομαι [{{LXX+6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102; late δραμῶ LXX.Cant.1.4; but ὑπερ-δραμῶ {5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 3 (uncertain reading)}}]; δράμομαι in _compound_ ἀναδράμεται {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.575 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus):}}_aorist 2_ ἔδρᾰμον (see. below): _perfect_ δεδράμηκα [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 38, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 741, (ἀνα-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55, (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.7.6, (περι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Clitopho” 410a, (συν-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.9:}}_pluperfect_ ἐδεδραμήκεσαν (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92: poetry}}_perfect_ δέδρομα (ἀνα-, ἐπι-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.412, 20.357:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ δεδράμημαι (ἐπι-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.1.}}—The Verb is not common in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, who has _present_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.520, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.386; in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.599, 602, _Ionic dialect_ Iterat. θρέξασκον (ἔθρεξα}} was also old _Attic dialect_, epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 20, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1569 (assuming variant, ἔβρεξε Weil), (περι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 657)}}; but the common _aorist_ was ἔδραμον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.393, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.207, +others}}—_Doric dialect_ **τράχω** [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.32, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, “Etymologicum Magnum” 356.10}}: _future_ θραξοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**run,** of men, ἰθὺς δράμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.207, etc.; θρέξασκον ἐπισταμένοισι πόδεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.599; τρέχει Ὅρκος ἅμα.. δίκῃσιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 219; ᾤχεο τρέχων 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 37, 110 (τράχων cf. Ahrens)}}; βαδίζειν καὶ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 468a}}; τρέχων, opposed to βάδην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.30; τ. χερσίν, οὐ ποδωκείᾳ σκελῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 37: of horses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.393, 520}}: the _participle_ is frequently added to another Verb, τί οὐ τρέχων σὺ τὰς τραπέζας ἐκφέρει; why do you not **run and** carry out.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 69.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327b; see below 2.}}
**2** of things, **move quickly,** τὸ δὲ [τρύπανον] τ. ἐμμενὲς αἰεί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.386, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.413; ναῦς παρὰ γῆν ἔδραμεν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 856; πόλιν.. ἐξ οὐρίων δραμοῦσαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1083}}; τὸ δ᾽ ἐν ποσὶ τράχον ἴτω let what is now before me go **trippingly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.32; ἐπὶ καρδίαν ἔδραμε.. σταγών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1121 (Lyric poetry); ἔρις δραμοῦσα τοῦ προσωτάτω}}**having run its course,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 731}}; πυρετὸς.. ἥκει τρέχων has come **quickly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Nicopho Comicus 12.}}
**3** οἱ τρέχοντες a constellation rising with Libra, Antiochus cited in Teucrum in {{Refs|Boll “Sphaera” 58.}}
**II** with _accusative_ loci, **run over,** ῥόθια πεδία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1117 (Lyric poetry); ὁ ἵππος τ. καὶ πρανῆ καὶ ὄρεια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.1:}} —in _Attic dialect_ Prose θέω seems to be more frequently in the _present_, and in some phrases used exclusively, e.g. θεῖν δρόμῳ, see at {θέω} (A) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.1 and compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.18.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, δραμεῖν ἀγῶνα, βῆμα, δίαυλον, δρόμον, **run** a course, a heat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 883, 954, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 235, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 741, etc.}}; λαμπάδας, i. e. torch-races, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1028.14: frequently _metaphorically_, ἀγῶνα δρ.}} **run** a risk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 489, compare “IA” 1455; ἀγῶνα θανάσιμον δραμούμενον [prev. author] “Or.” 878}}; πολλοὺς ἀγῶνας δραμέονται περὶ σφέων αὐτῶν **run** for their life or safety, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102; κινδύνων τὸν μέγιστον τ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.47}}; τὸν ὑπὲρ ψυχῆς ἀγῶνα, κίνδυνον ὑπὲρ τῆς ψυχῆς τ., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.48, 4.4; ἐσχάτην τρέχοντες ταύτην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.87.3: sometimes the _accusative_ is omitted, περὶ ἑωυτοῦ τρέχων}} **running** for his life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.57}}; περὶ τῆς ψυχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.37; φόνου πέρι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1264}}; περὶ νίκης _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8 (ἐπὶ νίκῃ Rehdantz)}}; compare θέω{{Refs|(A) 1.2, δρόμος 1.2, κρέας near the end}}
**II.3** παρὰ ἓν πάλαισμα ἔδραμε νικᾶν he was within one fall or bout of carrying off the victory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.33}}; compare παρά{{Refs|with 111.5, τριάζω 1.}}
**II.4** **commit,** μηδ᾽ ἑτέρας δραμεῖν ἀταξίας ἢ ἀσελγίας {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 5.1711.34 (6th c.AD).}}

5144:
** τρῐάκοντα** [ᾱκ],
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **τρῐήκ-**, late Gr. **τρῐάντα** (which see), οἱ, αἱ, τά, _indeclinable_; but _genitive_ τριηκόντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 696}} also in later _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 67; _dative_ τριηκόντεσσιν “Anthologia Graeca” 11.41 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus)}}:—**thirty,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.516, “IG” 12.1.9, etc.}}; τ. καὶ ἕνα **thirty-one,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]42(1).71.9 (Epid., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** οἱ τ., especially,
**II.1** at Sparta, **a council of war of thirty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.7, “HG” 3.4.2, 20, 4.1.5, +others}}
**II.2** at Athens, **the Thirty,** commonly called **the thirty tyrants,** appointed on the taking of Athens {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC), [prev. work] 2.3.2, “IG” 22.6.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1400a18.}}
**II.3** certain magistrates, see at {τεσσαράκοντ; οἱ λογισταὶ οἱ τ}. {{Refs|(“IG” 12.91.8) are sometimes called simply οἱ τ., “IG” 12.191.1, 193.1}}. [In late epigram ᾰκ, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.298, etc.]}}

5145:
** τρῐᾱκόσιοι**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **τρῐηκ-**, _Doric dialect_ **τριακάτιοι** (which see), αι, α, **three hundred,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.697, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 715, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.202, etc.}}
**II** οἱ τ. at Athens, the richest members of the συμμορίαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.29, 18.171, etc.}}
**II.2** a judicial body at Megara, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.295: compare τριακάσιοι.}}

5146:
** τρίβολος** [ῐ], ὁ,
name of various prickly plants,
**a** **water-chestnut, Trapa natans,** τ. ἔνυδρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.9.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.15.}}
**b** **caltrops, Tribulus terrestris,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 576}}; τ. περικαρπιάκανθος, χερσαῖος, {{LXX+7th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.1.6, 6.1.3, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus [same place]; ἄκανθαι καὶ τ. LXX.Gen.3.18; βάτοι καὶ τ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.680, compare “IG” 14.1934f1 (from Rome):— 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 47 calls sour wine ὀξύτερος τριβόλων.}}
**c** τ. φυλλάκανθος, **thorny trefoil, Fagonia cretica,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.5.3.}}
**d** τ. παραθαλάσσιος, **prickly samphire, Echinophora spinosa,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 32.}}
**II** τρίβολοι, οἱ, **a threshing-machine, a board with sharp stones fixed in the bottom,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 85.36, +others, LXX.2Ki.12.31, 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 3.30}}; τ. ξύλινος (in the section περὶ κάρρων) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Edict.Diocl.” 15.41; τριβόλους ἀχυρότριβας “Anthologia Graeca” 6.104 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** **caltrop,** i. e. a four-spiked implement thrown on the ground to lame the enemy's horses, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 100.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.200a, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.39.2, 4.3.17, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.15.2, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 3.24.}}
**III.b** τ. πηχῶν έ a larger contrivance for stopping boulders, etc., thrown down a slope, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 38.2.}}
**III.c** οἱ κατακρημνώμενοι τ. an instrument hung from the walls of a fortress as a defence against battering-rams, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 100.15.}}
**III.d** a kind of missile, τριβόλων σιδηρῶν σφενδονῆται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 20.1}}; οἱ τ. οἱ καιόμενοι a kind of incendiary missile, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 100.20, compare 94.9.}}
**IV** part of the bit of a bridle, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 782 (a). 9 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.148, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V** uncertain meaning in naval dockyard records, σίδηρος ἐκ τοῦ τ. {{Refs|“IG” 22.1629.1154, 1631.338.}}
**VI** as _adjective_, **three-tiered,** πυρὰ πυργοειδὴς τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.5.}}

5147:
** τρίβος** [ῐ], ἡ,
but ὁ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1251, 1258, “El.” 103, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 22}}: (τρίβω):— **worn** or **beaten track,** ἐν τρίβῳ μάλιστα οἰκημένοι in the **path** (of the war), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140. β᾽ (so ἐν τ. τοῦ πολέμου κείμενος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.34, 11.54); τ. ἁμαξήρης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1251; λεπτὴν τ. ἐξανύσαντες 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.156}}; ἡ τ. τῆς ἀτραποῦ **the track** of the path, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.49}}; διασχισθέντες [τῆς ὁδοῦ] τρίβῳ τινί by **following a track,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.13.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **path,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 448; ποίην τις βιότοιο τάμοι τρίβο; “Anthologia Graeca” 9.359 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus); βιότου τ. ὁδεύειν “Anacreontea” 38.2; ποίην τις πρὸς ἔρωτας ἴοι τρίβο; “Anthologia Graeca” 5.301.1 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus); τῆς αἰτίας ἴχνος καὶ τ.}}**track,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.68o f: _plural_, τρίβοι ἐρώτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1042 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **rubbing, attrition,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 391 (Lyric poetry)}}; τ. κρηπῖδος **the rubbing** of a shoe, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.12.}}
**II.2** **socket, friction-joint,** ἡ κεφαλὴ τοῦ βραχίονος.. τρίβον ἑωυτῇ πεποιημένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7, compare 55; τὸ ἔθος τρίβον ποιεῖ [prev. author] “Mochl.” 41}}; **area of friction** or **pressure** of a bandage, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Off.” 8.}}
**II.3** **delay,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 197 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **bodily exercise,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 592 (_plural_).}}

5149:
** τρίζω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.5, 7, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.55, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 504a19, +others}}; but _perfect_ τέτριγα is more frequently in _present_ sense, _Epic dialect_ _participle_ τετριγῶτες, for τετριγότες, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.314:}} —properly of sounds uttered by animals (compare τριγμός, τρύζω), **utter a shrill cry,** of young birds, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.314}}; of bats, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.7, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.110}}; of the Τρωγοδύται, τετρίγασι κατά περ αἱ νυκτερίδες {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.183}}; of partridges, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 536b14}}; of the ἴυγξ,{{Refs|[prev. work] 504a19}}; of locusts, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mir.” 844b26}}; of young swallows, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 21}}; of the elephant, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Zeux.” 10}}; of mice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1132, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 108.23, etc.}}; of the fish called σελάχη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535b25}}: also applied to the noise made by ghosts, 'squeak and gibber', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.101, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.5, 9}}; ἔτριζον δίκην ἀσπίδων (αἱ ψυχαί) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.44.}}
**2** of other sounds, τετρίγει (_Epic dialect_ _pluperfect_) δ᾽ ἄρα νῶτα θρασειάων ἀπὸ χειρῶν the wrestlers' backs **creaked,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.714}}; so τρίζει, **crepitates,** of a broken collar-bone, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de signis fracturarum - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 13; τέτριγε δ᾽ ὁ κυνόδων}}**grinds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 21}}; τὸ τρίζειν ἀκουσίως involuntary **gnashing,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.150; τ. τοὺς ὀδόντας NT.Mark.9.18; τοῖς ὀδοῦσι “Hippiatrica” 86}}; of a musical string, **give a crack,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 6.54 (Paul. Sil.)}}; of an axle, **creak,** ἄξων τετριγὼς ὑπ᾽ ἄμαξαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.4.14}}; so of a cart-wheel, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 52.2}}; of a shoe, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 37 (τρύζοι codices)}}; ἡ κοιλίη τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.55}}; of **singing** in the ears, τὰ ὦτα τέτριγε {{Refs|[prev. work] 15}}; of the **hissing** or **crackling** of a person burnt in the fire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 120.}}

5150:
** τρῐμην-ος**, ον,
(μήν) **of three months,** χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 164; so ἡ τ.}}**period of three months,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.124, “IG” 22.1358 ii 7, 11, +others, “PCair.Zen.” 124.5, 440.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ τ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.38.6, etc.; τ. παιδία}}**born after three months,** {{Refs|Comica Adespota 213}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, τρίμηνα ἐκτιτρώσκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.45.}}
**2** **three months old,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 562b28.}}
**3** πυροὶ τ. wheat sown in spring, **so as to ripen in three months,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 4, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.1.4, “CP” 4.11.1, “PCair.Zen.” 155.6 (3rd c.BC).}}

5152:
** τρί-στεγος**, ον,
**of** or **with three stories,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.68, 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 13.8, “POxy.” 99.5 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**II** τὸ τ. (i.e. οἴκημα) **the third story,** {{NT|NT.Acts.20.9}}:—also **τριστέγη**, ἡ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.46.}}

5153:
** τρισ-χίλιοι** [χῑ], αι, α,
_Doric dialect_ **τρισ-χήλιοι** **Abh.Berl. Akad.** 1925 (5). 25 (from Cyrene):—**three thousand,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|lines 20.221, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.97, etc.}}: in _singular_ with collective _substantive_, ἀσπὶς τρισχιλία {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 3.1.}}
**II** οἱ τ., at Athens, the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3000 nominated by the 30 Tyrants, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.18.}}

5154:
** τρίτος** [ῐ],
_Aeolic dialect_ **τέρτος** (see. τέρτα) η, ον, (τρεῖς) **third,** τοῖσι δ᾽ ἐπὶ τρίτος ἦλθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.185, compare 14.471}}; τρίτος αὐτός himself **the third,** i. e. **with two others** (see. αὐτός {{Refs|1.6) τ. ἡμίδραχμον}}**two drachmae and a half,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator _Attic dialect_ 8.4}}; compare ἡμιτάλαντο; τ. γενέσθαι to be **third** in a race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.34, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 11}}:—**the third** frequently appears as **completing the tale,** e.g. **the third and last** libation was offered to Ζεὺς Σωτήρ, Διὸς σωτηρίου σπονδὴ τρίτου κρατῆρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 425, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 55; ἔγχει κἀπιβόα τρίτον παιῶν᾽, ὡς νόμος ἐστίν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 131.5 (compare τριτόσπονδος)}}: _metaphorically_, Κράτος τε καὶ Δίκη σὺν τῷ τρίτῳ.. Ζηνί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 244, compare “Eu.” 759, “Supp.” 26 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τρίτην ἐπενδίδωμι (i.e. πληγήν) **the third and finishing** stroke, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1386}}; Ἐρινὺς.. αἷμα πίεται, τρίτην πόσιν, i. e. the blood of Clytemnestra and Aegisthus, the first being that of the children of Thyestes, the second that of Agamemnon, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 578, compare 1066 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** τρίτη, with or without ἡμέρα, **the day after tomorrow,** ἐς τρίτην ἡμέραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 612; εἰς τρίτην 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 4; τῇ τρίτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.17, etc.; τρίτῃ καὶ τετάρτῃ [prev. author] “An.” 4.8.21, etc.}}; but ἐχθὲς καὶ τ. ἡμέραν yesterday and **the day before,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.3.11:}} — διὰ τρίτης **two days later,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 368}}; but, **every other day,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 48, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.354.}}
**II.2** with other Nouns omitted, ἡ τ. (i.e. χορδή) **the third string** in the heptachord, ={ἡ παραμέση}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 920a16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1137b:}} —ἡ τ. (i.e. πληγή) **the third blow,** see above 1:—ἡ τ. (i.e. μερίς) **the third part** of a coin or weight, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see entry {ἕκτη}, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. post Τριτοπάτορε; ἐγένετο ὁ μέδιμνος χρυσοῦ καὶ δύο τριτῶν “IPE” 12.32 A 63 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}]; **third of a stater,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.64.}}
**III** τρίτον as _adverb_, **thirdly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 55, “fragment” 380}}; **a third time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1417, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.182 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; πρῶτον μὲν.., δεύτερον δὲ.., τ. δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358c}}; τοῦτο τ. this **third time,** {{LXX+NT+8th c.BC+|LXX.Num.22.32, NT.John.21.14:—in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always τὸ τρίτον, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.225, 6.186, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.55, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 997, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5, etc.:—also ἐκ τρίτου}}**in the third place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 54a}} (but = **the third time,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Matt.26.44, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.32); ἐκ τρίτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1178, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500a}}:—regular _adverb_ τρίτως **in the third degree,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 56b.}}
**III.2** τρίτον **thrice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Syrianus Philosophus et Rhetor “in Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in” 134.15, “Geoponica” 2.39.7, +others, Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.123; Elean ἐν τρίτον “Schwyzer” 412.4.}}
**IV** τὸ τ. μέρος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.177, etc.; τὸ τ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 46; τὸ τ. τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.7.4, cf. LXX.Num.15.6}}; ἐπὶ τῷ τ. at **the third** signal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.4.}}
**V** τρίτα, τά,
**V.1** (i.e. ἱερά) **a sacrifice offered the third day after the funeral,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 613, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.37, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.146.}}
**V.2** τὰ τρίτα λέγειν τινί play **the third part** (like τριταγωνιστεῖν τινι), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.246, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 223.17.}}
**V.3** πρῶτα δραμεῖν καὶ δεύτερα καὶ τ. win.. **third place** in the race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Epigrammata - PLG” 3 (τρίτατα conjecture Bgk.)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _trlīyas,_ Latin _terlius,_ etc.)

5156:
** τρόμος**, ὁ,
**trembling, quaking, quivering,**
**1** from fear, πάντας ἕλε τ. {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.14; ὑπό τε τ. ἔλλαβε γυῖα 3.34, etc.; τ. μ᾽ ὑφέρπει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 463 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 607 (troch.); τ. καὶ ἔκστασις NT.Mark.16.8; from love, τ. δὲ παῖσαν ἄγρει 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.13}}: _plural_, **shiverings,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 92.}}
**2** from cold, τ. καὶ ῥῖγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 62b, compare 85e; γίνεται ὁ τ. διὰ κατάψυξιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 871a33: generally, ἰνῶν ἀτονία καὶ τ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.76M.; περὶ τ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.584.}}
**3** of earthquakes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 366b18, “Mu.” 396a10; σεισμοὶ ἐν γῇ καὶ τρόμοι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.373d.}}

5157:
** τροπ-ή**, ἡ,
(τρέπω) **turn, turning**:
**I** τροπαὶ ἠελίοιο:
**I.a** ὅθι τροπαὶ ἠελίοιο apparently denotes a point on the horizon, probably the West or place where the sum sets (so {{Refs 8th c.BC+|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1787.20), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.404.}}
**I.b** each of two fixed points in the solar year, **the solstices,** first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, ἠελίοιο τροπῇς}}**at the time of the (winter) solstice, **{{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Op.” 479; μετὰ τροπὰς ἠελ. [prev. work]564,663 (with _Doric dialect_ _accusative_ _plural_ in -ᾰς); πεδὰ τὰς τροπάς 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 33.5:—later the two solstices were distinguished as τροπαὶ θεριναί and χειμεριναί, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 767c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 542b4ff, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.405, etc. (rarely in _sing._, τροπὴ θερινή 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 364b2, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 1.13}}; τ. χειμερινή{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 15); τροπαὶ νότιοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 542b11}}; τ. βόρειοι, νότιοι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.601a:}} —when τροπαί is used alone, it mostly refers to **the winter solstice,** but the sense is always determined by the context, see {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} [same places]; περὶ ἡλίου τροπάς (i.e. χειμερινάς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.39; εὐθὺς ἐκ τροπῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 542b20}}:—sometimes also of other heavenly bodies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39d; περὶ Πλειάδος δύσιν καὶ τροπάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 542b23, etc.; ἄστρων ἐπιτολάς, δύσεις, τροπάς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 30.5; τροπὰς τῶν ἐνδεδεμένων ἄστρων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 296b4; τροπαὶ ἡλίου καὶ σελήνης 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.40U.:—sometimes four in number (the two equinoxes and two solstices), 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.11, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).22; so (on a sun-dial) θερινὴ τ., ἰσημερινὴ τ., χειμερινὴ τ., Ἀρχ.Δελτ. 12.236 (from Samos).}}
**I.2** **turn, change,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1316a17; πλείους τραπόμενος τροπὰς τοῦ Εὐρίπου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.90}}; τ. πρὸς τὸ βέλτιον **turn** for the better, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.25S.; ὀξυτέρας τρεπόμενος τ. τοῦ χαμαιλέοντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 23; αἱ τοῦ κόλακος ὥσπερ πολύποδος τ. [prev. author] 2.52f; αἱ τῶ αἵματος τ. καὶ ἀλλοιώσιες 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 102c}}; αἱ περὶ τὸν ἀέρα τ. **changes** in the air or weather, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.946f}}; of wine, **a turning sour,** {{Refs|[prev. work]939f (compare τροπίας)}}; **going bad,** of food, τ. καὶ διαφθορὰ τῶν παρακειμένων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.208}}; of phonetic **change** in language, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 210.4, Hdn.Gr. 2.932.}}
**I.3** τροπαὶ λέξεως **a change** of speech **by figures** or **tropes** (τρόποι), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 6, compare 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.10, +others}}
**I.4** αἱ τροπαί, ={αἱ τροπαῖαι}, **alternating** winds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 940b16, 21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.3.1, “Vent.” 26.}}
**II** **the turning about of the enemy, putting to flight** or **routing** him, τροπήν (or τροπάς) τινος ποιεῖν or ποιεῖσθαι put one to **flight,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 246 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.19, 6.69, etc.}}; οἵαν ἂν τροπὴν Εὐρυσθέως θείμην (θείην codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 743; τροπὴ γινομένη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.167, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49, 50, etc.: poetry, ἐν μάχης τροπῇ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1237}}; ἐν τροπῇ δορός in **the rout** caused by the spear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1275, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 82.}}
**III** used by {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus for θέσις}} **position,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985b17, 1042b14, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.5.2, 4.5.6.}}
**IV** **a coin,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare τροπαϊκόν.

5158:
** τρόπος**, ὁ,
(τρέπω) **turn, direction, way,** διώρυχες παντοίους τρόπους ἔχουσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108; διώρυχας τετραμμένας πάντα τ. [prev. author] 1.189, compare 199: but}}
**II** commonly, **way, manner, fashion, guise,** τρόπῳ τῷ παρεόντι χρεώμενοι going on **as we are,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 97; τ. ὑποδημάτων Κρητικός 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 62; πᾶς τ. μορφῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 192; τίς ὁ τ. τῆς ξυμφορᾶ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 99; ἀσκεῖν τὸν υἱὸν τὸν ἐπιχώριον τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 47; ὁ αὐτός που τ. τέχνης ἰατρικῆς ὅσπερ καὶ ῥητορικῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270b}}; **tenor,** of documents, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PGen.” 16.11 (3rd c.AD)}}, etc.: also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, κεχώρισται τοὺς τ.}} in its **ways,** in its **kind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28; ψυχῆς τρποι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 445c, etc.; οἱ περὶ τὴν ψυχὴν τ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 588a20:}} —in various adverbial usages:
**II.1** _dative_, τίνι τρόπ; **how?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 793, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1294; τῷ τ.; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 679, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 909, 1008; ποίῳ τ.; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 763, etc.}}; τοιούτῳ τ., τ. τοιῷδε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94, 3.68; ἄλλῳ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232b, etc.}}; ἑνί γέ τῳ τ. **in** one **way** or other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 402, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 96d}}; παντὶ τ. **by** all **means,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 301 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.25}}; οὐδενὶ τ., μηδενὶ τ., **in** no **wise, by** no **mdans, on** no **account,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.111, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49a, etc.}}; ἑκουσίῳ τ. willingly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 751}}; τρόπῳ φρενός by **way** of intelligence, i.e. in lieu of the intelligence which is lacking to the child, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 754 (assuming variant): poetry in _plural_, τρόποισι ποίοι; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 468}}; τρόποισιν οὐ τυραννικοῖς not after **the fashion** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 479; ναυκλήρου τρόποις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_ in _accusative_, τίνα τρόπο; **how?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 170, “Ra.” 460}}; τ. τινά **in** a **manner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1300, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432e}}; τοῦτον τὸν τ., τόνδε τὸν τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 199a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.9; ὃν τ.}}**how,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.8; as, LXX.Psa.41(42).1; τ. τὸν αὐτόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 274; πάντα τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 700 (Lyric poetry), etc.; μηδένα τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.8}}; τὸν μέγαν τ., οὐ σμικρὸν τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 284, 465; τὸν Ἀργείων τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).58; Σαμιακὸν τ. 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 13}}; βάρβαρον τ. (βρόμον ex Scholia Schütz) **in** barbarous **guise** or **fashion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 463}}; πίτυος τρόπον **after the manner** of a pine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37}}; ὄρνιθος τ. **like** a bird, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.57, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 49 (_anapaest meter_), 390 (Lyric poetry), etc.; later, ἐς ὄρνιθος τ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Halc.” 1, compare “Bis Acc.” 27: rarely in _plural_, πάντας τρόπους}} in all **ways,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 94d.}}
**II.3** with Preps., τὸν ἐγκώμιον ἀμφὶ τρόπον in **way** of praise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).77:— δι᾽ οὗ τρόπου 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 539.6; διὰ τοιούτου τ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.66:— ἐς τὸν νῦν τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6; εἰς τὸν αὐτὸν τ. μετασκευάσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.8}}; ἐς ὄρνιθος τ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(see. above 2):— ἐκ παντὸς τ. [prev. author] “An.” 3.1.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.95, etc.; ἐξ ἑνός γέ του τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 187, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34; μηδὲ ἐξ ἑνὸς τ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.30; μηδ᾽ ἐξ ἑνὸς τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.3:— ἐν τῷ ἑαυτῶν τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67, compare 1.97, etc.; ἐν τρόπῳ βοσκήματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 807a: in _plural_, γυναικὸς ἐν τρόποις, ἐν τ. Ἰξίονος, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 918, “Eu.” 441:— κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.5; κατὰ πάντα τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 451 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.30, etc.; κατ᾽ οὐδένα τ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.84.8, etc.; κατ᾽ ἄλλον τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 417b; κατὰ τὸν Ἑλληνικὸν τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.28: in _plural_, κατὰ πολλοὺς τ. [prev. work] 8.1.46, etc.:}} —μετὰ ὁτουοῦν τ. in any **manner** whatever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27:— ἑνὶ σὺν τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.14.}}
**II.4** κατὰ τρόπον,
**II.4.a** **according to custom,** κατὰ τὸν τ. τῆς φύσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 804b; opposed to παρὰ τὸν τ. τὸν ἑαυτῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.63, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.1.}}
**II.4.b** **fitly, duly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 283, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310c, etc.; οὐδαμῶς κατὰ τ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 638c; opposed to ἀπὸ τρόπου}}**unreasonable, absurd,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 421d, “Tht.” 143c, etc.; so θαυμαστὸν οὐδὲν οὐδ᾽ ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀνθρωπείου τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76.}}
**II.5** πρὸς τρόπου **fitting, suitable,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 309.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III** of persons, **a way of life, habit, custom,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.29}}; μῶν ἡλιαστ; Answ. μἀλλὰ θατέρου τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 109; ἐγὼ δὲ τούτου τοῦ τ. πώς εἰμ᾽ ἀεί [prev. author] “Pl.” 246, compare 630.}}
**III.2** a man's **ways, habits, character, temper,** ὀργὴν καὶ ῥυθμὸν καὶ τ. ὅστις ἂν ᾖ (variant{ὅντιν᾽ ἔχει}) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 964}}; τρόπου ἡσυχίου of a quiet **temper,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107, compare 3.36; φιλανθρώπου τ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 11; γυναικὶ κόσμος ὁ τ., οὐ τὰ χρυσία 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 92; οὐ τὸν τ., ἀλλὰ τὸν τόπον μόνον μετήλλαξεν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.78}}; τρόπου προπέτεια, ἀναίδεια, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38, 45.71; ἀφιλάργυρος ὁ τ. NT.Heb.13.5:— οὐ τοὐμοῦ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1002}}; σφόδρ᾽ ἐκ τοῦ σοῦ τ. quite of your **sort,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 93; ξυγγενεῖς τοὐμοῦ τ. [prev. work] 574:}} —πρὸς τρόπου τινός agreeable to one's **temper,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252d, compare “Lg.” 655d; πρὸς τοῦ Κύρου τρόπου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11:—opposed to ἀπὸ τρόπου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278d, “R.” 470c:—after _adjectives_, διάφοροι ὄντες τὸν τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.96; σολοικότερος τῷ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.21:—especially in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.38, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 397, 1051}}; σκληρός, ἀμνοὶ τοὺς τρόπους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 350, 935; σφόδρα τοὺς τ. Βοιώτιος 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 39; πουλύπους ἐς τοὺς τ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 101; μεθάρμοσαι τ. νέους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 311; τοὺς φιλάνορας τ. [prev. author] “Ag.” 856; νέας βουλὰς νέοισιν ἐγκαταζεύξας τ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 736; τοῖς τρόποις ὑπηρετεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1432}}; opposed to νόμοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39; ἤθη τε καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 924d.}}
**IV** in Music, like {ἁρμονία}, a particular **mode,** Αύδιος τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.17}}; but more generally, **style,** νεοσίγαλος τ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.4; ὁ ἀρχαῖος τ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 303}}; ᾠδῆς τρόπος, μουσικῆς τρόποι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398c, 424c}}; διθυραμβικοὶ τ. (distinguished from ἦθος) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.9K.; ὁ ἁρμονικὸς τῆς μουσικῆς τ. 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.12, compare 2.1}}; of art in general, πάντες τῆς εἰκαστικῆς τ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.7.}}
**V** in speaking or writing, **manner, style,** ὁ τ. τῆς λέξεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.45}}: especially in Rhet. in _plural_, **tropes,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus “Trop.” at Title Page, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Brutus.” 17.69, 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus “Inst.” 8.6.1.}}
**VI** in Logic, **mode** or **mood** of a syllogism, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.269}}, compare 1.108, 2.83: more generally, **method** of instruction or explanation, ὁ ἄνευ φθόγγων τ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.32U.}}; ὁ μοναχῇ τ. the **method** of the single cause, opposed to ὁ πλεοναχὸς τ. the **method** of manifold causes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 2p.41U.}}; **mode** of inference, ὁ κατὰ τὴν ὁμοιότητα τ., opposed to ὁ κατ᾽ ἀνασκευὴν τ. τῆς σημειώσεως, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 30, 31; αἰτιολογικὸς τ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 143 G.}}
**VII** **beam,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.208c (so in Mod.Gr., compare “Glotta” 11.249).}}

5160:
** τροφ-ή**, ἡ,
(τρέφω) **nourishment, food,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.48, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 32, 953, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, NT.Matt.3.4, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.35, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 3.16, etc.; ἡ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν ἀναγκαία τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2}}; **the means of maintaining** an army, **provisions, forage,** τροφὴν παρέχειν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.57, compare 6.93: _plural_, “OGI” 56.70 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** βίου τροφαί **way** of life, **livelihood, living,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 338, 446}}; τροφή alone, δουλίαν ἕξειν τροφήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 499, compare “OC” 362; φεῦ τῆς ἀνύμφου.. σῆς τροφῆς [prev. author] “El.” 1183; τὰς ἐκ γῆς τ. ηὕρετο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322a}}: then, simply, **mode of life,** δίκην τίνουσαι τῆς προτέρας τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 81d, compare 84b; βώμιοι τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 52.}}
**3** **that which provides** or **procures sustenance,** as the bow of Philoctetes, χερὶ πάλλων τὰν ἐμὰν μελέου τροφάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1126 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** **a meal,** τροφαῖς τέτταρσιν ἐχρῶντο Philem.{{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}} cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.11d.}}
**II** **nurture, rearing,** παιδία.. τρέφειν.. τροφήν τινα τοιήνδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, compare 3}}; χάριν τροφᾶς ἀμείβων variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 729 (Lyric poetry); νέας τροφῆς στερηθείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 511; μητρὸς τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1377: frequently in _plural_, ἐν τροφαῖσιν}} **while in the nursery,** opposed to ἐφηβήσας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 665; ἠνυτόμαν τροφαῖς [prev. author] “Ag.” 1159 (Lyric poetry); ὦ δυσάθλιαι τ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 330; αἱ ἐμαὶ τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1187; τ. δημόσιαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361a36}}; ἐκτίνων τροφάς, much like{τροφεῖα}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 548; οἷς ὀδύνας ἀντὶ τροφῶν ἔλιπον “IG” 12(5).973 (from Tenos).}}
**II.2** **education,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 599 (_plural_); τ. τε καὶ παιδεία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1179b34, 1180a26, +others}}
**II.3** **rearing** or **keeping of animals,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65; τροφαῖς ἵππων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.16.}}
**III** sometimes in Poets for the concrete θρέμμα, **brood,** νέα τ. a new **generation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 786 (Lyric poetry)}}; of animals, ἀρνῶν τροφαί, i.e. young lambs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 189.}}
**IV** a **place in which** animals **are reared,** ἰβίων τροφαί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.70, compare 62.19, +others (2nd c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.221 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}

5161:
** τρόφ-ῐμος**, ον,
also ος, η, ον, see infr.:— **nourishing, nutritious,** γάλα -ώτατον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 523a11, cf. “Pr.” 927a22 (_comparative_), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 27, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.94, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.382}}; opposed to ἄτροφος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.4.5: with _genitive_, γᾶ τρόφιμε τῶν ἐμῶν τέκνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1302 (Lyric poetry), cf. “Ion” 235 (Lyric poetry); also ὕδωρ τὸ περὶ κηπείας τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845d}}; τ. κλυστῆρες **nutritive** enemata, Lycusap. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.34t it.}}
**II** _substantive_ τρόφιμος, ὁ, a slave's **young master,** ὁ τ. σου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Epit.fragment” 1, cf. “Epit.” 160, “Pk.” 74, +others, “Comica Adespota” 24.20, 25.41 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; rendered **erilis filius** by Ter.{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Andr.” 602, “Eun.” 289, “Phorm.” 39, see 4th c.AD: Donatus, Aelius Grammaticus Latinus ad locc.: _metaphorically_, ἡ βουλὴ τὸν ἑαυτῆς τ. καὶ εὐεργέτην “SIG” 879.10 (Erythrae, 3rd c.AD): ἡ τροφίμη}}**the mistress,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.73.}}
**II.2** _neuter_ τρόφιμον, τό, **maintenance, sustenance**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 297.21 (1st c.AD)}}; τ. δουλικόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PMich.Teb.” 121vi18, +others (1st c.AD)}}: esp. **food-supply** of Alexandria, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Edict.” 13.26, “PKlein.Form.” 328.4 (6th c.AD,cf. “Arch.Papyrus” 5.294): τροφίμη σύνταξις}} contract **for board,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.175 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** _passive_, **nursling, foster-child,** παῖς τ. τίνο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 684 (Lyric poetry), cf. 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272b}}; ὁ τ., frequently in Inscrr., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“IG” 22.3969,3.3396, etc., and Papyrus, “POxy.” 1491.10 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; τ. ἀδελφός {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PCair.Preis.” 42.6 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}; _feminine_ τροφίμη {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 903.6 (4th c.AD): οἱ τ.}}**our nurslings, pupils,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520d, cf. “Lg.” 804a; τ. τῆς ἀρετῆς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 6,cf. “Anthologia Graeca” 10.52 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus):—at Sparta, οἱ τ. were young persons too poor to pay their quota to the φιλίτια}} and brought up as companions of the richer class, who paid for them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.9:— also τ. κύνες}} dogs **kept in the house,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.13, 16.31.}}
**III.2** of bodies, **healthy, strong, well-nourished,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 20 (_comparative_)}}; of plants, **flourishing, luxuriant,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.15.4 (_comparative_).}}
**III.3** τ. κύημα **viable, capable of life,** opposed to ἀνεμιαῖον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.6.}}

5162:
** τροφός**, ὁ
and ἡ, (τρέφω) **feeder, rearer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}} and always _feminine_ of a **nurse,** φίλη τ. Εὐρύκλεια {{LXX+5th c.BC+|2.361, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156, 6.61, LXX.Gen.35.8, “PCair.Zen.” 292.157 (3rd c.BC), “Glotta” 16.274 (from Egypt), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.105, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.36, etc.; ἡ τ. βασιλέως “Sammelb.” 4980 (1st c.BC)}}; of a mother, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 849.}} —The _masculine_ was usually τροφεύς (which see); but τροφός as _masculine_ occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 45, “El.” 409, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 268a, 268c.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of a city, Συράκοσαι, ἀνδρῶν ἵππων τε δαιμόνιαι τροφοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.2; Γῇ τε μητρί, φιλτάτῃ τροφῷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 16; αἵματ᾽ ἐκποθένθ᾽ ὑπὸ χθονὸς τροφοῦ [prev. author] “Ch.” 66 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1092 (Lyric poetry), “OC” 760; μήτηρ ἁπάντων γαῖα καὶ κοινὴ τ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 617; νὺξ ἄστρων τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 54; τὴν γεωργίαν τῶν ἄλλων τεχνῶν μητέρα καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 267d}}; of Miletus, τ. τοῦ.. Ἀπόλλωνος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“SIG” 906 A5 (4th c.AD).}}
**3** in _neuter_, τὸ τροφόν **that which nourishes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289a.}}
**4** τροφός, ἡ, name of a plaster, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 99.}}
**II** _passive_, **nursling,** τροφοί· ἀντὶ τοῦ θρέμματα (Meineke τροφαί), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5163:
** τροχιά**, ἡ,
**wheel-track, rut,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., etc.}}
**II** **the round of a wheel,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.478 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), 9.418 (Antip.), 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 816.}}
**III** **path,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.4.26 (= NT.Heb.12.13).}}
**IV** **elastic strand in the** τόνος **of a torsion-engine,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 54.41, probably to be read instead of τριχέα in 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 108.3.}}

5164:
** τροχός**, ὁ,
(τρέχω) **wheel,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.42, 23.394, etc.; γῆ ἐπημαξευμένη τροχοῖσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 252}}; ἐν πτερόεντι τ... κυλινδόμενον, of Ixion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.22}}; τροχοὺς μιμεῖσθαι to imitate **wheels,** of one who bends back so as to form a wheel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.22, 7.3: _metaphorically_ of fortune, πότμος ἐν.. θεοῦ τροχῷ κυκλεῖται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871; also μανίας τροχῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Pirith.Oxy.” 2078 “fragment” 1.14.}}
**2** **potter's wheel,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.600}}; τροχῷ ἐλαθεὶς [λύχνος] (compare τροχήλατος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 4; τροχοῦ ῥύμαισι τευκτὸν.. κύτος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420e.}}
**3** **wheel of a stage-machine,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 188}}; also of a water-**wheel,** ὁ τ. τῆς μηχανῆς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 1292.13 (1st c.AD)}}; τ. καὶ μηχανή {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PSI” 9.1072.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**4** **wheel of torture,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 21.9; ἐπὶ τοῦ τ. στρεβλοῦσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 875, “Lys.” 846, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.40; ἕλκεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 452; ἐπὶ τὸν τ. ἀναβῆναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.40; ἀναβιβάζειν τινὰ ἐπὶ τὸν τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.43; ἐν τῷ τ. ἐνδεδεμένον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.509c}}; τῷ τ. προσηλοῦν ['Ιξίονα] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]19e,compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 6.5.}}
**5** rotating **wheels** used in sieges as a defence against projectiles, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.45.}}
**II** child's **hoop,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.26.5, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.106.}}
**III** **round cake,** κηροῖο, στέατος τ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.173, 21.178}}; τ. ἡλίου the sun's **disk,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 17 (see. infr. B)}}; **coil** of a serpent, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 136.}}
**III.2** **large pill** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (τροχίσκος 2), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.65, “POxy.” 2144.25 (3rd c.AD).}}
**IV** θαλάττης γῆς τε τ. **circles** or **zones** of land and sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113d, compare 115c, 116a, 117c.f, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 39.}}
**V** **circuit of a wall** or **fortification,** Κυκλώπιος τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 227, cf. Scholia A 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681a (see. facsim. fol. 175v).}}
**VI** **ring playing on the bit** of a bridle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.6, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.184.}}
**VI.2** **ring for passing a rope through,** on board ship, {{Refs|[prev. work] 94.}}
**VII** **whirlwind,** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.76(77).18.}}
**VIII** **washpot** (?), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).671.}}
**IX** a fish or sea-monster (Latin _rota,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 9.8), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.20.}}
**X** _metaphorically_, ὁ τ. τῆς γενέσεως {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.Jam.3.6; ὁ τῆς εἱμαρμένης τε καὶ γενέσεως τ. 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 377.14.}}
**B** τρόχος, ὁ, **circular race,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.63, 3.68, “Insomn.” 89}}; μὴ πολλοὺς τ. ἁμιλλητῆρας ἡλίου not many racing **courses** of the sun, i.e. not many days (codices τροχούς **wheels**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1065; παῖδες ἐκ τρόχων πεπαυμένοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 46.}}
**B.2** **place for running, race-course,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1133 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II** an animal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Herodorus Historicus 58J. (1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.131 V. distinguished the two senses as above.)}}

5165:
** τρύβλιον**, τό,
**cup. bowl,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 650, “Av.” 77, +others; εἰρήνης ῥοφήσει τρύβλιον [prev. author] “Ach.” 278; μισθοῦ τ. ῥοφῆσαι [prev. author] “Eq.” 905}}: also in later Gr., {{LXX+NT|LXX.Num.7.13, +others, NT.Matt.26.23.}}
**II** in in Medicine texts prescriptions, a measure, = {ὀξύβαφον}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 1, “Mul.” 2.109, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 142, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.752.}}
**III** = {κεραμεοῦν ἄγγος ἐπιδεχόμενον ὡς εἴκοσι ἀρτάβας}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus Euerg. 9J. [Proparox. _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr. 1.357}}, and so in Ar.[same places], {{Refs|LXX [same places] (codex B), 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; τρυβλίον (assuming variant) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus [same places], 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.752, 753, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, “Geoponica” 7.36.1.]}}

5166:
** τρῠγάω**,
(τρύγη):
**I** with _accusative_ of the fruit gathered, **gather in** the fruit or crop, ἑτέρας [σταφυλὰς] τρυγόωσιν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.124, cf. NT.Luke.6.44}}; σῦκα, σῖτον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 812 (_passive_), 787 (_anapaest meter_): _metaphorically_, τρυγήσομεν αὐτήν (i.e. Εἰρήνην) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1338 (Lyric poetry); τ. παίδων ἄνθος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.256 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); ὄμφακας ἡλικίης “IG” 14.769 (from Naples)}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.199, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 925b15, “PCair.Zen.” 184.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; of honey, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.35}}; καθ᾽ ὥραν τετρυγημένοι (by death) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 5.}}
**I.2** _absolutely_, θερίζουσι καὶ σπείρουσι καὶ τρυγῶσι ταῖς γλώτταισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1698 (Lyric poetry), compare “Pax” 912, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844e, “PCair.Zen.” 300.17 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** with _accusative_ of that from which the fruit is gathered, **gather** or **reap off** the trees or ground, ὅτε τρυγόῳεν ἀλωήν (_Epic dialect_ _optative_ for τρυγῷεν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.566; οἱ δ᾽ ἐτρύγων οἴνας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 292; ἀμπέλους τρυγῶν “Comica Adespota” 437; τοὺς Ταντάλου κήπους [prev. work]530; τ. ἑαντήν (i.e. τὴν ἄμπελον) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.19.}}
**II.2** proverbial, ἐρήμας τρυγᾶν (i.e. ἀμπέλους) **strip** unwatched **vines,** of one who is bold where there is nothing to fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 886, “V.” 634, ubi see Scholia}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, with _accusative_ _person_, **strip** one, i. e. **rob** him, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 1.2}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **rob,** βίᾳ τρυγήσαντες τὸν περιστερεῶνα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1855.13 (1st c.BC).}}

5171:
** τρῠφάω**,
(τρυφή) **live softly, luxuriously, fare sumptuously,**, ἐν ἀγκάλαις μητρὸς τρυφῆσαι, of a child, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1376, compare “Ba.” 969; τ. ἐν ταῖς ἐσθῆσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.32; τ. καὶ μεγαλοπρεπῶς διαιτᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.11; λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος, παχύς, ἀργός.., εἰωθὼς τρυφᾶν Sosicrates Comicus 1, cf. NT.Jam.5.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.416, etc.; παῖσον, τρύφησον, ζῆσον· ἀποθανεῖν σε δεῖ “epigram Gr.” 362.5 (Cotiaeum, 2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**2** _participle_ τρυφῶν as _adjective_, **effeminate, luxurious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 48, etc.; τ. καὶ ἀμελής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 901a; τὸ τρυφῶν}}**effeminacy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1455 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of things, **dainty, delicate,** βασιλικὴ καὶ τρυφῶσα παιδεία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 695d; ἀσπίδα.. τρυφῶσαν 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 14, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.10 (troch.); ἄρτοι τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 5.}}
**II** **to be licentious, run riot, wax wanton,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 405, etc.}}; **to be extravagant,** opposed to γλίσχρως ζῆν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266b26.}}
**II.2** **revel** in, ἐν τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς τινος {{LXX|LXX.Sir.14.4}}; **delight** in, ἐν ἀγαθωσύνῃ σου{{LXX|LXX.Neh.9.25.}}
**III** **give oneself airs, be dainty, fastidious,** ἆρ᾽ οὐ τρυφῶμεν..., οἷσιν οὐκ ἀρκεῖ τάδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 214}}; τ. δ᾽ ὁ δαίμων **is fickle,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 552}}; ἐπειδή μοι δοκεῖς σὺ τρυφᾶν **to hang back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 11e, compare “La.” 179d, Alcaeus1.114a}} οἱ τρυφῶντες **spoiled pets,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 76b}}; ἐν ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις τ. καὶ κολακεύεσθαι, of the people, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.34; οὐκ ἀνεκτὸν εἶναι.. αἰχμάλωτον οὖσαν τρυφᾶν [prev. author] 19.197}}; τρυφῶσιν ἕτεροι πρὸς ἑτέρους, of philosophers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 221.14}}; followed by a modifying clause, νῦν δὲ τρυφᾷς διότι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327e; ἐν ταῖς.. ἑτέρων.. ἀτυχίαις τ. 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 12}}; later, τ. κατά τινος **make sport of**.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 12.2.}}

5172:
** τρῠφ-ή**, ἡ,
(θρύπτω) **softness, delicacy, daintiness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 892.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 900e, etc.}}; στολίδος κροκόεσσαν.. τρυφάν (sic to be read pro στολίδα.. τρυφᾶς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1491 (Lyric poetry)}}: _plural_, **luxuries, daintinesses,** τ. Τρωϊκαί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 1113}}; τρυφὰς τοιάσδε [τρυφᾶν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ba.” 970; αἱ ἄγαν τ. [prev. author] “fragment” 54.2; εἰς πλούτους ἀποβλέψαι καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122c, compare “Lg.” 637e.}}
**II** **luxuriousness, wantonness,** τῶν γυναικῶν ἡ τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 387}}; τ. καὶ ἀκολασία, τ. καὶ μαλθακία, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 492c, “R.” 590b; ἡ ἐν ἡμέρᾳ τ. NT.2Pet.2.13; ὑπερτεταμένη τ. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.54: personified, Τρυφῆς πρόσωπον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 973 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 230.3.}}
**III** **daintiness, fastidiousness,** ὑπὸ τρυφῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 818; ὕβρις ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶ καὶ πολλὴ τ. [prev. author] “Ra.” 21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295b17.}}

5174:
** Τρω-ϊάς**,
_contraction_ **Τρῳάς** (frequently written** Τρωάς**), άδος, _feminine_ of Τρώϊος, **Trojan,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.263; Τρωϊάδας γυναῖκας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.139, +others; Τρωϊάδες alone, 18.122, +others}}; Τρῶας καὶ Τρῳάδας Trojan men and **Trojan women,** {{Refs|22.105.}}
**II** γῆ Τρῳάς **the Troad,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 819, +others}}; ἡ Τρωάς alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.122.}}

5176:
** τρώγω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.90, etc.}}: _future_ τρώξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 806, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.8:}}_aorist 1_ ἔτρωξα (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 182, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 8, Timo 66.6:}}_aorist 2_ ἔτρᾰγον, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ τράγῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 67.5 (elsewhere only in _composition_ with ἐν- (which see), κατα-, παρα-):}} —_passive_, _perfect_ τέτρωγμαι (δια-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 371}}, (παρεν-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 15.8}}:—**gnaw, nibble, munch,** especially of herbivorous animals, as mules, τ. ἄγρωστιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.90}}; of swine, ἐρεβίνθους τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 801, compare 806}}; of cattle, τὸν θαλλόν, κόμαρον τ., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.45, 9.11}}; rarely of dogs, Εὐριπίδην ἔτρωγον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus 15.15}}; of human beings in disease, λίθους τε καὶ γῆν τρώγουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.31.}}
**II** of men, **eat** vegetables or fruit, τοὺς γενομένους [κυάμους] οὔτε τρώγουσι οὔτε ἕψοντες πατέονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; τὸ κάτω [τῆς βύβλου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|] [prev. work] 92; τὸν καρπὸν τοῦ λωτοῦ [prev. author] 4.177; τ. βότρυς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1077; βολβοὺς τρώγων, τυροὺς κάπτων 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 18.3 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of dessert, **eat fruits,** as figs, almonds, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1324, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 159 (see. τρωγάλια)}}; ἴτρια, μελίπηκτα, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 38.1, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 140.4}}; of small fish as hors-d'oeuvres, κἀν ποίᾳ πόλει τοσοῦτος [ὢν] τὸ μέγεθος ἰχθῦς τρώγετα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 23 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator: _absolutely_, πίνειν καὶ τ.}} drink and **eat dessert,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.197: Comedy texts _metaphorically_, γνώμας τ. Πανδελετείους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 924 (_anapaest meter_)}}:—_passive_, τρώγεται ἁπαλὰ ταῦτα καὶ αὖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92.}}
**III** later, simply **eat,** serving as _present_ to ἔφαγον instead of ἐσθίω, ὁ τρώγων μου τὴν σάρκα καὶ πίνων μου τὸ αἷμα {{LXX+NT|NT.John.6.54 (compare _aorist_ φάγητε.. πίητε [prev. work] 53); τρώγοντες καὶ πίνοντες NT.Matt.24.38; never in LXX (ὁ ἐσθίων ἄρτους μου LXX.Psa.40(41).10 becomes ὁ τρώγων μου τὸν ἄρτον}} when cited in {{NT|NT.John.13.18)}}; δύο τρώγομεν ἀδελφοί is uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.9; ἔδωκεν εὔζωμον νήστῃ τρώγειν “SIG” 1171.9 (Crete, perhaps 1st c.BC)}}; ἡ νὺξ τὴν ἡμέραν τ. (of a black man eating white bread) Diog.Cyn. cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5730 (4th-5th c.AD)}}; ψυχρὰ τρώγοντα κατακαίεσθαι {{Refs|“PMag.Lond.” 121.177; ἔμοιγε, ὅσσα παρ᾽ ἀνθρώποις, τρώγειν ἔθος Batrachomyomachia 34}}; this usage is mentioned by {{Refs 9th c.AD+|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 114, censured by 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}

5177:
** τυγχάνω**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 253, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.104, “O.” 2.47, etc.}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ τύγχανον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.231}}, (παρετ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.74}}: _future_ τεύξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.609, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.314, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 112, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.23}} (also as _future_ _middle_ of τεύχω): _aorist 2_ ἔτῠχον, _Epic dialect_ τύχον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.287, 587}}, etc.; _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ τύχωμι, -ῃσι, {{Refs|7.243, 11.116}}; later also τετύχῃσι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 577}}; late _Epic dialect_ _optative_ τετύχοιμι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.299}}: _Epic dialect_ also _aorist 1_ ἐτύχησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.581, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 15:}}_perfect_ τετύχηκα (_intransitive_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.88 (_participle_ τετυχηκώς}} variant{τετυχηώς}. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.748), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, (_transitive_) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.59}}; later also τέτευχα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.150 (codex S), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119a10, “PA” 647b15, frequently later, “PEnteux.” 6.7 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 123.30 (2nd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 98.10 (2nd c.BC), “Inscription Prien.” 108.287 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _perfect_ _infinitive_ τετεύχεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 398.5 (Cos, 3rd c.BC)}}; but _Ionic dialect_ _pluperfect_ ἐτετεύχεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14}}; τέτυχα variant in {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Heb.8.6, variant in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.5.4}}, (συν-) {{Refs|Aristeas 180}}, etc.; _participle_ τετυχώς {{Refs|“Jahresh.” 29 “Beibl.” 163 (Stara Zagora)}}:—_middle_, _aorist 1_ τεύξασθαι {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.15.7}}:— _passive_, _imperfect_ ἐτυγχάνετο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 39.3 (uncertain): elsewhere in compds}}_aorist 1_ ἐτεύχθην (ἐν-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.6.1:}}_perfect_ τέτευγμαι (ἐπι-) {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.53.2.}}
**A** **happen to be at** a place, εἴ πέρ τε τύχῃσι μάλα σχεδόν even if **she be** quite near, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.116}}; μὴ σύ γε κεῖθι τύχοις **may'st** thou not **be** there, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.106; πέτρη τετύχηκε διαμπερὲς ἀμφοτέρωθεν 10.88; πεδίοιο διαπρύσιον τετυχηκώς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.748}} (but in these last two places the meaning may be '**has been made**' (though not by human agency), compare [γαῖα] οὐδ᾽ εὐρεῖα τέτυκται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.243; γυναικὸς ἄρ᾽ ἀντὶ τέτυξο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.163, etc.}}; see ad near the end).
**2** of events, and things generally, **happen to** one, **befall** one, **come to** one's **lot,** with _dative_ _person_, οὔνεκά μοι τύχε πολλά because much **fell** to me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.684; καί μοι μάλα τύγχανε πολλά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.231; θέλοιμ᾽ ἂν ὡς πλείστοισι πημονὰς τυχεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 348, compare “Pers.” 706 (troch.); οἷ᾽ αὐτοῖς τύχοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 275; εἴ τι δεσπόταισι τυγχάνει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 138: _absolutely_, εἰ δ᾽ αὖθ᾽, ὃ μὴ γένοιτο, συμφορὰ τύχοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 5, compare “Ag.” 347, etc.; ἄριστα πρὸς τὸ τυγχάνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1290, compare “Ion” 1511.}}
**2.b** _aorist_ _participle_ ὁ τυχών, **the first one meets, any chance person,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 973, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 539d, etc.; οἱ τ.}}**everyday men, the vulgar,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.10, etc.; εἷς ἦν τῶν τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.21}}; οὐχ ὁ τ. ἀνήρ, of {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Moses Alchemista, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 9.9: so of things, τὸ τυχόν}} **any chance** result, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 46e; ὃν ἐξαλείφει πρόφασις ἡ τυχοῦσ᾽ ὅλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1041}}; οὐχ ὁ τ. λόγος no **common** discourse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 723e; σύνεσιν οὐ τὰν τυχοῦσαν 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra}}**Spir.Praef.;** οἱ τ. φόβοι **trifling** fears, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 37}}; καίπερ τὸ τ. καταβαλοῦσιν though they may have paid **a trifling sum,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.2.7:}} —in Mathematics texts, τυχὸν σημεῖον **any** point (at random), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.5, compare 6.9}}; ἄλλα, ἃ ἔτυχεν, ἰσάκις πολλαπλάσια **any** other equimultiples **taken at random,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.4.}}
**3** in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _aorist_ or _imperfect_, _impersonal_ (sometimes also _person_) in _relative_ clauses, as (when, where, etc.) **it (he, she,** etc.) **happened** (**may happen,** etc.), i. e. **any**how, at **any** time, place, etc., καὶ ἀρχομένοις καὶ μεσοῦσι καὶ ὅπως ἔτυχέ τῳ at the beginning, middle, or **any** other point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.20}}; ὡς ἔτυχε ζημιοῦσθαι to be penalized just **any**how, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.13}}; οὐχ ὡς ἔτυχεν **in** no **ordinary manner,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 79, “BMus.Inscription” 4.481*.340 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD)}}; τὴν μὲν δικαίαν, τὴν δ᾽ ὅπως ἐτύγχανεν just **any**how, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 929}}; ἀποτετμάσθω δύο τμάματα ὡς ἔτυχεν let two segments be cut off **at random,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 24; χώρᾳ γ᾽ ἐν ᾗ ἔτυχε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.3; ὅπου ἔτυχεν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.4.3; ὅπου ἂν τύχῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 242e}}; ὁπότε τύχοι **sometimes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89b}}; ὅταν τύχῃ **sometimes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1169 (Lyric poetry)}}; but, **at any odd time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142; ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν τ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.3}}; ἂν τύχῃ, εἰ τύχοι, **it may be,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 430e, “Hp.Mi.” 367a; τὸ δέ, εἰ ἔτυχεν, οὐχ οὕτως ἔχει [prev. author] “Cra.” 439c; εἰ οὕτως ἔτυχεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 8b12; τὸ ὅπῃ ἔτυχεν}}**mere chance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 28d}}: with attraction of the _relative_ _pronoun_, τὸ οἷς ἔτυχε προσκρούειν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 27; ὡμίλει ᾧ τύχοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.1.3; ὧν ἔτυχε πιμπλάμενος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 9}}; οὐδὲ γὰρ ὧν ἔτυχ᾽ ἦν they were not **just any** acts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.130.}}
**3.b** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἔτυχε ὄμβρον συνεργῆσαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 28, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.6}}; ἔτυχεν ὥστε.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.12.}}
**4** sometimes the Verb agrees in person and number with the subject of the principal clause, perhaps by assimilation, ἀπαίροντες ἀπὸ τῆς Πελοποννήσου ὁπόθεν τύχοιεν, for ὁπόθεν τύχοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26, compare 93, 5.56, 7.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179c}}; ὅ τι ἂν τύχωσι, τοῦτο λέγουσι they say **just anything,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 353a; ὅ τι ἂν τύχωσι, τοῦτο πράξουτιν [prev. author] “Cri.” 45d, compare “Grg.” 522c, “Smp.” 181b; ἀναφύονται ὁπόθεν ἂν τύχῃ ἕκαστος [prev. author] “Tht.” 180c; ὡς ἐτύγχανον ἕκαστοι, ηὐλίζοντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.17, compare 3.1.3; τάχ᾽ ἄν, εἰ τύχοιεν, σωφρονέστεροι γένοιντο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.16; δουλεύειν μᾶλλον ἢ μεθ᾽ ὁποτέρου ἂν τύχωσι τούτων ἐλευθέρους εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48}}; πρὸς ὀργὴν ἥν τινα τύχητε ἔστιν ὅτε σφαλέντες τὴν τοῦ πείσαντος μίαν γνώμην ζημιοῦτε yielding to **the impulse of the moment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.43; εἶτ᾽ οὐκ ἐλήρουν ὅ τι τύχοιμ᾽ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” 945}}: with attraction of the _relative_ _pronoun_, οὓς ἂν τύχῃς ἐπαινῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.206.}}
**5** _neuter_ _participle_ τυχόν, used _absolutely_ like{ἐξόν, παρόν}, etc., **since it so befell,** οὕτως τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 43.}}
**5.b** as _adverb_, **perchance, perhaps,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.171, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.140a, 150c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.221, 21.41, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 184, NT.1Cor.16.6; τ. ἴσως 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 277, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 287, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.58.9}}; τυχὸν μὲν.., τυχὸν δὲ.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.6, etc.}}
**A.II** joined with the _participle_ of another Verb to express a coincidence, τύχησε γὰρ ἐρχομένη νηῦς a ship **happened to be,** i. e. **was just then,** starting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.334; ξεῖνος ἐὼν ἐτύχησε παρ᾽ ἱπποδάμοισι Γερηνοῖς 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 15.3, compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.19, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.49; πρυτανεία ἣ ἂν τυγχάνῃ πρυτανεύουσα “IG” 12.63.27}}, compare 52; τὰ νοέων τυγχάνω what I **happen** to have, i.e. have **at this moment,** in my mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, compare 8.65, 68. ; ἐτετεύχεε ἐπισπόμενος [prev. author] 3.14}}; ὃ τυγχάνω μαθών which I have **just** learnt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 370}}; παρὼν ἐτύγχανον I was by **just then,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 748}}; τυγχάνει καθεύδων he is sleeping **just now,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 336 (troch.)}}; ἔτυχον στρατευόμενοι they were **just then** engaged in an expedition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.104}}; ἔτυχε κατὰ τοῦτο καιροῦ ἐλθών he came **just** at this point of time, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.2}}; ἥτις δέ τοι μάλιστα σωφρονεῖν δοκεῖ, αὕτη μέγιστα τυγχάνει λωβωμένη she **is just the one who**.., {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.109}}; but frequently τυγχάνω cannot be translated at all, especially in phrase τυγχάνω ὤν, which is simply ={εἰμί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 88, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313c, etc.}}
**II.2** the _participle_ ὤν is sometimes omitted, ὁ γὰρ μέγιστος τυγχάνει δορυξένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 46; εἴ σοι χαρτὰ τυγχάνει τάδε [prev. work] 1457}}; νῦν δ᾽ ἀγροῖσι τυγχάνει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 313; ἔνδον γὰρ ἄρτι τυγχάνει [prev. author] “Aj.” 9; εἴ τις εὔνους τυγχάνει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1141; εἰ σὺ τυγχάνεις ἐπιστήμων τούτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313e, compare “Grg.” 502b, “R.” 369b, +others: sometimes τυγχάνειν}} is used much like{εἶναι, Σωτὴρ γένοιτ᾽ ἂν Ζεὺς ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδος τυχών} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 520}}; οὐκ ἀποδάμου τυχόντος not **being** absent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.5 (compare τόσσαις); ποῦ χρὴ τηνικαῦτα τυγχάνει; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 730}}; τ. ἐν ἐμπύροις **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Andr.” 1113; frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, δύο μέρη τετύχηκεν ἐξ ὧν ἡ πόλις “Pol.” 1318a31, compare 1289b16, “Top.” 151b11}}; also in later Gr., τὰ ἑπτάμηνα γόνιμα τυγχάνειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.55, compare 69, +others; νέος πάνυ τυγχάνων “PLips.” 40 ii 7 (4th c.AD), etc.:— 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 244 rejects this usage in 2nd c.AD: Atticus Philosophus}}
**II.2.b** τυγχάνον, ={τὸ ἐκτὸς ὑποκείμενον}, the external **reality,** e. g. αὐτὸς ὁ Δίων as distinguished both from the word (φωνή) Δίων and its meaning, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.48.}}
**II.2.c** τὰ πράγματα τυγχάνοντα καλοῦσι (i.e. οἱ Στωϊκοί), τέλος γὰρ τὸ τυχεῖν τούτων,{{Refs|[prev. work]77.}}
**II.3** later with _infinitive_, τυγχάνομεν ἐπιδεδωκέναι we **happen** to have handed in.., we **have just** handed in.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 796.13 (2nd c.BC),compare “PSI” 10.1118.8 (1st c.AD), 1.39.4 (2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus et 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.8.21, 25, 44.23.21, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).394.}}
**B** **gain one's end** or **purpose, succeed,** οὐκ ἐτύχησεν ἑλίξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.466; εἰ τύχῃ τις ἔρδων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.11, compare 55}}; τὸ τυχεῖν, ={νίκη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 2.51; πείθειν.. τυγχάνειν θ᾽ ἅμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 819; εἰ τύχοιμεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.63}}; τυχόντες **if successful,** opposed to σφαλέντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.39, compare 82, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.62; τυγχάνουσι καὶ ἀποτυγχάνουσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450a3; ὀρθῶς πράττειν καὶ τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 280a}}; **gain one's request,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.213 (so τυχόντα γνώμης in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42)}}; in speaking, **to be right,** τί νιν καλοῦσα.. τύχοιμ᾽ ἄ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1233, compare “Ch.” 14, 317 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 223, “OC” 1580; Δίκαν νιν προσαγορεύομεν τυχόντες καλῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 950 (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, _impersonal_, αὐτῷ πρὸς τὸ ἔργον οὐδὲν ἐτυγχάνετο nothing **went right,** uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 39.3:}} —in _participle_ τυχήσας or τυχών, combined with νύξε, βάλε, οὖτα, etc., pierce, wound, etc., **successfully,** so that the whole phrase means **hit,** ἔγχεϊ νύξε κατὰ κληῗδα τυχήσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.579, compare 858, 12.394}}; βάλε δουρὶ κατὰ ζωστῆρα τυχήσας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 189; ὑπὸ στέρνοιο τυχήσας βεβλήκει 4.106, compare 5.98, 582, 13.371, 397, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.452, +others; also conversely, θηρητὴρ ἐτύχησε βαλών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.581; βαλὼν τύχω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35}}; also apart from such combinations, **hit,** with _genitive_, προβιβάντος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.609; μηρίνθοιο 23.857; τ. τοῦ σκοποῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717b, compare “R.” 523b, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.19, “Ap.” 1: with double _genitive_, εἰ.. τοῦ παιδὸς.. τύχω μέσης τῆς καρδίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35: _absolutely_, ἤμβροτες οὐδ᾽ ἔτυχες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.287; αἰ κε τύχωμι 7.243, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.7.}}
**B.II** **hit upon, light upon:**
**B.II.1** **meet, fall in with** persons, Αακεδαίμονι.. τυχήσας **having met** [him] in Lacedaemon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.13: with _genitive_, θρηνητοῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1075; τριακτῆρος [prev. work] 172 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀγαθῶν ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.5; γυναικῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.7}}: with a predicate added, μή τευ μελαμπύγου τύχῃς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 110; προφρόνων Μοισᾶν τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).43(61); θεῶν ἀμεινόνων τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 351; ἐμοῦ.. οἰκητοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1450, compare 677; ἡμῶν τ. οἵων σε χρή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1300, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.23; ἐρωτᾶτε αὐτοὺς ὁποίων τινῶν ἡμῶν ἔτυχον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.15; τοῦ δαίμονος.. κακοδαίμονος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 112.}}
**B.II.2** **light on** a thing, τύχε γάρ ἀμάθοιο βαθείης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.587}}; **attain, obtain** a thing, with _genitive_, πομπῆς καὶ νόστοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.290; αἰδοῦς 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 253, compare 256}}; [οἴκτου] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 241; ξυγγνώμης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15; τῆς ἀξίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1223}}; of **meeting with** misfortunes, βίης τυχεῖν **meet with, suffer** violence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.108}}; τραυμάτων, κακῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 866, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280}}; δίκης, κρίσεως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472d, “Phdr.” 249a, compare “Lg.” 869b}}: _absolutely_, **have the lot** or **fate,** ἄλλος μὲν ἀποφθίσθω ἄλλος δὲ βιώτω, ὅς κε τύχῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.430; τὴν παρὰ Δαρείου αἰτήσας ἔτυχε μισθόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23 (where τὴν is governed by αἰτήσας).}}
**B.II.2.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also with _accusative_ of _neuter_ _adjective_ or _pronoun_, τὰ πρόσφορα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 711, compare “Eu.” 30, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1106, “Ph.” 509 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 758, “Hec.” 51}}: later the _accusative_ is used more freely, τ. ἐπίστασιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5235.15 (1st c.AD)}}; ὑπογραφήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 615.23 (2nd c.AD)}}; βοήθειαν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGoodsp.Cair.” 15.14 (4th c.AD)}}; εὐκαιρίαν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PSI” 9.1082.5 (4th c.AD)}}; τὰ γυναίκια δεσμὸν οὐδένα βούλεται τυγχάνειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.70b.}}
**B.II.2.c** after either case a _genitive_ _person_ may be added, **obtain** a thing **from** a person, ὧν δέ σου τυχεῖν ἐφίεμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1315; σου τοῦτο τ. [prev. author] “OC” 1168}}; or the _person_ may be added with a _preposition_, τ. ἐπαίνου ἔκ τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 665; παρὰ σεῖο τ. φιλότητος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.158; τιμίαν ἕδραν παρ᾽ ἀνδρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 856 (uncertain); αἰδοῦς ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.10, compare “Mem.” 4.8.10, etc.: _absolutely_, χρὴ πρὸς μακάρων τυγχάνοντ᾽ εὖ πασχέμεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.104.}}
**B.II.2.d** with _infinitive_, οἶμαί σου τεύξεσθαι μεθεῖναί με {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 50d; ἐὰν ψαῦσαι τοῦ νεκροῦ τύχωμεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 33}}; οὐ τυχὼν ἐπιδείξειν (={ἐπιδεῖξαι}) not **having succeeded** in proving, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.153 (3rd c.BC). (Τυ-γ-χ-άνω, with ἐτύχησα, τετύχηκα, is formed from the}}_aorist_ τυχ-εῖν, which was originally the _aorist_ _passive_ (with _active_ form) of τεύχω 'make'; ἔτυχε = **factum est,** as ἔτραφον = **I was nourished** (see. τρέφω); senses {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.1-3 are the oldest and are parallel to τεύχω 11 (especially _passive_)}}; many of the forms belong equally to both verbs; τιτύσκομαι like wise belongs to both verbs; τ(ε)υχ- from *θ(ε)υχ-, compare ἀποθύσκειν, ἐνθύσκει, συνθύξω, and perhaps [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **taugen** 'to be capable, useful', Engl. **dow, doughty.**)

5178:
** τυμπᾰν-ίζω**,
**beat a drum,** as was done in the worship of Cybele, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 77, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.60a}}:—_passive_, τυμπανίζεσθαι κατὰ τὰς ἐξόδους **march out to the sound of drums,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.58.}}
**2** τ. ἐπὶ ταῖς θύραις **drum with the hand** on them, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21.13.}}
**II** perhaps ={ἀποτυμπανίζω} (which is variant), {{NT|NT.Heb.11.35 (_passive_)}}; coupled with ἀνασκολοπίζεσθαι in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 19; compare τύμπανον 11.}}
**III** of orators, 'beat the big drum', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.5.}}

5179:
** τύπ-ος** [ῠ], ὁ,
(τύπτω) **blow,** τ. ἀντίτυπος Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67}}; **beat** of horses' hoofs, variant for{κτὺπος} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.12; αἰθερίου πατάγοιο τ. βρονταῖον ἀκούων 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 20.351; so perhaps νάβλα τ. 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 16.}}
**II** **the effect of a blow** or **of pressure**:
**II.1** **impression** of a seal, τύποι σφενδόνης χρυσηλάτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 862, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 192a, 194b, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.23, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 21; τ. ἐνσημήνασθαί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 377b}}; **stamp** on a coin, τὰ ἀκριβῆ τὸν τ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 10, compare 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Mens.” 60, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see entry {Κυζικηνοι στατῆρες}; on a branding-iron, ὄ τ. τοῦ καυτῆρος ἔστω ἀλώπηξ ἢ πίθηκος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 46}}: generally, **print, impression,** χύτρας τύπον ἀρθείσης ἐν σποδῷ μὴ ἀπολιπεῖν, ἀλλὰ συγχεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.727c, compare 982b, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21. κθ᾽, “Geoponica” 2.20.1}}; στίβου γ᾽ οὐδεὶς τ. no foot**print,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 29 (variant{κτύπος})}}]; ὡς ἡδὺς ἐν πόρπακι σὸς (i.e. τοῦ βραχίονος) κεῖται τύπος thy **imprint,** (O arm), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1196 (σῷ conjecture Dobree)}}; τ. ὀδόντων **imprint** of teeth, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.57.5 (Paul. Sil.)}}; **print,** βάλω τὸν δάκτυλόν μου εἰς τὸν τ. τῶν ἥλων {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.20.25; οἱ τ. τῶν πληγῶν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.585c.}}
**II.1.b** **impressions** supposed by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus and 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus}} to be made on the air by things seen, and to travel through space, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 52, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.9U., “Nat.” 2.6, +others; ὁ θεὸς.. πνεῦμα ἐνεκέρασεν [τοῖς ὀφθαλμοῖς] οὕτως ἰσχυρὸν καὶ φιλότεχνον ὥστε ἀναμάσσεσθαι τοὺς τ. τῶν ὁρωμένων 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.23.3.}}
**II.2** **hollow mould** or **matrix,** καθάπερ ἐν τύπῳ τὰ σχήματα πλασθῆναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 676b9, compare “Pr.” 892b2}}; used by κοροπλάθοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 60.9, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 1.335, 394 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see at {χοάνη}; by fruit-growers, to shape the fruit while growing, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 10.9.3}}; **die** used in striking coins, _metaphorically_, Κύπριος χαρακτήρ τ᾽ ἐν γυναικείοις τύποις εἰκὼς πέπληκται τεκτόνων πρὸς ἀρσένων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 282.}}
**II.3** **engraved mark, engraving,** δέλτον χαλκῆν τύπους ἔχουσαν ἀρχαίων γραμμάτων **engravings** of letters, i. e. engraved letters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 275a; τὰ γεγραμμένα τύποις [prev. author] “Ep.” 343a}}; τὸ μέτρον τοῦ ποδὸς ὑποτέτακται τούτοις τοῖς τ. the length of the foot is subjoined in this **engraving,** {{Refs|Rev.Bibl. 35.285 (from Jerusalem).}}
**II.4** **the depression between the underlip and chin,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.90.}}
**II.5** **pip** on dice, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.95.}}
**III** **cast** or **replica** made in a mould, τ. κατάμακτος {{Refs|“IG” 22.1534.87; τ. ἔγμακτος [prev. work]64.}}
**IV** **figure worked in relief,** whether made by moulding, modelling, or sculpture, αἱμασιὴ ἐγγεγλυμμένη τύποισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.138, compare 106, 136, 148, 153; θεοῦ τ. μὴ ἐπίγλυφε δακτυλίῳ 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.κγ}}; σιδηρονώτοις ἀσπίδος τύποις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1130; χρυσοκόλλητοι τ. [prev. author] “Rh.” 305; τ. ἀργυροῦς “IG” 22.1533.30, 11(2).161 B 77, compare 115 (Delos, 3rd c.BC); τύπους ἐργάσασθαι καὶ παρέχειν [prev. work]42(1).102.36 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; **tablet bearing a relief,** καθελέσθαι τοὺς τ. καὶ εἴ τι ἄλλο ἐστὶν ἀργυροῦν ἢ χρυσοῦν {{Refs|[prev. work] 22.839.30, compare 56, +others}}; τ. Ἔρωτα ἔχων ἐπειργασμένον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.23.5; τῶν τ᾽ ἄλλων ὧν τύπος εἰκόν᾽ ἔχει “IG” 2.2378, compare 22.2021.8, 3.1330.5; ἐνταῦθά εἰσιν ἐπὶ τύπου γυναικῶν εἰκόνες 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.11.3}}; πεποιημένα ἐν τύπῳ in **relief,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.19.17}}; **typos scalpsit,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 35.128}}; **impressā argillā typum fecit,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 151}}; πρὸς Ναυσίαν περὶ τοῦ τ., title of speech by Lysias, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see entry {λιθουργικ; Γάλλοι.. ἔχοντες προστηθίδια καὶ τύπους} {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.37.6, compare 21.6.7.}}
**V** **carved figure, image,** ποιεῦνται ξύλινον τ. ἀνθρωποειδέα, ποιησάμενοι δὲ ἐσεργνῦσι τὸν νεκρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86; τ. ποιησάμενος λίθινον ἔστησε· ζῷον δέ οἱ ἐνῆν ἀνὴρ ἱππεύς [prev. author] 3.88}}; χρυσέων ξοάνων τύποι, _periphrastic_ for χρύσεα ξόανα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1074 (Lyric poetry)}}; γραφαῖς καὶ τ. paintings and **statues,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.10.12}}; γραπτοὶ τ. probably painted pediment-**figures,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 764, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.74, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.730 (4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus)}}; **idol, graven image,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Amos.5.26, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.19.10.}}
**V.2** **exact replica, image,** as children are called the τύποι of their parents, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.45}}; τ. λογίου Ἑρμοῦ, of Demosthenes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.307 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**VI** **form, shape,** οὐλῆς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 721b32; σώματος [prev. author] “Phgn.” 806a32; προσώπου [prev. author] “Mir.” 832b15}}; ἀγγείου Crates Grammars cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.495b; τὸν ἄρτον ἔχειν ἴδιον τ. “OGI” 56.73 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; οἱ τ. τῶν γραμμάτων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 52; ὁ τ. τῶν χαρακτήρων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.577f; τοὺς τ. τῶν συλλαμβανομένων 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.39}}; Ἱππομέδοντος σχῆμα καὶ μέγας τ., _periphrastic_ for H. himself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 488; Γοργείοισιν εἰκάσω τ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 49}}; ὄμφακος τ., ={ὄμφαξ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 255.5; βραχιόνων ἡβητὴς τ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 858; κάλλος ἔχουσα τύποισι}}**features,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.2135 (from Rome), compare 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus Tyr. 31.3, 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus 1.4.}}
**VI.2** **thing having a shape,** οὐλοφυεῖς.. τ. χθονὸς ἐξανέτελλον undifferentiated **forms** rose from the earth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 62.4}}; τ. τις πορφυροῦς κατὰ χρόαν, τῷ σχήματι ἐμφερὴς κιβωρίου θύλακι (_i.e. _ the placenta) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.57.}}
**VI.3** **form of expression, style,** ὁ πραγματικὸς τ. [τοῦ Ξενοφῶντος] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 4; ὁ τ. τῆς γραφῆς 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 19; ὁ τ. ὁ πολιτικός 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.11; οὐδ᾽ ἀληθινοῦ τύπου μέτεστι τῷ ἀνδρί [prev. passage]; ὁ διὰ τῶν συμβόλων προτρεπτικὸς τ. 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21; ὁ αἰνιγματώδης τ. [prev. author] “VP” 23.103.}}
**VI.4** Grammars, **mode of formation, form,** τ. πατρωνυμικῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.29; τ. παθητικός 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 278.25.}}
**VII** **archetype, pattern, model,** capable of exact repetition in numerous instances, αὑτὸν ἐκμάττειν.. εἰς τοὺς τῶν κακιόνων τ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396e; οἰκισταῖς (i.e. πόλεως) τοὺς μὲν τ. προσήκει εἰδέναι, ἐν οἷς δεῖ μυθολογεῖν τοὺς ποιητάς.., οὐ μὴν αὐτοῖς γε ποιητέον μύθους·.. οἱ τ. περὶ θεολογίας τίνες ἂν εἶε; [prev. work] 379a, compare 380c.}}
**VII.2** **character recognizable in a number of instances, general character, type,** πάντα ὅσα τοῦ τ. τούτου {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 171e; τοῦ αὐτοῦ μετέχοντα τύπου [prev. author] “R.” 402d; τοῦτον τὸν τ. ἔχοντα [prev. author] “Phlb.” 51d.}}
**VII.3** **type** or **form of disease** (especially fever) with reference to the order and spacing of its attacks and intervals, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.463, compare 475,490,512.}}
**VIII** **general impression, vague indication,** γίνεται ἀμυδρὸς ὁ τ. τῆς ῥάχεως (in the foetus) {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 175}}; τ. ἀμυδροί, opposed to ἀκριβὲς εἶδος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.5}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ τ. ἐνῇ τοῦ πράγματος as long as there is an **approximate indication** of the thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 432e}}; of the **general type** or **schema** corresponding with a name, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 255.}}
**VIII.2** **outline, sketch, general idea,** ὅσον τοὺς τ. ὑφηγεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 403e; περιγραφὴ καὶ τύποι [prev. author] “Lg.” 876e; ἔχεις τὸν τ. ὧν λέγω [prev. author] “R.” 491c; τοὺς τ. μόνον εἰπόντες περὶ αὐτῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341b31; ἐξηγεῖσθαι τύποις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 816c; ὁ τ. τῆς φιλοσοφίας τοιοῦτός τίς ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.186, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.166 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; ὁ τ. τῆς ὅλης πραγματείας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.3U.; _plural_, [prev. work] p.4 U.; δέονται.. ὑγρᾶς διαίτης, ἧς τὸν τ. ἀρτίως ὑπέγραψα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.397}}; τύπῳ, ἐν τύπῳ, **in outline, in general,** ὡς ἐν τύπῳ, μὴ δι᾽ ἀκριβείας, εἰρῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414a}}; ἵνα τύπῳ λάβωμεν αὐτάς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 559a; ἐν ἑνὶ περιλαβόντα εἰπεῖν αὐτὰ οἷόν τινι τύπῳ [prev. author] “Lg.” 718c; τύπῳ, καὶ οὐκ ἀκριβῶς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a1; παχυλῶς καὶ τ. ἐνδείκνυσθαι [prev. work] 1094b20}}; τ. καὶ ἐπὶ κεφαλαίου λέγομεν{{Refs|[prev. work] 1107b14; ὡς ἐν τ. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1323a10}}; ὅσον τύπῳ **in outline** only, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Top.” 101a22; ὡς τύπῳ λαβεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 1.1.}}
**VIII.3** **outline,** ταῦτα ὅσα εἴρηται καθάπερ ἐν γραφαῖς ἀχρόοις γραμμῇ μόνῃ τύποι ἀνδρῶν εἰκασμένοι εἰσί {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus 2.61.}}
**IX** **prescribed form, model to be imitated,** ἢν ἁμάρτωσι τοῦ πατρικοῦ τ. τοῦ ἐπιμελέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 228; οὗτος.. εἷς ἂν εἴη τῶν περὶ θεοὺς νόμων καὶ τύπων, ἐν ᾧ δεήσει τοὺς λέγοντας λέγειν καὶ τοὺς ποιοῦντας ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380c, compare 383c; ἐν τοῖς τ. οἷς ἐνομοθετησάμεθα [prev. work] 398b}}; εἰς ἀρχήν τε καὶ τ. τινὰ τῆς δικαιοσύνης{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 443c; τ. εὐσεβείας.. παισὶν.. ἐκτέθεικα “OGI” 383.212 (Nemrud Dagh, 1st c.BC)}}; ὥστε γενέσθαι ὑμᾶς τύπον πᾶσι τοῖς πιστεύουσιν ἐν τῇ Μακεδονίᾳ {{LXX+NT|NT.1Thes.1.7; κατὰ τὸν τ. τὸν δεδειγμένον σοι LXX.Exo.25.39(40), cf. NT.Act.7.44.}}
**IX.2** **general instruction,** δόντες τοὺς τ. τούτους ὑπὲρ τῆς ὅλης διοικήσεως, ἐξέπεμπον τοὺς δέκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.24.9}}; **general principle** in law, τ. ἐστὶν καθ᾽ ὃν ἔκρεινα πολλάκις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 75.8 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IX.2.b** **rule of life, religion,** ἐξεταστέον ποταπῷ χρῆται τύπῳ ὁ νοσῶν (e. g. whether Jewish or Egyptian) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “fragment” 33.}}
**IX.3** **rough draft** of a book, βιβλίον γεγραμμενον ἐν τύποις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).875, compare 15.587,624, Anonymus cited in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. “Bibliotheca - Patrologia Graeca” p.491 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; **draft** of an official letter, τύπον ποιεῖ he **drafted** a letter, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 14.135 (2nd c.BC)}}; τ. χειρογραφίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PMich.Teb.” 123r ii 38 (1st c.AD)}}; τ. ἐπιστολικοί **models** of letters, {{Refs|“Characteres Epistolici” at Title Page}}
**IX.4** **form** of a document, ἔστιν δὲ ὁ τ. τῆς εἰθισμένης διαγραφῆς ὁ ὑποκείμενος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 9v. 3 (3rd c.BC)}}; σωματισθῆναι.. τύπῳ τῷδε· τί ἑκάστῳ ὑπάρχει κτλ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1460.12 (3rd c.AD)}}; κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν τ. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 279.16 (6th c.AD).}}
**IX.5** **text** of a document, ὁ μὲν τῆς ἐπιστολῆς τ. οὕτως ἐγέγραπτο {{LXX+NT|LXX.3Mac.3.30, cf. Aristeas 34, NT.Act.23.25, probably conjecture in LXX.1Mac.15.2.}}
**IX.6** **written decision,** θεῖος τ. an imperial **rescript,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.2.20, +others, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 113 at Title Page, compare “PMasp.” 32.41 (6th c.AD)}}; αἰτῆσαι θεῖον καὶ πραγματικὸν τ. Mitteis {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Chr.” 319.47 (6th c.AD)}}; given by a bishop, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7449.14 (5th c.AD)}}; by the ἔκδικος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PSI” 9.1075.11 (5th c.AD)}}; by others, χρὴ.. δοῦναι τ. εἰς τὴν συγχώρησιν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1911.145 (6th c.AD)}}: in _plural_, of the **acta** of a πάγαρχος, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work] 1829.2, 12 (6th c.AD).}}
**X** as law-term, **summons, writ,** οἱ τ. γράμμα εἰσὶν ἀγορᾶς, ἐρήμην ἐπαγγέλλον τῷ οὐκ ἀποδιδόντι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.25.9; δίκης λῆξις εἴη ἂν ὁ νῦν καλούμενος τ. 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.29.}}

5180:
** τύπτω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.561, etc.}}: _future_ τύψω {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 44.160, 4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor “Facet.” 200:}}_aorist 1_ ἔτυψα, _Epic dialect_ τύψα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.529, +others, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 43, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64}}, but rare in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 156 (Lyric poetry), [5th c.BC: Lysias Orator] “fragment” 20 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}: _Attic dialect_ _future_ τυπτήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1443, “Pl.” 21. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.204:}}_aorist 1_ ἐτύπτησα first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274b20 (as variant), then 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.8, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 66, 4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor “Facet.” 86}}: _aorist 2_ ἔτῠπον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 766 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ τετυπόντες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 61 (perhaps _perfect_ τετύποντες):}}_perfect_ τέτῠφα only in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Theodosius Alexandrinus Grammaticus “Canones - Grammatici Graeci, pars” p.47 H.; τετύπτηκα 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.129, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.10.3}}:— _middle_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.61, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 3, etc., (κατ-) 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 62}}: _aorist 1_ ἐτυψάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 14, (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40:}}_future_ (in _passive_ sense) τυπτήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1379}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ ἐτύφθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 26, “Geoponica” 18.17.7, 4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor “Facet.” 138, 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 2.68; ἐτυπτήθην 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.323:}}_aorist 2_ ἐτύπην [ῠ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.191, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.53, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 363, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1194 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.57}}: _perfect_ τέτυμμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.782, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 889 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 509 (Lyric poetry)}}, _infinitive_ τετύφθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64; τετύπτημαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 16, 4th c.BC: Arg. Demosthenes Orator 54}}:—In _Attic dialect_ and LXX the _future_ and _aorist_ are supplied by πατάσσω, e.g. τύπτει.. καὶ καταβάλλει πατάξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.71}}; later sometimes by παίω, e.g. ὁ δὲ παίσας ἐπερωτᾷ ποτέρᾳ τετύπτηκεν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.129}}; the _perfect_ by πλήσσ; the _passive_ partly (especially in _perfect_ and _aorist_) by πλήσσω: a complete paradigm of this verb is given by {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Theodosius Alexandrinus Grammaticus “Canones - Grammatici Graeci, pars” p.43 H., +others}}:—**beat, strike, smite,** τύπτουσιν ῥοπάλοισι (i.e. τὸν ὄνον) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.561; ἀμφὶ δέ μιν σφυρὰ τύπτε καὶ αὐχένα δέρμα 6.117; ἴχνια τύπτε πόδεσσι πάρος κόνιν ἀμφιχυθῆναι 23.764; χθόνα τύπτε μετώπῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.86; ἅλα τύπτον ἐρετμοῖς 4.580, 9.104, +others; but in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly with weapons of war, [ξίφει], δουρί, ἄορι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.531, 13.529, 20.378; ἐγχείῃσιν 13.782 (_passive_)}}; φασγάνῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.98; σκήπτρῳ τυπεὶς ἐκ τῆσδε χειρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 811}}; μάστιγι Legal cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.139 (_passive_): with _accusative_ _cognate_, τ. τινὰ σχεδίην (i.e. πληγήν) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.830; πληγὰς τ. τινά 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.1, see below 111.2: the part struck is sometimes in _accusative_, γαστέρα γάρ μιν τύψε παρ᾽ ὀμφαλόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.180, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.26, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1150, etc.}}: with a _preposition_, Φόρκυνα.. κατὰ γαστέρα τύψε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.313}}; ἐγκύμονά τις ἔτυψε κατὰ γαστρός [{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator] [same place]; τ. τινὰ εἰς τὸν ὦμον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.5; ἐπὶ κόρρης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527a; ἐπὶ τὴν σιαγόνα NT.Luke.6.29}}; τ. χαλκώματα **beat** pots and pans (to make a noise), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.29}}: _absolutely_, **strike,** τύπτε δ᾽ ἐπιστροφάδην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.20, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.308, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 610; τ. καὶ πνίγων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.6}}; Ζέφυρος λαίλαπι τύπτων **beating** with fury, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.306, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.14 (assuming variant).}}
**2** even of missiles, ἐκ χειρὸς τοῖς λίθοις τύπτοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.53.4; whereas 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus opposes τύπτειν to βάλλειν, δουρὶ τυπεὶς ἢ βλήμενος ἰῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.191 = 206, compare 15.495, +others}}
**3** later, **sting,** ὄφις υ᾽ ἔτυψε μικρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anacreontea” 33.10; ὑπὸ σφηκῶν τύπτεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.12, compare “Geoponica” [same place]; πόδα κάκτος τ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.4; οἱ βασιλεῖς [μελιττῶν].. οὐ τύπτουσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 553b6.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, τὸν δ᾽ ἄχος ὀξὺ κατὰ φρένα τύψε βαθεῖαν sharp grief **smote** him to the heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.125; Καμβύσεα ἔτυψε ἡ ἀληθείη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64; ἔτυπεν ὀδύνα με πλευμόνων ἔσω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 766 (Lyric poetry); ξυμφορᾷ τετυμμένος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 509 (Lyric poetry); ἀνίαισι τυπείς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.53}}; τύπτειν τὴν συνείδησίν τινος ἀσθενοῦσαν **wound** his conscience, {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.12}}; of divine punishment, ἐγώ εἰμι Κύριος ὁ τύπτων {{LXX+NT|LXX.Eze.7.6(9); τύπτειν σε μέλλει ὁ θεός NT.Act.23.3.}}
**5** **strike** a coin, ἐξ ἀργύρου τυπτόμενον νόμισμα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Mens.” 60.}}
**II** _middle_ τύπτομαι, **beat, strike oneself,** especially, like{κόπτομαι}, **beat one's breast** for grief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.61}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **mourn for** a person, {{Refs|[prev. work] 42, 132.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be beaten, struck,** or **wounded,** δουρὶ τυπείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.191}}; ὑπὸ δουρί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 433; δορὸς ὕπο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1194 (Lyric poetry); κράτων τυπτομένων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.309.}}
**III.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **receive** blows or wounds, ἕλκεα.. ὅσσ᾽ ἐτύπη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.421}}; τύπτομαι πολλάς (i.e. πληγάς) **I get** many **blows,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 972 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Pax” 644 (troch.), “Ra.” 636, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.139: with _dative_, καιρίῃ (i.e. πληγῇ) τετύφθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64; see above 1.1.}}

5181:
** τύραννος** [ῠ], ὁ,
also ἡ (see. below 1.2), **an absolute ruler,** unlimited by law or constitution, first in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Martem” 5 (unless the hymn is late)}}, where it is used of a god, Ἆρες,.. ἀντιβίοισι τύρανν; so ὁ τῶν θεῶν τ., of Zeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 736, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 564 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὦ τύραννε τᾶς ἐμᾶς φρενός, i.e. Apollo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 217 (Lyric poetry); σὺ δ᾽ ὦ τύραννε θεῶν τε κἀνθρώπων Ἔρως 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 136}}; Μὴν Τύραννος, a Phrygian deity worshipped in Attica, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 22.1366.2(1st c.AD), +others}}; οὔ, τὴν τ. (perhaps Hera), in an oath, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.77}}: first used of **monarchs** in the time of {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (compare τυραννίς 1) _accusative_ to Hippias 9 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; Φίττακον ἐστάσαντο τ. 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 37 A; ἢν μή τις ἢ τ. ἢ σκηπτοῦχος ᾖ 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.69; λαγέτας τ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.85}}; interchangeable with βασιλεύς in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.4 (compare 1), 35 (compare 36)}}; later, **chief, princeling**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 654.8 (Egypt, 1st c.BC)}}; τ. ἴδιοι καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ἐμτπόριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 14: with _genitive_, Κροῖσος.. τ. ἐθνέων τῶν ἐντὸς Ἅλυος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6}}; Κλεισθένης ὁ Σικυῶνος τ., Ἱστιαῖος ὁ Μιλήτου τ., etc., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.67, 7.10.γ, etc.}}; ὁ τῶν Κυπρίων τ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.39}}; οἱ τ., of the Sicilian **tyrants,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.14}}; of the Pisistratidae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.33, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 13.5, “Pol.” 1275b36, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182c; τὸν τ. κτανέτην “Scol.” 9.3}}; οἱ τ. the **monarchical party,** προδιδοὺς τοῖς τ. τὴμ πόλιν τὴν Ἐρυθραίων {{Refs|“IG” 12.10.32}}: frequently in a bad sense, δημοφάγος τ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1181, compare 823, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510b, “Plt.” 301c, “R.” 569b, etc.; ὕβρις φυτεύει τύραννον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 873 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** in a wider sense, of **members of the ruler's family,** οἱ τ. 'the royal house', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 316, compare “OC” 851, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.2: ἡ τύραννος}} is used both of **the queen** herself and **the king's daughter, princess,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 809, “Med.” 42, 877, 1356, compare below 11}}; πρέπει γὰρ ὡς τ. εἰσορᾶν, of Clytemnestra, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 664; αὐτὴ.. τ. ἦ Φρυγῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 204.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ἵνα Δίκη τ. ᾖ that Justice may be **supreme,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.6D.; Ερως τ. ἀνδρῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 538 (Lyric poetry); Πειθὼ τὴν τ. ἀνθρώποις μόνην [prev. author] “Hec.” 816.}}
**4** **golden-crested wren, Regulus cristatus,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592b23; compare τροχίλος 1.2.}}
**II** τύραννος, ον, as _adjective_, **kingly, royal,** τύραννα σκῆπτρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 761; τ. σχῆμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1169}}; τύραννα δρᾶν to act **as a king,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 588; ἡ τύραννος κόρη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1125}}; τύραννον δῶμα **the king's** palace, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 843 (Lyric poetry), etc.; τ. ἑστία [prev. author] “Andr.” 3}}; τ. δόμος the **royal** house, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 478, etc.}}; ἐς τύρανν᾽ ἐγημάμην into **the royal house,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 474.}}
**II.2** **imperious, despotic,** τ. πόλις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, 124; αἱ τ. φύσεις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ner.” 2. (Loan-word, probably from Phrygian or Lydian.)}}

5182:
** τυρβ-άζω**,
**trouble, stir up,** τὸν πηλὸν ὥσπερ ἀτταγᾶς τυρβάσεις βαδίζων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 257; τυφλὸς.. Ἄρης συὸς προσώπῳ πάντα τυρβάζει κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 838:}} —_passive_, πολὺς δὲ πηλὸς ἐκ πίθων τυρβάζεται **bursts in turbid stream** from.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 783: with _dative_ _person_}}**jostle against**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1007 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τ. περὶ πολλά (variant for{θορυβάζῃ}) **to be troubled** about.., {{NT|NT.Luke.10.41.}}
**II** **revel, enjoy oneself,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 25.6.}}

5185:
** τυφλός**, ή, όν,
**blind,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.139, compare “hymnus ad Apollinem” 172}}, frequently in other writers; τυφλὸς ἐκ δεδορκότος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 454}}; τ. Ἄρης, Πλοῦτος, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 838, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.19}}; τ. ὄψις, ὀφθαλμοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 697, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518c, etc.: with _genitive_, τ. τινός}} **blind to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 12}}; but τ. τῆς προνοίας **lacking** vision of the future, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.975c}}; τὰ τ. τοῦ σώματος, i. e. one's **back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.45}}; καὶ τυφλῷ γε δῆλον even a **blind** man can see that, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 55o d; for 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 6, see at {κωφός} II.2.}}]
**2** of the limbs of the blind, τ. πούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1050, “Ph.” 834, etc. (compare τυφλόπους); χείρ [prev. work] 1699}}; [βάκτρον], τοξεύματα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 744, “HF” 199.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ of the other senses and the mind, τ. ἦτορ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.23; τυφλὸς τά τ᾽ ὦτα, τόν τε νοῦν, τά τ᾽ ὄμματ᾽ εἶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 371; τὴν τέχνην ἔφυ τ. [prev. work] 389.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, τ. ὄλβος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 776; ἡ φύσις ἄνευ μαθήσεως τυφλόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.2b; τῇ τύχῃ.., ἣν τυφλὴν λοιδοροῦμεν [prev. work]98a; τ. ἔδραμε πᾶσα τρόπις “Anthologia Graeca” 9.289 (1st c.AD: Bassus, Lollius Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** of things, **dark, dim, obscure,** ἐλπίδες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 252; ἄτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1104; τὸ δ᾽ ἐς αὔριον αἰεὶ τ. ἕρπει [prev. author] “fragment” 593.6 (Lyric poetry)}}; τ. σπιλάδες **blind** rocks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.275 (1st c.AD: Gaetulicus, Cn. Lentulus Epigrammaticus); αἱ ἄνευ ἐπιστήμης δόξαι τυφλαί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 506c; δεσμῶν τ. ἀρχαί}}**hidden,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 18; τ. ὑπόνοια [prev. author] 2.587c}}; τ. κίνημα, of revolution, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Galb.” 18.}}
**II.2** of passages or apertures, **blind, closed, with no outlet,** τοῦ ἐντέρου τυφλόν τι, of the **intestinum caecum** (τὸ τυφλόν in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 4.18, +others), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 675b7, compare 676a5; τ. ἔντερον 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.26.25}}; τ. τρῆμα the **foramen caecum** (stylo-mastoid), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 144, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 9.10; τ. στενωποί 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.17; τ. ὁδοί Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; τ. ῥύμη a **blind** alley, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 99.9 (1st c.AD)}}; of rivers and harbours, **choked with mud,** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 20 (see. {τυφλόστομος}), compare “Caes.” 58}}]; of the halcyon's nest, **closed, tight,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.983d}}; τυφλοὶ ὄζοι branches **without buds** or **eyes,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.8.4, compare “CP” 3.2.8; τ. κῦμα}}**dark, trackless,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 7.400 (from Serapio), 12.156}}; τ. μώλωψ a wound **without an outlet,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 19}}; τὸ τ. ἅμμα καλούμενον the so-called **unescapable** knot, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.669}}; of a hook (compare τυφλάγκιστρον), **blunt,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.18.9.}}
**III** _adverb_, πρὸς τὸ ὠφέλιμον τυφλῶς ἔχειν to be **blind** to it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 479b; τ. καὶ ἀσκέπτως Antip.Stoic. 3.256; τ. καὶ οὐ γνωρίμως διασαφεῖ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.5.21}}. [ῠ by nature, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 389, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1050, etc., frequently ῡ by position: probably not connected with τύφω [ῡ]}}: perhaps cf. Gothic _daufs,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **déaf** 'stupid', Olr. **dub** 'black'.]

5186:
** τυφλό-ω**,
**blind, make blind,** τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.2}}; ὄμματα, ὄψιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 470, “Ph.” 764}}:—_passive_, **to be blinded, to be** or **become blind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111; τυφλοῦμαι φέγγος ὀμμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1035}}; ἕλκος τυφλωθέν a **blinding** wound (_passive_ of τυφλόω ἕλκος **inflict a blinding** wound), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 973 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **blind, baffle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 72, variant in 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.26; τετύφλωται μόχθος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).56; τῶν μελλόντων τετύφλωνται φραδαί [prev. author] “O.” 12.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 47b; τὴν ψυχὴν τυφλωθῆναι [prev. author] “Phd.” 99e, compare 96c; τ. περὶ τὸν φιλούμενον ὁ φιλῶν [prev. author] “Lg.” 731e.}}
**II** **make blind** or **without passage, stop up,** τὰς διόδους ἁμάξαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 2.5}}; τ. ὀφθαλμοὺς [ἀμπέλου] {{Refs|“Geoponica” 5.9.7; τ. τὸν μαστόν}}**makeit cease to yield milk,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.39}}:—_passive_, βλάστησις τυφλουμένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.17.7; οὖρα τυφλοῦται 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 340; ἡ φωνὴ τυφλοῦται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.721b; τυφλωθείσης τῆς τοῦ δέρματος τρώσεως 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.388}}:—also in _middle_, τυφλώσατο νηδύς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 285.}}

5187:
** τῡφόω**,
(τῦφος) **delude,** rare in _active_, ἐτύφωσ᾽ ἐκ δ᾽ ἔλετο φρένας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 68 (conjecture Porson), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.59a; τ. τινὰ εἰς ἐλπίδα μειζόνων πραγμάτων 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.5.10:}} —but mostly in _perfect_ _passive_ τετύφωμαι (_aorist_ _passive_ τυφωθείς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.193)}}, **to be crazy, demented,** ὦ τετυφωμένε σύ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 290a; ληρεῖν καὶ τετυφῶσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.20; οὐ δὴ ποιήσω τοῦτο· οὐχ οὕτω τετύφωμαι [prev. author] 18.11, compare 24.158, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.81.1, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 12.25.2, NT.1Tim.6.4, +others; ἴσως ἔγωγε τετύφωμαι ταῦτα λέγων καὶ τὰ μὴ δεινὰ ἀξιῶ δεδιέναι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.52; ὁ οἶνος τετυφωμένους ποιεῖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 873a23, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.54 K.; γόητες καὶ σοφισταὶ καὶ τετυφωμένοι καὶ φαρμακεῖς 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.197d; ἀνόητος καὶ τετυφωμένος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 1, compare “Icar.” 7, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.150}}: with _dative_ modi, τετυφωμένος τοσαύταις εὐτυχίαις **demented, rendered vain,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.5}}; ἐπὶ πλούτοις τε καὶ ἀρχαῖς **filled with insane arrogance,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 12: 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}} explanation τετύφωμαι by ἐμβεβρόντημαι.
**II** τυφῶσαι· πνῖξαι, ἀπολέσαι, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5188:
** τύφω** [ῡ],
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.196, etc.}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ θῦψαι [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {ἀτυφία}:}}]_perfect_ τέθῠφα uncertain conjecture in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Crobylus Comicus 4 (τέθαιφε codex A 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.42.3}} (ὑπο-): —_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362a7, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 141, etc.:}}_future_ τῠφήσομαι (ἐκ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 505:}}_aorist_ ἐτύφην (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 221:}}_perfect_ τέθυμμαι (ἐπι-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230a}}:—**raise a smoke,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.36: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τύφειν καπνόν}} Hdt.[same place]: _absolutely_, **smoke,** ἐπὶ σποδῷ μυδῶσα κηκὶς μηρίων ἐτήκετο κἄτυφε κἀνέπτυε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1009.}}
**II** _transitive_, **smoke,** τῦφε πολλῷ τῷ καπνῷ (i.e. τοὺς σφῆκας) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 457 (troch.), compare 1079 (troch.)}}:—_passive_, [μέλισσαι] καπνῷ τυφόμεναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.134}}; τυφόμεθα (variant{-ούμεθα}) ὑπὸ τοῦ καπνοῦ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 310d.}}
**II.2** **consume in smoke, burn slowly,** τυφέτω, καιέτω τὸν Αἴτνας μηλονόμον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 659 (Lyric poetry); τ. τὸν χόρτον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.29 (as variant for{πυροῦσι})}}]: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Crobylus Comicus}} [same place]:—_passive_, **smoke, smoulder,** τύφεται Ἴλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 145 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ba.” 8; [χθὼν] καπνῷ κατερείπεται τυφομένα [prev. author] “Hec.” 478 (Lyric poetry); τυφέσθω Κύκλωψ [prev. author] “Cyc.” 655}}; λίνον τυφόμενον **smouldering** flax, [{{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.12.20 (={λ. καπνιζόμενον} LXX.Isa.42.3): _metaphorically_, τυφόμενος πόλεμος}}] **smouldering,** but not yet broken out, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 6}}; also of the fire of love, πόθοις τυφόμενον γλυκὺ πῦρ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.63 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 92 ([prev. author]), 5.123 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus), 130 ([prev. author]), 11.41 ([prev. author]).}}

5189:
** Τῡφων-ικός**, ή, όν,
**Typhonian, of** or **from Typhon,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.421c.}}
**II** (τυφῶν 11.1) **tempestuous,** ἄνεμος {{NT|NT.Act.27.14.}}

5192:
** Ὑάκινθος** [ῠᾰ], ὁ,
**Hyacinthus,** a Laconian youth, beloved by Apollo, who killed him by an unlucky cast of the discus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1469 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.10.3, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.1.3: compare Ὑακίνθια.}}
**B** as _appellative_,
**B.I** **ὑάκινθος**, ὁ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.348, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.35.4}}; but ἡ in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 94, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.8.2, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus, etc. (see. below)}}:—**wild hyacinth, bluebell, Scilla bifolia,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place], “hymnus ad Cererem” 7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.8.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.62.}}
**B.I.2** **blue larkspur, Delphinium Ajacis,** ὑ. σπαρτή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.8.2}}; said to have sprung up from the blood of Hyacinthus or (_accusative_ to others) of Telamonian Ajax: and the ancients thought they could decipher on the petals the initial letters AI, or the interj. AIAI,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|compare 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.6}}; hence the epithets γραπτά {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.28; αἰαστής 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.31; πολύθρηνος [prev. author] “Th.” 902; πολύκλαυτος “IG” 14.607; cf. Pseudo-Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.73.}}
**B.I.3** ὑ. πορφυρέη, probably **Lilium Martagon,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 40, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.146 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.II** ὑάκινθος, ἡ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus, ὁ 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus and 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}:—a precious stone, of blue colour {{Refs 1st c.AD+|(1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.7.7)}}, perhaps **aquamarine,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rev. 21.20, “Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 56, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 32, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.30, “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 11.12.1, etc.; compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.125.}}
**B.III** name of a **blue** colour, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.6.1, “PHolm.” 17.3}}; **blue stuff,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Exo.28.8, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.148, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.5.4; χιτώνια τὴν χροιὰν ὑακίνθου 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 34.6.}}

5194:
** ὕᾰλος**
or **ὕελος** (see. below), ἡ, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 217; but in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 49}}, ὁ:—the form **ὕαλος** is said to be _Attic dialect_, **ὕελος** Hellenic, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 281, [prev. author] “PS” p.118 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, Moer.p.373 P., Thom.Mag.p.365 R.; in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus codices vary between ὕελος}} and ὕαλο; ὕελος is read in {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 88a14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus [same place], “Ign.” 73, “PHolm.” 10.7, but ὕαλος in LXX.Job.28.17, 1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 39.18, NT.Rev.21.18}}; compare ὑάλινος:—originally **some kind of crystalline stone,** such as that used by the Ethiopians to enclose their mummies in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.24; ὕ. ἀργή “Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 49; ὕ. ὀρωρυγμένη}}**rock-crystal,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.3.}}
**2** **a convex lens of crystal,** used as a burning-glass, λίθος διαφανὴς ἀφ᾽ ἧς τὸ πῦρ ἅπτουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 768, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 73.}}
**II** **glass,** first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 61b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 389a8, and ὑαλῖτι;}}**glass-ware,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PFay. 134.4 (4th c.AD).}}
**III** ὕαλος χνοώδης, in {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.22}}, is **an absorbent** of some kind:—ὕαλος is also explained by βόρβορος in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus and 9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 18}}. [ῠᾰλος, as appears from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 768}}:—but late Poets make ῡ in some derivations to bring them into dactylic verses, ῡάλεος {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.33, 12.249}}; ῡέλινος {{Refs|[prev. work] 14.52}}; ῡαλόεις {{Refs|[prev. work]5.47}}; ῡαλοειδής {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 280}}; in iambic metre, ῡαλόχροα {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.211.]}}

5195:
** ὑβρίζω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.381, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ **-ίσδω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.9}}: _future_ _Attic dialect_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.221}}, (ἐν-) probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 720 (-ίσεις codex R)}}: _aorist_ ὕβρισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.87, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 560, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὕβρικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 400, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.128}}: _pluperfect_ ὑβρίκειν {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.14}}:—_middle_, _future_ ὑβριοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 666 (_anapaest meter_)}}:—_passive_, _future_ ὑβρισθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.222}}: _aorist_ ὑβρίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 367, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 885a}}: _perfect_ ὕβρισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 665, etc.}}: (ὕβρις):—**wax wanton, run riot,** in the use of superior strength or power, or in sensual indulgence, ὑβρίζοντες ὑπερφιάλως δοκέουσι δαίνυσθαι κατὰ δῶμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.227; ὑβρίζοντες ἀτάσθαλα μηχανόωνται 3.207, 17.588; ἀλλὰ μάλ᾽ ὑβρίζεις 18.381; ὁππότ᾽ ἀνὴρ ἄδικος καὶ ἀτάσθαλος.. ὑβρίζῃ πλούτῳ κεκορημένος 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 751; ἐνταῦθα νῦν ὕβριζε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 82, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 480, etc.}}; especially of lust, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.30}}; opposed to σωφρονεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.1.30, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.2.}}
**2** of over-fed asses, **neigh** or **bray and prance about,** ὑβρίζοντες οἱ ὄνοι ἐτάρασσον τὴν ἵππον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.129}}; of horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.62}}; of elephants, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.10.}}
**3** of plants, **run riot, grow rank and luxuriant,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.6, “CP” 3.15.4.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, of a river that swept away and drowned a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189}}; so γῆ ὕβριστο **had been carried away** by river-floods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus (?) 154.}}
**II** _transitive_, ὑ. τινά **treat** him **despitefully, outrage, insult, maltreat,** ἡμέας ὑβρίζοντες ἀτάσθαλα μηχανόωντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(see. below 2) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.695; ὑ. τοὺς ὑβρίζοντας χρεών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 970; ὑ. γυναῖκα τὴν ἑαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.15}}; με, ἐμέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 840 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.4; τὰς νήσους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.99: more frequently (especially in Prose) ὑ. εἴς τινα(s)}} **commit an outrage** upon or towards him (them), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 620 (troch.), “Hipp.” 1073, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 899}}; ὑ. εἰς (uncertain reading) τοὺς θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1506; εἰς σὲ καὶ τὴν σὴν γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.16; εἰς τὰς πατρίδας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.111; εἰς ταύτην τὴν παροιμίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174b (_accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 10, ὑ. τινά}} was **to do one a personal injury,** ὑ, εἴς τινα **to injure that which belongs to one;** but the distinction was not observed): also ὑ. ἐν κακοῖσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1612, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1151.}}
**II.2** frequently with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὑ. ὕβριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 880 (Lyric poetry); ὕβρεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 247; ὕβριν ἐς ἡμᾶς ὑ. [prev. author] “IA” 961, compare “Heracl.” 18; ὕ. ὑβρίζεις ἐπὶ θανοῦσι τοῖς ἐμοῖς [prev. author] “HF” 708; ὕβρεις ἃς κατὰ τὴν ἀγορὰν ὕβριζεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.68: with _neuter_}} _adjective_, ὑ. τάδε **commit** these **outrages,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.118; ὑ. τἄλλα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 400; ὅσα περὶ θεοὺς ὑ. τις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 885b, compare 761e}}: and with other Nouns, τῶν ἀδικημάτων.., τῶν ἐς Ἀθηναίους ὕβρισαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.87 (so probably θεοὶ τεισαίατο λώβην, ἣν οἵδ᾽ ὑβρίζοντες ἀτάσθαλα μηχανόωνται (see. above 11.1) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.170): and with double _accusative_, τοιαῦτα ὑ. τινά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 613; τίνος δέ σ᾽ οὕνεχ᾽ ὕβρισ᾽ Αἴγισθος τάδ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 266, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.2, “Cyr.” 5.2.28}}:—_passive_, ὕβριν ὑβρισθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1297, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.121; τάλαιν᾽ ἐγὼ τῆς ὕβρεος ἧς ὑβρίζομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1044; ὧν δ᾽ εἰς τὸ σῶμα ὑβρίσθαι φημί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.25.}}
**II.3** in legal sense, **commit a physical outrage on one** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ὕβρις 11.2,3), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.26, 24.18, “fragment” 44, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.6 (_passive_), etc.}}; so later, ἐμὲ δέ, ἐὰν δύνῃ, καὶ ὕβριζε καὶ ἄπαγε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 454.9 (3rd c.BC),compare “PEnteux.” 79.7, +others (3rd c.BC)}}; γυναῖκες καὶ παῖδες ὑβρίζονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.74; ὑβρισθῆναι βίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 874c}}; τὰς γνάθους ὑβρισμένη **mauled** on the cheeks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 903}}; ὑβριζομένους ἀποθανεῖν to die **of ill-treatment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.13}}; ὑβρίσθαι **to be mutilated,** of eunuchs, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.4.35: of acts, τὰ ὑβρισμένα}}**outrages,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.7.}}
**II.4** _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, of things, **arrogant, ostentatious,** σημεῖ᾽ ἔχων ὑβρισμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1112; στολὴ οὐδέν τι ὑβρισμένη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.5.}}

5196:
** ὕβρις** [ῠ
by nature, ῡ by position in _Epic dialect_ etc.], ἡ, _genitive_ εως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 425, “Th.” 465 (Lyric poetry), εος [prev. author] “Pl.” 1044, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 67.9}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 217, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189}}:— **wanton violence,** arising from the pride of strength or from passion, **insolence,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}}, mostly of the suitors, μνηστήρων, τῶν ὕ. τε βίη τε σιδήρεον οὐρανὸν ἵκει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.329, 17.565; μνηστῆρες ὑπέρβιον ὕ. ἔχοντες 1.368, 4.321; λίην γὰρ ἀτάσθαλον ὕ. ἔχουσι 16.86, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 27.10; ὕβρει εἴξαντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.262, 17.431}}; θεοὶ.. ἀνθρώπων ὕβριν τε καὶ εὐνομίην ἐφορῶντες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 487; δίκη ὑπὲρ ὕβριος ἴσχει 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place], compare 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, “IG” 12.394 (6th c.BC), 42(1).122.98 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; joined with ὀλιγωρίη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.106; δυσσεβίας μὲν ὕβρις τέκος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 533 (Lyric poetry); ἐπιθυμίας.. ἀρξάσης ἐν ἡμῖν τῇ ἀρχῇ ὕ. ἐπωνομάσθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a; in Poets frequently joined with κόρος (see. κόρος (A) 2)}}: predicated of actions, ἆρ᾽ οὐχ ὕβρις τάδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 883; ταῦτ᾽ οὐχ ὕβρις δῆτ᾽ ἐστί; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1299, compare “Ra.” 21, “Pl.” 886; ὕβρις τάδ᾽ ἐστί, κρείσσω δαιμόνων εἶναι θέλειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 474}}; ὕβρει **in wantonness** or **insolence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 881, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26e; ἐφ᾽ ὕβρει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1581, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38, “PCair.Zen.” 462.9 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; δι᾽ ὕβριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.42; διὰ τὴν ὕ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.10; πρὸς ὕβριν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 37, etc.}}
**2** **lust, lewdness,** opposed to σωφροσύνη, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 379, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.14.}}
**3** of animals, **violence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189; ὕβρις ὀρθία κνωδάλων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.36, compare “N.” 1.50 (see. ὑβρίζω 1.2); ἡ ἐκ τοῦ χαλινοῦ ὕ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 63.5.}}
**II** ={ὕβρισμα}, **an outrage** (though it is frequently difficult to separate this concrete sense from the abstract), [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.203, 214; ὕβριν τεῖσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.352; ὑπὸ γυναικὸς ἄρχεσθαι ὕ. ἐσχάτη 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 111, compare 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.17: sometimes, like{ὑβρίζω}, followed by a _preposition_, Ἥρας μητέρ᾽ εἰς ἐμὴν ὕβρις}}] her **outrage** towards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 9}}; ἡ κατ᾽ Ἀργείων (-ους codices {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Priscianus Grammaticus) ὕ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 368; ἡ πρὸς τοὺς δημότας ὕ. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.4.1: with _genitive_ objecti, ὕ. τινός}} towards him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.8.4, etc.}}: _plural_, **wanton acts, outrages,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 146, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 247, “HF” 741, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 884a, etc.:—for ὕβριν ὑβρίζειν,compare ὑβρίζω 11.2.}}
**II.2** **an outrage on the person,** especially **violation, rape,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.28, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.2, etc.; παίδων ὕβρεις καὶ γυναικῶν αἰσχύνας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.114, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.8.5; τὴν ὕ. τὴν εἰς τὸ ἑαυτοῦ σῶμα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.116; τὴν τοῦ σώματος ὕβριν πεπρακώς [prev. work]188; so τὸ σῶμα ἐφ᾽ ὕβρει πεπρακώς [prev. work]29; γυναῖκας ἤγαγε δεῦρ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ὕβρει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.309; γυναικῶν ὕβρεις ἢ εἰς αὑτοὺς ἢ εἰς υἱεῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1373a35.}}
**II.3** in Law, a term covering all **the more serious injuries done to the person,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.2, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.15, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.33, 45.4; see especially 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21 (against Meidias); ὁ τῆς ὕβρεως νόμος [prev. work]35 (the text is given [prev. work]47); δίκη ὕβρεως ἢ πληγῶν “PHal.” 1.115 (3rd c.BC),compare “PHib.” 1.32.8 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**III** used of **a loss by sea,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. ναυσίστονος), NT.Act.27.21.}}
**B** as _masculine_, ={ὑβριστής}, **a violent, overbearing man,** κακῶν ῥεκτῆρα καὶ ὕβριν ἀνέρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 191.}}

5197:
** ὑβρ-ιστής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**violent, wanton, licentious, insolent man,** ὑβριστῇσι.. τῶν μένος αἰὲν ἀτάσθαλον, οὐδὲ δύνανται φυλόπιδος κορέσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.633; ὑβρισταί τε καὶ ἄγριοι οὐδὲ δίκαιοι ἠὲ φιλόξεινοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.120, 9.175, 13.201}}; of the suitors {{Refs 6th c.BC+|compare (ὕβρις 1.1), ὑ. καὶ ἀτάσθαλοι 24.282; στρατὸν ὑβριστὴν Μήδων 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 775; Πέρσαι φύσιν ἐόντες ὑ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89; ἀνδρῶν δυναστέων παῖδες ὑβρισταί [prev. author] 2.32}}; στρατὸν θηρῶν ὑ., of the Centaurs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1096}}: also in Prose, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.14, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.15, NT.Rom.1.30, etc.}}; in a milder sense, **sarcastic,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 355c.}}
**2** especially, opposed to σώφρων, **lustful, lewd,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1068 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.21, etc.; ὁ εἰς ὁτιοῦν ὑ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.17}}; ὑ. πενίης **insolent** towards.., {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.172b (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** of animals, **wanton, restive, unruly,** ταῦροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 743; ἵπποι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.62, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254c.}}
**4** of natural forces, ὑβριστὴς Τυφάων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 307; Ὑβριστὴν ποταμὸν οὐ ψευδώνυμον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 717.}}
**5** of things, ὑ. οἶνος διὰ νεότητα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 8}}; μέλι Ἀττικὸν ποιεῖ ὑ. [τὸν πλακοῦντα] makes it **proud,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 62.18}}; νάρθηκας ὑ., of the Bacchae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 113 (Lyric poetry).}} —Cf. ὕβριστος near the end

5198:
** ὑγῐ-αίνω**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 255, etc.}}: _future_ **-ᾰνῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 93, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1032b18}}: _aorist_ ὑγίᾱνα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.1}}, _Ionic dialect_ ὑγίηνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 20, “Epid.” 2.2.4}}. [ῠ, but ῡ in augmented tenses, {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 115]}}:—**to be sound, healthy,** or **in health,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus [same place], “Scol.” 8, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 12 (but ἐκφυγγάνει codex C, Littré), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 605 (_anapaest meter_)}}; opposed to νοσεῖν, κάμνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 495e, 505a; ὑγιάνας καὶ σωθείς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.1}}; _participle_ ὑγιαίνων, ={ὑγιής},{δυνάμενος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.13; ὀφθαλμοὶὑγιαίνοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.6: of things, ὑγιαίνων καὶ τεταγμένος βίος}}**healthy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.5a}}, compare 43b; ὄψα τῶν λιτῶν καὶ ὑ. {{Refs|[prev. work]66of: generally}}**to be in a certain state of health,** ὑ. νοσηρότερον and ὑγιεινότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 6.2.}}
**2** **to be sound of mind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1275, “Av.” 1214, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 190c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 220, etc.; in full, τὰς φρένας ὑ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.33.}}
**3** of **soundness** in political or religious matters, especially in _participle_, τὸ ὑγιαῖνον τῆς Ἑλλάδος {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.157}}; οἱ ὑγιαίνοντες, opposed to turbulent agitators, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.12; ὑγιαίνουσα διδασκαλία NT.1Tim.1.10; ὑγιαίνουσα ἀριστοκρατία 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 12; δόξαι περὶ θεῶν ὑ. [prev. author] 2.20f, etc.}}
**4** ὑγίαινε, like{χαῖρε}, a form of taking leave, **farewell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 165, “Ec.” 477}}; found on tombstones, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3706 (from Cyzicus), “IG” 14.2526, +others (from Lugdunum), “BMus.Inscription” 1123a (uncertain _locative_)}}; but σὺ δ᾽ ὑγίαινέ μοι salutation at meeting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 44, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 297; frequently in letters, Μνησίεργος ἐπέστειλε τοῖς οἴκοι χαίρειν καὶ ὑγιαίνειν “SIG” 1259 (Athens, 4th c.BC)}}; σεαυτοῦ ἐπιμελοῦ ἵν᾽ ὑγιαίνῃς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“POxy.” 745.10 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD).}}
**II** Causal, = ῦγιάζω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus 2.11}}:—_passive_, _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 4.5.}}

5199:
** ὑγι-ής**, ές,
_genitive_ _Attic dialect_ -οῦ; _dative_ ὑγιεῖ; _accusative_, _Ionic dialect_ ὑγιέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, etc. (ὑγιᾶ}} variant{ὑγιέα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 33)}}; _Attic dialect_ ὑγιᾶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155e, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13; also ὑγιῆ “IG” 22.1673.42, 42(1).121.38,60,85, 122.109 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89d, “Lg.” 857e, compare “IG” 14.1014 (2nd c.AD)}}, erroneously called un-Attic by Moer.p.375 P., Thom.Mag.p.365 R.: dual ὑγιῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 88c: _neuter_ _plural_ ὑγιᾶ “IG” 22.120.59, 13th-14th c.AD: Thomas Magister Grammaticus [same place], but ὑγιῆ in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684c, 735b}}, and frequently in _Attic dialect_ _inscriptions_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.120.52, 1541.8, etc.; _accusative_ _plural_ _masculine_ ὑγιᾶς [prev. work] 12.74.20; but ὑγιεῖς [prev. work]42(1).121.36 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 12(5).572.13 (Ceos, 3rd c.BC)}}, and as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 948; _genitive_ ὑγιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 735c}}:— _comparative_ ὑγιέστερος [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 154 (with variant{ὑγιώστερον})}}], _superlative_ **-έστατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 526d}}; irregular _comparative_ ὑγιώτερος in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 34, probably conjecture in 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus}} [same place]:—**healthy, sound** in body, ὑγιέα ποιέειν or ἀποδέξαι τινά restore him **to health,** make him **sound,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130, 134; ὑγιῆ σώματα ἀπεργάζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684c}}; τὸ ὑ. τοῦ σώματος, opposed to τὸ νοσοῦν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 186b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.13}}; πόλις (opposed to φλεγμαίνουσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372e: proverbial, ὑγιέστερος κολοκύντας or ὄμφακος}} **'sound** as a bell', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [same place], 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; ὑγιέστερος κροτῶνος or Κρότωνος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 318, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.1.12.}}
**2** of one's case or condition, σῶς καὶ ὑ. safe and **sound,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.76, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.34.}}
**3** of things, **safe and sound, in good case,** of the Hermae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.12}}; of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.107; κόσμος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13; τὸ ἔδαφος καὶ οἱ τοῖχοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 842a33; σῶν καὶ ὑγιὲς μένειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 82b}}; **in good condition, unbroken,** πίθοι, κώθων, λίθος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.326.7, 42(1).121.85 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 7.3073.32 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC)}}; πίθοι ὑ., opposed to ἀγγεῖα τετρημένα καὶ σαθρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493e, compare “Cra.” 440c, “Men.” 77a (see. below 111.1); ἱμάτια “POxy.” 530.20 (2nd c.AD); μύλος ὑ. καὶ ἀσινής [prev. work] 278.18 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **sound in mind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.4, etc.; φρένες ὑγιεῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 948}}; **virtuous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89d; ἦθος [prev. author] “R.” 409d, etc.; ὡς ὑγιεστάτη ψυχή [prev. author] “Grg.” 526d}}; as a complimentary epithet, ὑγιέστατον ἀνθύπατον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 568.6 (Tlos, 3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** of words, opinions, and the like, **sound, wholesome, wise,** μῦθος ὃς.. νῦν ὑγιής the word which is now **fitting,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.524}} (the only place where any of this family of words occurs in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); ὑ. δόξαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 584e; εἴ τι ὑ. διανοοῦνται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b; χεῖρας καὶ γνώμην καθαροὶ καὶ ὑγιεῖς “IG” 12(1).789.5 (Lindus, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** frequently with a _negative_, λόγος οὐκ ὑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8; οὐδὲν ὑ. βούλευμα [prev. author] 6.100}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ὦ μηδὲν ὑ. μηδ᾽ ἐλεύθερον φρονῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1006; ἑλικτὰ κοὐδὲν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 448; οὐδὲν ὑ. διανοουμένων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75; μηδὲν ὑ. λέγειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 201, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 636, “Pl.” 274, etc.}}; φέρειν, ἀσκεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 956, “Pl.” 50; οὐδὲν ὑ. οὐδ᾽ ἀληθὲς ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69b}}: also of persons, τὰς οὐδὲν ὑγιές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 394; πανοῦργον, ἄδικον, ὑγιὲς μηδὲ ἕν [prev. author] “Pl.” 37: with _genitive_, οὐδ᾽ ἦν ἄρ᾽ ὑ. οὐδὲν ἐμπύρου φλογός}} there is nothing **sound** or **good** in it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 746; φεῦ· ὡς οὐδὲν ἀτεχνῶς ὑ. ἐστιν οὐδενός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 362, compare 870, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 90c, “Grg.” 524e, “R.” 584a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.23, etc.; οὐχ ὑ. οὐδὲν ἔτι λέγω τῶν ὀργίων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 262, compare “Cyc.” 259; ἐπ᾽ οὐδενὶ ὑγιεῖ οὐδ᾽ ἀληθεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 603b, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.4.}}
**II.4** logically **sound,** τὸ ὑ. συνημμένον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.118; ὑ. ἀπόδειξις [prev. author] “P.” 1.116, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.1.4.}}
**III** _neuter_ as _adverb_, ὑγιὲς φθέγγεσθαι ring **sound and clear,** opposed to σαθρόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179d}}: also in phrase ἐξ ὑγιοῦς, φροντίζειν ὅπως καὶ τἆλλα γένηται.. ἐξ ὑ. **correctly, in order,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 27.60 (2nd c.BC)}}; οὐκ ἐξ ὑ. τὰς κτήσεις ποιοῦσιν, i. e. dishonestly, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 90.32.}}
**III.2** regular _adverb_ ὑγιῶς, **healthily,** διάγειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.46f}}; **soundly,** κρίνειν, φιλοσοφεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409a, 619d; ὑ. πεπολίτευμαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.298; ὑ. ἀπαγγεῖλαι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.19}}; ὑ. καὶ πιστῶς **honourably** and faithfully, frequently in Papyrus, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1031.18 (3rd c.AD), etc. (Prob. from ὑ-}} cf. Sanskrit _su-_ 'well', and -γιη-, I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus γυιψē}} compare **guiyō** in βιῶναι.)
